window.PREVIEW_MOCKS["gmch-2025"] = {"id": "gmch-2025", "title": "GMCH Chandigarh 2025", "fullTitle": "GMCH Chandigarh 2025, M.Phil Clinical Psychology entrance (real paper, official key)", "sub": "100 real questions · official answer key · untimed", "sectionLabels": {"general": "General and Cognitive Psychology", "assessment": "Psychological Assessment", "social": "Social Psychology", "personality": "Personality", "physio": "Biopsychology", "dev": "Developmental Psychology", "clinical": "Clinical Psychology and Therapy", "disorders": "Psychopathology", "research": "Research Methods and Statistics"}, "questions": [{"q": "Which of the following models emphasizes the influence of early childhood experiences on psychopathology?", "options": {"a": "Behavioral model", "b": "Cognitive model", "c": "Psychodynamic model", "d": "Biopsychosocial model"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: the psychodynamic model emphasises the influence of early childhood experiences on psychopathology. The model grew out of Sigmund Freud's psychoanalysis, developed in Vienna from the 1890s. Freud argued that adult symptoms are the surface signs of unconscious conflicts between the drives of the id, the ego and the superego, and that these conflicts take shape in the first five or six years of life as the child passes through the oral, anal and phallic psychosexual stages. If a stage is badly frustrated or overindulged, libido stays partly fixated there, and stress in adulthood can trigger regression to it. Defence mechanisms such as repression keep the painful material out of awareness, so a symptom is a compromise between a wish and a defence. Later psychodynamic writers kept the emphasis: object-relations theorists put the early bond with the caregiver at the centre. Example: a woman who was harshly criticised by her father becomes anxious and self-punishing whenever a male boss evaluates her work; a psychodynamic therapist traces the anxiety to that early relationship and watches it replayed in the transference. Option c is right because only the psychodynamic model makes childhood experience, processed unconsciously, the main cause of later disorder. Exam trap: the biopsychosocial model also counts early experience as one psychological factor, and behaviourists accept that learning starts in childhood, but neither gives childhood a special causal status; 'early childhood experiences' is the signature of Freud's tradition.", "topic": "Psychodynamic model of psychopathology"}, {"q": "What is the primary characteristic of a diathesis-stress model?", "options": {"a": "Emphasizing unconscious conflicts", "b": "Interaction between genetic vulnerability and environmental stress", "c": "Solely attributing mental illness to social factors", "d": "Only considering biological factors"}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b: the primary characteristic of the diathesis-stress model is the interaction between a vulnerability, often genetic, and environmental stress. A diathesis is a predisposition: usually an inherited biological vulnerability, but it can also be psychological, such as a pessimistic thinking style or an early loss. A stressor is the triggering event, such as a bereavement, a break-up or an exam failure. The model's central claim is that neither part is enough alone: a disorder appears only when stress pushes a vulnerable person past a threshold. Paul Meehl applied the idea to schizophrenia in 1962, proposing an inherited 'schizotaxia' that becomes illness only under adverse conditions, and Joseph Zubin and Bonnie Spring (1977) formalised it as the vulnerability-stress model: the more vulnerable you are, the less stress it takes to tip you into illness. Later versions add protective factors, such as social support, coping skills and continued medication, which raise the threshold and are used in relapse prevention. Example: identical twins share the genes for schizophrenia, yet only one, who loses her job and moves alone to a new city, develops psychosis; the shared diathesis was activated only in the twin under stress. Option b is right because the model is defined by the interaction of predisposition and stress, not by either alone. Exam trap: any option containing 'only', 'solely' or a single cause is wrong for diathesis-stress, and the wider biopsychosocial model of George Engel (1977) is a different framework.", "topic": "Diathesis-stress model"}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT a component of Beck's cognitive triad?", "options": {"a": "Negative view of self", "b": "Negative view of world", "c": "Negative view of past", "d": "Negative view of future"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: a negative view of the past is NOT part of Beck's cognitive triad. Aaron T. Beck, an American psychiatrist trained in psychoanalysis, noticed that his depressed patients reported quick, believable, self-critical automatic thoughts rather than the anger turned inward that Freudian theory predicted. He set out his model in Depression (1967) and the treatment in Cognitive Therapy of Depression (1979, with colleagues). At its centre is the cognitive triad: a negative view of the self ('I am worthless'), of the world or current experience ('everyone is against me') and of the future ('it will never get better'). These views are fed by deeper core beliefs, or schemas, formed in childhood, and are kept alive by cognitive distortions such as overgeneralisation, personalisation and all-or-nothing thinking. Therapy teaches the client to catch automatic thoughts, test them against evidence and build balanced alternatives. Example: a student who fails one test thinks 'I am stupid' (self), 'the teachers are unfair to me' (world) and 'I will fail the whole course' (future). Option c is right because the triad has exactly three parts, self, world and future, and the past is not one of them. Exam trap: students remember 'three time points' and pick past, present and future, or confuse the triad with the thoughts, feelings and behaviours that CBT links; remember self, world, future. Depressed people do brood about the past, but that is rumination, not a triad component.", "topic": "Beck's cognitive triad"}, {"q": "In research methodology, which type of validity ensures that the test measures what it intends to measure?", "options": {"a": "Content validity", "b": "Face validity", "c": "Criterion validity", "d": "Construct validity"}, "correct": "d", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is d: construct validity is keyed as the type of validity that ensures a test measures what it intends to measure. Validity in general asks whether a test measures what it claims to measure, the classic definition associated with Henry Garrett. Many attributes psychologists care about, such as anxiety, intelligence or extraversion, are constructs: hypothetical qualities that cannot be observed directly. Lee Cronbach and Paul Meehl (1955) argued that such a test is validated by placing the construct in a nomological network, the web of relationships the theory predicts, and checking that scores behave accordingly. Evidence includes convergent validity (high correlations with other measures of the same construct), discriminant validity (low correlations with measures of different constructs), factor structure, group differences and changes after experimental manipulation. Samuel Messick (1989) went further and treated construct validity as the unifying idea that absorbs all the other kinds. Example: a new anxiety scale should correlate highly with the Beck Anxiety Inventory, weakly with a vocabulary test, and rise in students just before an exam; if it does, it really measures the anxiety construct. Option d is right because, of the four, construct validity is the one that asks directly whether a test captures the theoretical attribute it was built to measure. Exam trap: face validity sounds closest because it concerns whether a test looks as if it measures what it should, but appearance to laypeople is not evidence. Key note: the stem is really the general definition of validity itself, so a purist could say no single subtype fits; construct validity is the best fit, especially under Messick's unified view, and the official key stands.", "topic": "Construct validity"}, {"q": "Which statistical test is appropriate for comparing means between two independent groups?", "options": {"a": "Chi-square test", "b": "ANOVA", "c": "t-test", "d": "Regression analysis"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: the t-test, specifically the independent-samples t-test, is the appropriate test for comparing the means of two independent groups. William Sealy Gosset, a chemist at the Guinness brewery in Dublin, worked out the t distribution for small samples and published it in Biometrika in 1908 under the pen name 'Student', hence Student's t. The test divides the difference between two sample means by the standard error of that difference; a large t means the gap is big relative to the chance variation expected. For two independent groups the degrees of freedom are n1 + n2 minus 2. It is a parametric test, assuming an interval or ratio dependent variable, roughly normal distributions, homogeneity of variance (checked with Levene's test) and independent observations. There are three versions: the one-sample t-test, the independent-samples t-test for two separate groups, and the paired (related) t-test for the same people measured twice. Example: a researcher compares the mean anxiety scores of 30 students given mindfulness training with those of 30 controls; an independent-samples t-test asks whether the difference exceeds what chance would produce. Option c is right because two groups plus a comparison of means is exactly the t-test's job. Exam trap: ANOVA can also handle two groups (F then equals t squared), but it is designed for three or more; and if the data are ordinal or badly non-normal, the non-parametric alternative is the Mann-Whitney U test, not chi-square.", "topic": "Independent-samples t-test"}, {"q": "The probability of rejecting a true null hypothesis is known as:", "options": {"a": "Type I error", "b": "Type II error", "c": "Confidence interval", "d": "Standard deviation"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: the probability of rejecting a true null hypothesis is the probability of a Type I error, written alpha. In significance testing the null hypothesis (H0) states that there is no effect or difference. Because decisions are made from samples, two mistakes are possible. A Type I error (alpha error) is rejecting H0 when it is actually true: a false positive. A Type II error (beta error) is retaining H0 when it is actually false: a false negative, a real effect missed. Jerzy Neyman and Egon Pearson, in papers of 1928 and 1933, framed testing as a decision between H0 and an alternative hypothesis with fixed error rates, which gave us these two errors and the idea of power (1 minus beta). The researcher chooses alpha in advance, usually .05 or .01, so the significance level is the Type I error rate. With sample size fixed, lowering alpha cuts Type I errors but raises beta; only a larger sample or a more precise measure reduces both. Example: a trial concludes at p < .05 that a new antidepressant beats placebo, but in truth the drug has no effect; the researchers have made a Type I error. In court terms, a Type I error convicts the innocent and a Type II error frees the guilty. Option a is right because rejecting a true null is the textbook definition of a Type I error. Exam trap: examiners swap the wordings; accepting a false null is Type II, and the probability of correctly rejecting a false null is power, not alpha.", "topic": "Type I error"}, {"q": "Which neurotransmitter is most commonly associated with schizophrenia?", "options": {"a": "Dopamine", "b": "Serotonin", "c": "Acetylcholine", "d": "Glutamate"}, "correct": "a", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is a: dopamine is the neurotransmitter most commonly associated with schizophrenia. The dopamine hypothesis grew from drug observations. Jean Delay and Pierre Deniker reported in 1952 that chlorpromazine, the first antipsychotic, calmed psychotic patients. Arvid Carlsson (Nobel Prize, 2000) showed that dopamine is a neurotransmitter, and in 1963 he and Margit Lindqvist argued that antipsychotics block dopamine receptors. Drugs that raise dopamine activity, such as amphetamine and L-dopa, can produce psychotic symptoms. The key evidence came in 1976, when Philip Seeman's group, and Ian Creese, David Burt and Solomon Snyder, showed that the clinical potency of typical antipsychotics tracks how strongly they bind to the D2 receptor. The revised hypothesis says that excess dopamine in the mesolimbic pathway produces positive symptoms (delusions, hallucinations), while too little in the mesocortical pathway contributes to negative and cognitive symptoms. Blocking D2 in the nigrostriatal pathway explains extrapyramidal side effects, and blocking it in the tuberoinfundibular pathway raises prolactin. Example: a man who binges on methamphetamine develops paranoid delusions that look like acute schizophrenia, and they settle when an antipsychotic blocks his D2 receptors. Option a is right because dopamine is the classic, best-established transmitter linked to schizophrenia and to how its drugs work. Exam trap: glutamate (NMDA receptor hypofunction) and serotonin (5-HT2A, the target of atypical drugs) are genuine modern additions, so do not be lured by them; 'most commonly associated' means dopamine.", "topic": "Dopamine hypothesis of schizophrenia"}, {"q": "A patient presents with fluctuating levels of consciousness, visual hallucinations, and autonomic dysfunction. Which condition is most likely?", "options": {"a": "Schizophrenia", "b": "Dementia with Lewy bodies", "c": "Major depressive disorder", "d": "OCD"}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b: fluctuating consciousness, visual hallucinations and autonomic dysfunction point to dementia with Lewy bodies (DLB). Lewy bodies are abnormal clumps of the protein alpha-synuclein inside neurons, named after Friedrich Lewy, who described them in 1912 in Parkinson's disease. In DLB they spread through the cortex as well as the brainstem. The consensus criteria of Ian McKeith and colleagues (fourth report, 2017) list four core clinical features: fluctuating cognition with marked variation in attention and alertness; recurrent, well-formed visual hallucinations; REM sleep behaviour disorder, in which the person acts out dreams; and parkinsonism (slowness, rigidity, tremor). Supportive features include severe sensitivity to antipsychotics, repeated falls, fainting and autonomic dysfunction such as low blood pressure on standing, constipation and urinary incontinence. Memory may be fairly spared early, while attention, executive and visuospatial skills fail first. Example: a 72-year-old man is lucid one afternoon and drowsy and confused the next, sees small children sitting in his living room, has shouted and kicked in his sleep for years, feels faint on standing and walks with a shuffle. Option b is right because fluctuation, vivid visual hallucinations and autonomic problems together fit DLB and none of the other options. Exam trap: severe antipsychotic sensitivity is the safety point examiners love, since a typical antipsychotic given for the hallucinations can cause dangerous, even fatal, worsening of rigidity and confusion. Delirium also fluctuates, but it is acute and is not offered here.", "topic": "Dementia with Lewy bodies"}, {"q": "The primary feature distinguishing bipolar I disorder from bipolar II disorder is:", "options": {"a": "Presence of major depressive episodes", "b": "Presence of hypomanic episodes", "c": "Presence of manic episodes", "d": "Absence of mood symptoms"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: the presence of at least one manic episode is what distinguishes bipolar I disorder from bipolar II disorder. Emil Kraepelin grouped recurrent mood illness as manic-depressive insanity in 1899, and Karl Leonhard separated unipolar from bipolar forms in 1957. DSM-5-TR defines the episodes by severity and duration. A manic episode is at least one week of abnormally elevated, expansive or irritable mood with increased energy (any duration if hospitalisation is needed), causing marked impairment, hospitalisation or psychotic features. A hypomanic episode has the same kinds of symptoms for at least four days but is not severe enough to cause marked impairment and has no psychosis. Bipolar I needs only one manic episode; depressions are common but not required. Bipolar II needs at least one hypomanic episode and at least one major depressive episode, and never a full manic episode; a first mania changes the diagnosis to bipolar I. Example: a woman with two past depressions has a four-day spell of little sleep, extra productivity and chattiness that friends notice but that does not disrupt her job: bipolar II. If she later spends her savings, believes God has chosen her and is admitted to hospital, the diagnosis becomes bipolar I. Option c is right because mania is required for bipolar I and excluded from bipolar II. Exam trap: bipolar II is not simply milder bipolar I, since its depressions are frequent and disabling, and hypomania can occur in both types, so hypomania alone does not separate them.", "topic": "Bipolar I versus bipolar II"}, {"q": "Which of the following tests is primarily used for assessing frontal lobe functioning?", "options": {"a": "Wisconsin Card Sorting Test", "b": "Wechsler Memory Scale", "c": "Bender-Gestalt Test", "d": "Rey-Osterrieth Complex Figure Test"}, "correct": "a", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is a: the Wisconsin Card Sorting Test (WCST) is the test primarily used to assess frontal lobe functioning. It was devised by David Grant and Esta Berg (1948), and Robert Heaton later wrote its clinical manual (1981). The patient matches response cards to four stimulus cards that differ in colour, shape and number of symbols. The examiner never states the rule and says only 'right' or 'wrong', and after ten correct sorts the rule changes without warning. A healthy person notices the run of 'wrong' feedback and shifts; a patient with frontal damage keeps sorting by the old rule. The key scores are categories achieved, perseverative errors and failure to maintain set, so the test measures executive functions such as abstract reasoning, set-shifting and cognitive flexibility. Brenda Milner (1963) showed in Montreal that patients with dorsolateral frontal excisions made many perseverative errors while those with temporal or posterior lesions did not, establishing the WCST as sensitive to dorsolateral prefrontal dysfunction. Example: after the rule switches from colour to number, a man who has had a frontal stroke keeps placing red cards under the red stimulus card, even while saying the rule has changed. Option a is right because the WCST is the classic executive, frontal-lobe test among the four. Exam trap: the WCST is sensitive to frontal damage but not specific to it, since perseveration also appears in schizophrenia and diffuse damage; and the Rey-Osterrieth figure involves planning in its copy phase but is mainly a test of visuospatial construction and visual memory.", "topic": "Wisconsin Card Sorting Test"}, {"q": "A lesion in the left inferior frontal gyrus is most likely to cause:", "options": {"a": "Wernicke's aphasia", "b": "Broca's aphasia", "c": "Conduction aphasia", "d": "Anomic aphasia"}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: a lesion in the left inferior frontal gyrus is most likely to cause Broca's aphasia. In 1861 the French surgeon and anatomist Paul Broca examined a patient, Leborgne, nicknamed 'Tan' because it was nearly the only syllable he could say; at autopsy Broca found damage in the left frontal lobe. The region, Broca's area, lies in the pars opercularis and pars triangularis of the left inferior frontal gyrus (Brodmann areas 44 and 45), just in front of the motor cortex that controls the face and mouth. It programmes articulation and assembles grammatical sentences. Broca's aphasia, also called expressive or non-fluent aphasia, produces slow, effortful, telegraphic speech made of content words with the small grammatical words missing (agrammatism). Comprehension is relatively preserved, repetition is impaired, and patients know their speech is wrong and are frustrated. Because the motor strip is next door, right-sided weakness is common. Example: asked about his weekend, a man who has had a left frontal stroke says 'Saturday ... son ... park ... ball' and shakes his head in frustration, though he follows spoken instructions well. Option b is right because the left inferior frontal gyrus is the anatomical home of Broca's area. Exam trap: Broca's area is frontal and serves production, while Wernicke's area is temporal and serves comprehension; a handy mnemonic is 'Broca is broken speech, Wernicke is word salad'.", "topic": "Broca's aphasia and its lesion site"}, {"q": "The primary function of the limbic system is:", "options": {"a": "Motor coordination", "b": "Emotion regulation and memory", "c": "Visual processing", "d": "Language production"}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is b: the primary function of the limbic system is emotion regulation and memory. The name comes from the Latin limbus, 'border', because these structures form a ring around the thalamus at the inner edge of the cerebral hemispheres. In 1937 James Papez proposed an emotional circuit running from the hippocampus through the fornix, mammillary bodies, anterior thalamus and cingulate gyrus and back to the hippocampus. Paul MacLean added the amygdala and other structures and named the whole the limbic system in 1952. Its main members are the hippocampus, which forms new declarative memories and spatial maps; the amygdala, which detects threat and drives fear and emotional memory; the cingulate cortex; the fornix; the septum; and the mammillary bodies, with the hypothalamus closely linked. Evidence comes from H.M., who lost both medial temporal lobes in 1953 surgery and could no longer form new declarative memories, and from the monkeys of Heinrich Klüver and Paul Bucy, which became tame and fearless after removal of the temporal lobes including the amygdala. Example: you remember exactly where you were when you heard shocking news because the amygdala tags the event as emotional and the hippocampus stores it strongly. Option b is right because emotion and memory are the two functions every textbook assigns to the limbic system. Exam trap: the basal ganglia sit close by and help select movements, but motor coordination belongs to the cerebellum, not to the limbic system.", "topic": "Functions of the limbic system"}, {"q": "The MMPI-2 is primarily used to assess:", "options": {"a": "Intelligence", "b": "Personality traits and psychopathology", "c": "Neuropsychological deficits", "d": "Emotional intelligence"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: the MMPI-2 is primarily used to assess personality traits and psychopathology. The original Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory was built by the psychologist Starke Hathaway and the psychiatrist J. Charnley McKinley at the University of Minnesota and published in 1943. They used empirical (criterion) keying: an item joined a scale if patients with a given diagnosis answered it differently from normal people, whatever the item seemed to be about. The MMPI-2 (James Butcher and colleagues, 1989) has 567 true-or-false items, updated wording and new US norms. Its validity scales check test-taking attitude, such as L (lie), F (infrequency) and K (defensiveness), and its ten clinical scales include Hypochondriasis, Depression, Hysteria, Psychopathic Deviate, Paranoia, Psychasthenia, Schizophrenia and Hypomania. Scores are T scores, with 65 or above marking clinical elevation, and clinicians read the pattern of high scales as a profile code. It is used in psychiatric diagnosis, treatment planning, forensic and custody evaluations and screening for high-risk jobs. Example: a profile with high scales 1 and 3 and a lower scale 2, the 'conversion V', suggests a person who turns stress into physical complaints. Option b is right because the MMPI-2 is the most widely used objective inventory of personality and psychopathology. Exam trap: it is an objective self-report test, not a projective one, and its relatives form one family: the MMPI-A for adolescents (1992), the MMPI-2-RF (2008, 338 items) and the MMPI-3 (2020).", "topic": "Purpose of the MMPI-2"}, {"q": "The WAIS-IV measures which of the following cognitive abilities?", "options": {"a": "Verbal comprehension, perceptual reasoning, working memory, and processing speed", "b": "Emotional intelligence, memory, and executive functioning", "c": "Personality traits and problem-solving skills", "d": "Sensory-motor integration and coordination"}, "correct": "a", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is a: the WAIS-IV measures verbal comprehension, perceptual reasoning, working memory and processing speed. David Wechsler, chief psychologist at New York's Bellevue Hospital, published the Wechsler-Bellevue scale in 1939 because the Stanford-Binet suited adults poorly, leaned heavily on words and relied on mental age. The Wechsler Adult Intelligence Scale followed in 1955, and the fourth edition, the WAIS-IV, appeared in 2008 for ages 16 to 90. It dropped the old Verbal IQ and Performance IQ and organised its ten core subtests into four index scores: Verbal Comprehension (Similarities, Vocabulary, Information), Perceptual Reasoning (Block Design, Matrix Reasoning, Visual Puzzles), Working Memory (Digit Span, Arithmetic) and Processing Speed (Symbol Search, Coding). The four combine into the Full Scale IQ, a deviation IQ with a mean of 100 and a standard deviation of 15, and an optional General Ability Index uses only the first two. Example: a student with a learning disorder may score 115 on Verbal Comprehension but 82 on Processing Speed, a gap that the index profile reveals and a single IQ would hide. Option a is right because those four domains are exactly the four WAIS-IV indices. Exam trap: the WISC-V (2014) and the WAIS-5 (2024) have five primary indices because perceptual reasoning was split into visual spatial and fluid reasoning, so do not carry a five-index answer into a WAIS-IV question.", "topic": "WAIS-IV index scores"}, {"q": "Which psychological test is used to assess attention deficits in children?", "options": {"a": "Wisconsin Card Sorting Test", "b": "Connors' Continuous Performance Test", "c": "Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory", "d": "Beck Depression Inventory"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is b: the Conners' Continuous Performance Test (the paper spells it Connors') is the test used to assess attention deficits in children. The continuous performance test paradigm began with H. Enger Rosvold and colleagues (1956), who used it to detect brain damage through lapses of sustained attention. C. Keith Conners, an American psychologist also known for the Conners Rating Scales for ADHD, developed the computerised Conners' CPT, now in its third edition (Conners CPT 3), with a separate version for young children. Letters flash on a screen in a long, monotonous stream and the child must press a key for every letter except X, which appears only rarely, so the task demands steady vigilance and the inhibition of a habitual response. Errors of omission (missed responses) reflect inattention, errors of commission (responding when one should not) reflect impulsivity, and slow or highly variable reaction times reflect poor sustained attention. Scores are compared with age norms and support, but never alone make, a diagnosis of attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder. Example: a nine-year-old whose omission errors and reaction-time variability climb steeply in the last blocks of the test shows the declining vigilance typical of ADHD. Option b is right because it is the only option built to measure attention. Exam trap: the correct spelling is Conners, after C. Keith Conners, not Connors; and the WCST also needs attention, but it is primarily a test of executive set-shifting rather than sustained attention.", "topic": "Conners' Continuous Performance Test"}, {"q": "Which of the following is a core principle of cognitive-behavioural therapy (CBT)?", "options": {"a": "Free association", "b": "Identifying and modifying dysfunctional thoughts", "c": "Dream analysis", "d": "Unconscious conflict resolution"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: identifying and modifying dysfunctional thoughts is a core principle of cognitive-behavioural therapy (CBT). CBT rests on the cognitive model: it is not events themselves but how we interpret them that shapes our feelings and behaviour, and thoughts, emotions, bodily sensations and actions keep feeding one another. Two founders stand out: Albert Ellis, whose rational therapy (1955) became rational emotive behaviour therapy, and Aaron Beck, whose cognitive therapy grew from his research on depression. In Beck's approach therapist and client work in collaborative empiricism, treating thoughts as hypotheses to be tested rather than facts. Socratic questioning ('What is the evidence? Is there another way to see it?') guides the client to notice overlooked information. The dysfunctional thought record (1979) has columns for the situation, the emotion and its intensity, the automatic thought, a balanced response and the outcome. Behavioural experiments, activity scheduling and homework turn the new thinking into action, and sessions are structured, time-limited and focused on present problems. Example: a man with social anxiety thinks 'Everyone will see me sweating and think I am weird'; he records the thought, rates his belief at 90 percent, weighs the evidence and tests it by speaking at a meeting, after which his belief falls to 30 percent. Option b is right because cognitive restructuring of dysfunctional thoughts is the defining method of CBT. Exam trap: free association, dream analysis and resolving unconscious conflict are all psychoanalytic; CBT works with conscious, accessible thoughts in the present.", "topic": "Core principle of CBT"}, {"q": "In exposure therapy for anxiety disorders, the patient is:", "options": {"a": "Gradually exposed to the feared stimulus in a controlled manner", "b": "Taught to suppress anxious thoughts", "c": "Encouraged to avoid anxiety-provoking situations", "d": "Given medication to reduce anxiety before exposure"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: in exposure therapy the patient is gradually exposed to the feared stimulus in a controlled manner. Exposure grew out of behaviour therapy, especially Joseph Wolpe's systematic desensitisation (1958), which paired gradual imagined exposure with relaxation. Modern exposure therapy is repeated or prolonged confrontation with a feared object, situation, memory or bodily sensation, without escape, until the fear subsides. Therapist and client first build a fear hierarchy, rating each step on the Subjective Units of Distress Scale (SUDS, 0 to 100), and then climb it step by step. It works through habituation, as anxiety falls with repeated contact, and through extinction or new learning, as the feared outcome fails to happen. Exposure can be in vivo (real life), imaginal, interoceptive (deliberately producing feared sensations in panic disorder) or by virtual reality; exposure and response prevention for OCD and Edna Foa's prolonged exposure for PTSD are leading variants. Example: a woman afraid of dogs first looks at photographs, then watches a dog through a window, then stands across the room from a calm puppy and finally strokes it, staying in each situation until her SUDS rating has halved. Option a is right because gradual, controlled, repeated contact with the feared stimulus is the essence of exposure. Exam trap: flooding is also exposure but starts at the top of the hierarchy at full intensity, so 'gradually' describes graded exposure, the commonest form; and avoidance and safety behaviours maintain anxiety, which is why exposure removes them rather than adding them.", "topic": "Graded exposure therapy"}, {"q": "Which type of therapy focuses on the client's capacity for self-actualization?", "options": {"a": "Cognitive therapy", "b": "Humanistic therapy", "c": "Behavioral therapy", "d": "Psychoanalysis"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: humanistic therapy focuses on the client's capacity for self-actualisation. Humanistic psychology, the 'third force' after psychoanalysis and behaviourism, took shape in the United States in the mid-twentieth century around Abraham Maslow and Carl Rogers. It sees people as basically trustworthy, free to choose and naturally striving to grow. Maslow (1943) placed self-actualisation, becoming everything one is capable of becoming, at the top of his hierarchy of needs. Rogers built a therapy on the same faith. His client-centred therapy (Client-Centered Therapy, 1951) assumes an inborn actualising tendency that is blocked when a child absorbs conditions of worth, producing incongruence between self-concept and experience. Healing comes not from interpretation or advice but from three core conditions offered by the therapist: genuineness (congruence), unconditional positive regard and accurate empathic understanding. In that climate the client's own growth tendency resumes and moves the person towards becoming fully functioning. Example: a student who has always studied engineering to please her parents is allowed, without judgement, to voice her love of music; she gradually trusts her own valuing and chooses her own path. Option b is right because self-actualisation and the actualising tendency are the centre of humanistic theory and therapy. Exam trap: existential therapy, a close cousin, aims at meaning, freedom and responsibility, so 'meaning' points to existential and 'self-actualisation' to humanistic; strictly, Rogers's own term was the actualising tendency and self-actualisation is Maslow's.", "topic": "Humanistic therapy and self-actualisation"}, {"q": "Which of the following therapeutic approaches emphasizes modifying maladaptive behaviours through reinforcement?", "options": {"a": "Psychoanalysis", "b": "Cognitive therapy", "c": "Behavior therapy", "d": "Humanistic therapy"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: behaviour therapy emphasises modifying maladaptive behaviours through reinforcement. Behaviour therapy applies experimentally established principles of learning to psychological problems. It grew from three roots in the 1950s: Joseph Wolpe and Arnold Lazarus in South Africa, working from classical conditioning; Hans Eysenck and Monte Shapiro at the Maudsley Hospital in London; and B. F. Skinner and his students in the United States, who carried operant conditioning onto hospital wards and into classrooms. The name first appeared in a 1953 report by Ogden Lindsley, Skinner and Harry Solomon. Its assumptions are that problem behaviour is learned by the same principles as normal behaviour, that the behaviour itself is the problem rather than a sign of hidden conflict, and that change must be measured. The operant strand uses reinforcement directly: positive reinforcement rewards a target behaviour immediately, shaping reinforces successive approximations, the Premack principle uses a preferred activity as the reward, and the token economy (Teodoro Ayllon and Nathan Azrin, 1968) gives tokens exchangeable for privileges. Unwanted behaviour is weakened by extinction, time-out or response cost. Example: a child with autism who never asks for things is rewarded first for eye contact, then for any sound, then for saying 'wa' and finally for saying 'water'. Option c is right because reinforcement is the core tool of behaviour therapy. Exam trap: cognitive therapy also sets homework and behavioural experiments, but its target is thoughts; reinforcement of overt behaviour is the behaviourist signature.", "topic": "Behaviour therapy and reinforcement"}, {"q": "Dialectical Behavior Therapy (DBT) was originally developed for treating which disorder?", "options": {"a": "Major depressive disorder", "b": "Schizophrenia", "c": "Borderline personality disorder", "d": "Generalized anxiety disorder"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: dialectical behaviour therapy (DBT) was originally developed for treating borderline personality disorder. Marsha Linehan, an American psychologist at the University of Washington, developed it for chronically suicidal, self-harming women, most of whom met criteria for borderline personality disorder (BPD), and published the treatment manuals in 1993. Standard CBT's constant push for change felt invalidating to these clients and many dropped out, yet acceptance alone did not stop the self-harm. DBT's dialectic synthesises the two: full acceptance of the person together with a firm push for change. The acceptance side, including mindfulness, came from Zen practice; the change side came from behaviour therapy. Linehan's biosocial theory sees BPD as pervasive emotion dysregulation that arises when a biologically sensitive, reactive child grows up in an invalidating environment. Standard DBT has four modes: individual therapy, a weekly skills group, telephone coaching between sessions and a therapist consultation team. Its four skills modules are core mindfulness, distress tolerance, emotion regulation and interpersonal effectiveness. Example: a 22-year-old who cuts herself after arguments learns distress tolerance skills, such as holding ice or exercising hard, to ride out the urge, and then uses interpersonal effectiveness skills to ask for what she needs. Option c is right because DBT was designed for, and first tested with, suicidal clients with BPD. Exam trap: DBT has since been adapted for eating disorders, substance use, adolescents and depression, so 'used for' is broad, but 'originally developed for' always means borderline personality disorder.", "topic": "Origins of dialectical behaviour therapy"}, {"q": "Which therapy technique involves identifying and challenging irrational beliefs?", "options": {"a": "Rational Emotive Behavior Therapy (REBT)", "b": "Client-centered therapy", "c": "Psychoanalysis", "d": "Gestalt therapy"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: rational emotive behaviour therapy (REBT) is the technique that involves identifying and challenging irrational beliefs. Albert Ellis, a New York psychologist trained in psychoanalysis, grew frustrated with its slow, passive method and launched rational therapy in 1955; it became rational emotive therapy in 1961 and REBT in 1993. Its core is the ABC model: an Activating event (A) does not directly cause the emotional and behavioural Consequence (C); the Belief (B) about the event does. Rational beliefs are flexible preferences ('I would like to pass'); irrational beliefs are rigid demands, or musts ('I must pass, or I am worthless'), with offshoots such as awfulising, low frustration tolerance and rating one's whole self. The therapist then Disputes (D) each belief actively and directly, asking whether it is logical, realistic and helpful, to reach an Effective new philosophy (E). Ellis grouped the musts into three, about oneself, about others and about the world, and aimed at unconditional self-acceptance: rate your actions, never your whole self. Example: after a rejection a man feels depressed because he believes 'I must be loved by everyone, and it is awful that she rejected me'; the therapist challenges the demand until he holds the preference 'I wanted her to like me, but I can bear this and still accept myself'. Option a is right because disputing irrational beliefs is the defining REBT method. Exam trap: Beck's cognitive therapy also challenges thoughts, but through gentler Socratic questioning of automatic thoughts; 'irrational beliefs' and forceful disputing are Ellis's signature.", "topic": "REBT and irrational beliefs"}, {"q": "In psychodynamic therapy, which defense mechanism involves attributing one's own unacceptable feelings to others?", "options": {"a": "Projection", "b": "Repression", "c": "Sublimation", "d": "Displacement"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: projection is the defence mechanism that involves attributing one's own unacceptable feelings to others. Defence mechanisms are the ego's unconscious strategies for protecting itself from anxiety. Sigmund Freud described many of them, and his daughter Anna Freud set them out systematically in The Ego and the Mechanisms of Defence (1936). In projection, an impulse or feeling that would be too threatening to acknowledge as one's own, such as hostility, envy or sexual desire, is disowned and perceived instead in another person. The feeling does not change; only its owner does. Freud linked projection to paranoia, and George Vaillant later placed it among the immature defences in his hierarchy, below neurotic defences such as repression and displacement and mature ones such as sublimation and humour. Freud also used the word in a broader sense for any colouring of perception by the inner world, which is the basis of projective tests such as the Rorschach. Example: a man attracted to a colleague, who cannot accept the feeling, accuses his wife of flirting with everyone; or a student who dislikes her roommate insists, 'She hates me'. Option a is right because attributing one's own unacceptable feelings to others is the textbook definition of projection. Exam trap: in displacement the feeling stays yours but moves to a safer target, whereas in projection the feeling is placed in someone else; ask whether the owner or the target has changed.", "topic": "Projection as a defence mechanism"}, {"q": "What is the main goal of existential therapy?", "options": {"a": "Resolving unconscious conflicts", "b": "Enhancing self-actualization", "c": "Finding meaning in life", "d": "Modifying maladaptive behaviours"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: the main goal of existential therapy is finding meaning in life. Existential therapy is more a philosophy of living than a set of techniques. It draws on philosophers such as Søren Kierkegaard, Jean-Paul Sartre and Martin Heidegger, whose Being and Time (1927) described human existence as Dasein, 'being there' in a world. Ludwig Binswanger and Medard Boss brought these ideas into psychiatry, Rollo May carried them to America, and Irvin Yalom, in Existential Psychotherapy (1980), named four ultimate concerns: death, freedom with its responsibility, isolation and meaninglessness. Anxiety about these givens is normal; problems arise when people flee into inauthentic living. The therapist helps the client face the givens honestly, accept responsibility for choices and build a meaningful life. Viktor Frankl, a Viennese psychiatrist who survived the Nazi camps, made meaning the explicit centre of his logotherapy and of Man's Search for Meaning (German original, 1946): the primary human motive is the will to meaning, and its frustration produces an existential vacuum. Meaning comes from creating, from loving and experiencing, and from the attitude one takes to unavoidable suffering. Example: a widower who feels his life is pointless is helped to see that caring for his grandchildren and honouring his wife's memory can give his remaining years purpose. Option c is right because confronting meaninglessness and finding meaning is the core aim of existential therapy. Exam trap: self-actualisation is the humanistic goal of Maslow and Rogers; the schools overlap and are sometimes taught together as existential-humanistic therapy, but 'meaning' is the existential keyword.", "topic": "Goal of existential therapy"}, {"q": "Which psychologist is known as the father of modern psychology?", "options": {"a": "Sigmund Freud", "b": "Wilhelm Wundt", "c": "Carl Rogers", "d": "B.F. Skinner"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: Wilhelm Wundt is known as the father of modern psychology. Wundt, a German physician who had worked as an assistant to Hermann von Helmholtz, argued that the mind could be studied with the experimental methods of physiology. In Principles of Physiological Psychology (1873 to 1874) he announced a new science, and in 1879 at the University of Leipzig his students began original research in what is regarded as the first psychology laboratory; 1879 is the conventional birth year of psychology as an independent science. He founded the journal Philosophische Studien in 1881 and trained many pioneers, including Edward Titchener, G. Stanley Hall, James McKeen Cattell, Lightner Witmer and Hugo Münsterberg. Wundt studied immediate conscious experience, sensations and feelings, through introspection under controlled experimental conditions: trained observers gave brief reports on precisely timed stimuli. Because he stressed attention and will in organising experience, his system is called voluntarism, and Titchener's structuralism was a narrower version of it. Example: in a Leipzig reaction-time study, an observer pressed a key when a light flashed and then reported whether attention had been on the light or on the movement. Option b is right because Wundt gave psychology its first laboratory, its first journal and its first trained students, making it a discipline separate from philosophy. Exam trap: 'father' depends on the qualifier: William James is called the father of American psychology, Freud the father of psychoanalysis, Witmer the father of clinical psychology and Fechner the founder of psychophysics.", "topic": "Wundt, father of modern psychology"}, {"q": "What is the primary focus of applied psychology?", "options": {"a": "Theoretical concepts", "b": "Experiments", "c": "Practical application in real-world settings", "d": "Research methodology"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: the primary focus of applied psychology is the practical application of psychological knowledge in real-world settings. Psychology has two broad aims. Basic (pure) research seeks understanding for its own sake, asking, for example, how memory or perception works. Applied research and practice use that knowledge to solve concrete problems in clinics, schools, workplaces, courts, hospitals and communities. Applied psychology took shape in the decades around 1900. Lightner Witmer, a student of Wundt, opened the first psychological clinic at the University of Pennsylvania in 1896, the start of clinical psychology. Hugo Münsterberg, another of Wundt's students, applied psychology to eyewitness testimony in On the Witness Stand (1908) and to work in Psychology and Industrial Efficiency (1913), founding forensic and industrial psychology. The intelligence test of Alfred Binet (1905) and the mass testing of soldiers in the First World War spread applied work further. Today its main fields include clinical, counselling, educational and school, industrial-organisational, health, forensic, sports and community psychology. Example: basic research shows that leading questions can distort memory; a forensic psychologist applies that finding to design police interview protocols that avoid suggestive questioning. Option c is right because applied psychology is defined by using psychological principles to solve practical, real-world problems. Exam trap: applied psychologists still run experiments and use research methods, so 'experiments' and 'research methodology' are tools shared with basic psychology, not the defining focus of the applied field.", "topic": "Applied versus basic psychology"}, {"q": "What is a primary focus of Acceptance and Commitment Therapy (ACT)?", "options": {"a": "Challenging irrational beliefs", "b": "Suppressing negative emotions", "c": "Developing psychological flexibility", "d": "Exploring unconscious conflicts"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: the central aim of Acceptance and Commitment Therapy (ACT) is developing psychological flexibility. ACT (said as the word 'act') was developed by Steven C. Hayes with Kirk Strosahl and Kelly Wilson; their main book appeared in 1999, and it is one of the third-wave cognitive behavioural therapies. It rests on relational frame theory, Hayes's account of human language: because words link events together, the memory of a failure can hurt almost as much as failing. People then try to escape or control painful thoughts and feelings (experiential avoidance), and this struggle narrows their lives. ACT therefore does not try to remove symptoms or win arguments with thoughts. Psychological flexibility means staying in contact with the present moment, thoughts and feelings included, while acting on one's chosen values. It is built through six processes, the hexaflex: acceptance, cognitive defusion, contact with the present moment, self-as-context, values and committed action. Example: a socially anxious student learns to say 'I am having the thought that everyone will laugh at me', notices the anxiety without fighting it, and still raises her hand in class because learning matters to her. Option c is right because psychological flexibility is the stated overall goal of ACT and sits at the centre of the hexaflex. Exam trap: 'challenging irrational beliefs' sounds like any CBT, but ACT deliberately does not dispute the content of thoughts; it changes the person's relationship to them, which is what makes it third wave rather than second wave.", "topic": "ACT and psychological flexibility"}, {"q": "Which technique is commonly used in Motivational Interviewing?", "options": {"a": "Free association", "b": "Reflective listening", "c": "Exposure therapy", "d": "Systematic desensitization"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: reflective listening is a core technique of Motivational Interviewing (MI). MI was first described by the psychologist William R. Miller in 1983 for problem drinkers and developed with Stephen Rollnick in their book Motivational Interviewing (1991). It is a collaborative, person-centred but goal-directed way of helping someone resolve ambivalence, the state of wanting and not wanting to change at the same time. Arguing for change usually makes the client defend the status quo, so the counsellor instead draws out the client's own reasons for change ('change talk'). The core skills are summarised as OARS: Open questions, Affirmations, Reflections (reflective listening) and Summaries. In a reflection the counsellor works out what the client means and says it back as a statement, often stressing the side that favours change. The earlier principles were express empathy, develop discrepancy, roll with resistance and support self-efficacy. Example: a client says, 'I know drinking is hurting my marriage, but it is the only way I relax.' The counsellor reflects, 'Part of you is worried about your marriage, and drinking feels like your only way to unwind', which lets the client hear both sides of his own ambivalence. Option b is right because reflective listening (the R of OARS) is the most used MI skill, inherited from Carl Rogers's client-centred approach. Exam trap: MI is often paired with the stages of change of Prochaska and DiClemente, but that is a separate model; the MI technique asked about here is reflective listening, not exposure, confrontation or advice-giving.", "topic": "Motivational interviewing: reflective listening"}, {"q": "Which therapy is most effective for Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder (OCD)?", "options": {"a": "Psychoanalysis", "b": "Cognitive Behavioral Therapy (CBT) with Exposure and Response Prevention", "c": "Client-centered therapy", "d": "Gestalt therapy"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: cognitive behavioural therapy (CBT) with exposure and response prevention (ERP) is the most effective psychological treatment for obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD). In OCD, intrusive obsessions (for example, 'my hands are contaminated') raise anxiety, and compulsions such as washing or checking bring brief relief. That relief negatively reinforces the compulsion, so the person never learns that the feared outcome would not happen and that anxiety falls on its own. ERP was introduced by the British psychologist Victor Meyer in 1966 and later refined by Edna Foa and others. It has two parts: exposure, in which the client faces the feared cue step by step up a hierarchy, and response prevention, in which the client deliberately does not perform the ritual. Anxiety then falls through habituation and extinction, and the obsession loses its power. Cognitive work is often added to tackle beliefs such as inflated responsibility or the overimportance of thoughts. Example: a woman who washes her hands 40 times a day touches a door handle and then refrains from washing for an hour, rating her anxiety as it peaks and then drops; over weeks the urge weakens. Option b is right because clinical guidelines (for example, NICE in the United Kingdom) recommend CBT including ERP as the first-line psychological treatment for OCD, often alongside SSRIs. Exam trap: insight-oriented therapies sound attractive because obsessions seem 'deep', but OCD responds to behavioural exposure, not interpretation; this paper asks the same question again as Q43, with ERP named on its own.", "topic": "First-line therapy for OCD: ERP"}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT a key element of Dialectical Behavior Therapy (DBT)?", "options": {"a": "Mindfulness", "b": "Interpersonal effectiveness", "c": "Dream analysis", "d": "Emotion regulation"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: dream analysis is not a key element of Dialectical Behaviour Therapy (DBT). DBT was developed by the American psychologist Marsha M. Linehan, whose treatment manuals appeared in 1993, for chronically suicidal women, most of whom had borderline personality disorder. Standard CBT's constant push for change felt invalidating to these clients, while pure acceptance left their self-harm untouched. The 'dialectic' is the synthesis of acceptance and change: the therapist fully validates the client and at the same time pushes firmly for new behaviour. The acceptance side comes from Zen practice, the change side from behaviour therapy. Linehan's biosocial theory sees borderline personality disorder as emotion dysregulation arising when a biologically sensitive child grows up in an invalidating environment. Standard DBT has four modes (individual therapy, a weekly skills group, telephone coaching and a therapist consultation team) and teaches four skills modules: core mindfulness, distress tolerance, emotion regulation and interpersonal effectiveness. Example: a client feeling an urge to cut herself uses a distress tolerance skill such as holding ice, then uses emotion regulation skills to name the feeling and act opposite to the urge. Option c is right because dream analysis is a psychoanalytic technique (Freud's 'royal road to the unconscious') and has no place in DBT's skills-based, present-focused model. Exam trap: in NOT questions all three wrong options are true DBT elements; students sometimes doubt 'mindfulness' because it sounds like ACT or MBCT, but core mindfulness is DBT's first module.", "topic": "DBT components: what does not belong"}, {"q": "In which therapy approach is the \"miracle question\" commonly used?", "options": {"a": "Solution-Focused Brief Therapy", "b": "Cognitive Therapy", "c": "Existential Therapy", "d": "Psychodynamic Therapy"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: the 'miracle question' is a signature technique of Solution-Focused Brief Therapy (SFBT). SFBT was developed by Steve de Shazer and Insoo Kim Berg and their colleagues at the Brief Family Therapy Center in Milwaukee, founded in 1978; de Shazer set out the miracle question in his 1988 book Clues. SFBT deliberately does not dig into the causes or history of a problem. It assumes that clients already have resources and that small changes can snowball, so therapy is brief and builds on what the client wants and what already works. Its main tools are questions: the miracle question (picturing life without the problem), exception questions (times when the problem was absent or smaller), scaling questions ('on a scale of 0 to 10, where are you now?') and coping questions. Example: the therapist asks, 'Suppose tonight, while you are asleep, a miracle happens and the problem that brought you here is solved. When you wake up, what is the first thing you would notice that tells you things are different?' A client may answer, 'I would have breakfast with my family instead of skipping it', which becomes a concrete first goal. Option a is right because the miracle question was created within, and is identified with, solution-focused brief therapy. Exam trap: narrative therapy (Michael White and David Epston) is the other postmodern brief therapy; its signature is externalising the problem, not the miracle question, so do not swap the two.", "topic": "Miracle question in SFBT"}, {"q": "Which of the following is a key component of trauma-focused CBT?", "options": {"a": "Exposure to traumatic memories", "b": "Free association", "c": "Dream interpretation", "d": "Systematic desensitization"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: gradual exposure to traumatic memories, through the trauma narrative, is a key component of trauma-focused cognitive behavioural therapy (TF-CBT). TF-CBT was developed by the child psychiatrist Judith A. Cohen with the psychologists Anthony P. Mannarino and Esther Deblinger, for children and adolescents (roughly 3 to 18 years) who have suffered abuse or other trauma, usually with a non-offending parent joining sessions. Its components are remembered by the acronym PRACTICE: Psychoeducation and parenting skills, Relaxation, Affect modulation, Cognitive coping, Trauma narrative and processing, In vivo mastery of trauma reminders, Conjoint child-parent sessions and Enhancing safety. Coping skills are taught first so that the child can tolerate distress; then the child gradually tells, writes or draws the story of the trauma in more and more detail. Repeated retelling in a safe setting reduces the fear attached to the memory and lets the therapist correct beliefs such as 'it was my fault'. Example: a nine-year-old who was sexually abused writes a 'book' about what happened, one chapter per session, and later reads it to her mother in a joint session. Option a is right because the trauma narrative is a structured, graded exposure to the traumatic memories and is the central goal of the model. Exam trap: option d, systematic desensitisation, sounds close because TF-CBT 'desensitises' memories, but systematic desensitisation is Joseph Wolpe's named phobia technique (relaxation plus a fear hierarchy), not a TF-CBT component.", "topic": "TF-CBT and the trauma narrative"}, {"q": "Which therapy technique involves challenging cognitive distortions?", "options": {"a": "Behavioral activation", "b": "Mindfulness meditation", "c": "Cognitive restructuring", "d": "Flooding"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: cognitive restructuring is the technique that involves identifying and challenging cognitive distortions. The idea comes from the cognitive therapies of Albert Ellis (rational therapy from 1955) and especially Aaron T. Beck, who from the 1960s described automatic thoughts and the systematic errors of thinking behind depression, such as overgeneralisation and arbitrary inference. Cognitive restructuring treats a thought as a hypothesis to test rather than a fact. The client learns to catch the automatic thought, name the distortion, weigh the evidence for and against it, and build a more balanced alternative. Beck's therapists do this through collaborative empiricism and Socratic questioning ('What is the evidence? How else could you see it?'), and clients practise it with the dysfunctional thought record (1979), which lists the situation, the emotion rated 0 to 100, the automatic thought, a balanced response and the outcome. Behavioural experiments then test predictions in real life. Example: a student who gets a B thinks, 'I am stupid; I will never pass the course.' Restructuring asks what her other grades are and what she would tell a friend, leading to, 'One B in a hard course does not make me stupid; I can revise this topic.' Her low mood eases as the thought changes. Option c is right because challenging and replacing distorted thoughts is the very definition of cognitive restructuring. Exam trap: behavioural activation is also part of Beck's package for depression, so students pick it, but it changes behaviour (scheduling activity), not the content of thoughts.", "topic": "Cognitive restructuring of distortions"}, {"q": "Which therapy technique is based on the concept of \"unconditional positive regard\"?", "options": {"a": "Psychoanalysis", "b": "Client-centered therapy", "c": "Behavioral therapy", "d": "Gestalt therapy"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: client-centred therapy is built on the concept of unconditional positive regard. It was developed by the American psychologist Carl Rogers (1902 to 1987), first as non-directive counselling in Counseling and Psychotherapy (1942) and then as client-centred therapy in his 1951 book of that name; it is now usually called person-centred therapy. Rogers held that every person has an actualising tendency, a drive to grow. Problems arise from conditions of worth: a child who is loved only when she meets others' standards learns to deny feelings that do not fit, creating incongruence between self-concept and experience. In a 1957 paper Rogers named the 'necessary and sufficient' conditions for change, three of which are the therapist's core conditions: congruence (genuineness), unconditional positive regard (warm acceptance of the client as a person, without judgement or conditions) and accurate empathic understanding. When the client feels accepted, the need to defend the self falls and he can own denied feelings. Example: a client admits he resents his ill mother. The therapist neither praises nor criticises but accepts him and reflects, 'You feel angry, and guilty about feeling angry', so he can explore the feeling rather than hide it. Option b is right because unconditional positive regard is one of Rogers's core conditions and the defining attitude of client-centred therapy. Exam trap: unconditional positive regard is an attitude, not a technique, and it does not mean approving of every behaviour; it means valuing the person, which separates it from praise or reinforcement in behaviour therapy.", "topic": "Unconditional positive regard: Rogers"}, {"q": "Which of the following is an essential component of Mindfulness-Based Cognitive Therapy (MBCT)?", "options": {"a": "Challenging automatic thoughts", "b": "Enhancing self-awareness and acceptance", "c": "Encouraging free association", "d": "Interpreting unconscious conflicts"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: enhancing self-awareness and acceptance is an essential component of Mindfulness-Based Cognitive Therapy (MBCT). MBCT was developed by Zindel V. Segal, J. Mark G. Williams and John D. Teasdale and set out in their 2002 book. They combined Jon Kabat-Zinn's mindfulness-based stress reduction (MBSR, begun in 1979) with ideas from cognitive therapy, and designed MBCT as an eight-week group course for people who have recovered from recurrent major depression, to prevent relapse. The key insight is that relapse is triggered when a small dip in mood reactivates old patterns of negative, ruminative thinking. Rather than arguing with those thoughts, MBCT teaches decentering: noticing thoughts as passing mental events, not facts, and accepting feelings without being drawn into them. Practices include the body scan, sitting meditation and the three-minute breathing space. In a trial by Teasdale and colleagues (2000), patients with three or more past episodes who received MBCT relapsed far less often than those given usual care. Example: a recovered patient wakes up feeling low and notices 'here is the thought that I am a failure again'; she takes a breathing space and lets the thought pass instead of ruminating for hours. Option b is right because awareness of one's present experience and acceptance of it, the stance of decentering, is the core of MBCT. Exam trap: option a, challenging automatic thoughts, is the method of Beck's standard cognitive therapy; although MBCT has 'cognitive' in its name, it changes the relationship to thoughts, not their content.", "topic": "MBCT: decentering and acceptance"}, {"q": "Which therapeutic approach was developed by Fritz Perls?", "options": {"a": "Gestalt Therapy", "b": "Cognitive Behavioral Therapy", "c": "Rational Emotive Behavior Therapy", "d": "Dialectical Behavior Therapy"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: Fritz Perls developed Gestalt therapy. Frederick Salomon 'Fritz' Perls (1893 to 1970), a German-born psychiatrist trained in psychoanalysis, created Gestalt therapy with his wife Laura Perls and the writer Paul Goodman; their book Gestalt Therapy appeared in 1951, and Perls later made the approach famous through workshops at the Esalen Institute in California. Gestalt therapy is an experiential, humanistic, here-and-now therapy. Its central aim is awareness: helping the client notice what he is feeling, thinking and doing in the present moment, take responsibility for it ('I' language rather than 'it' or 'you') and complete unfinished business, unexpressed feelings from the past that still intrude. Perls described the 'topdog' (the demanding 'you should' voice) and the 'underdog' (the excuse-making voice). Signature techniques include the empty chair, the hot seat in group workshops, and dream work in which the client speaks as each part of the dream, treating it as a disowned part of the self. Example: a woman who never said goodbye to her late father is asked to imagine him in an empty chair and tell him what she wishes she had said; her grief, held back for years, is finally expressed. Option a is right because Perls is the recognised founder of Gestalt therapy. Exam trap: Gestalt therapy is not Gestalt psychology, the perception school of Max Wertheimer, Wolfgang Köhler and Kurt Koffka; Perls borrowed the word 'Gestalt' (whole), but the perception school rejected his use of its terms.", "topic": "Founder of Gestalt therapy"}, {"q": "In cognitive therapy, which cognitive distortion involves interpreting ambiguous situations negatively?", "options": {"a": "Catastrophizing", "b": "Overgeneralization", "c": "Personalization", "d": "Selective abstraction"}, "correct": "d", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is d: the official key gives selective abstraction as the distortion in which ambiguous situations are read negatively. Aaron T. Beck, who set out the cognitive model of depression in Depression (1967) and its treatment in Cognitive Therapy of Depression (1979, with colleagues), named a set of systematic errors in thinking: arbitrary inference, selective abstraction, overgeneralisation, magnification and minimisation, personalisation and dichotomous thinking. David Burns later popularised a longer list in Feeling Good (1980). Selective abstraction, also called the mental filter, means focusing on one negative detail taken out of context while ignoring the rest of the situation, so that the whole experience is judged by that one detail. In an ambiguous situation that holds both good and bad cues, the person filters out the positive and builds a negative conclusion. Example: a teacher gives a lecture that most students enjoy, but she notices two students yawning and concludes, 'My class finds me boring.' Option d is right because, of the four choices, selective abstraction is the only distortion about how a mixed or unclear situation is processed, by attending only to its negative part. Exam trap: catastrophising (expecting the worst), overgeneralisation (a broad rule from one event) and personalisation (blaming oneself for outside events) all sound negative, so learn each by its exact mechanism rather than by its mood. Key note: strictly, 'interpreting ambiguous situations negatively' without evidence fits Beck's arbitrary inference (jumping to conclusions, including mind reading), which is not offered; selective abstraction is the best of the four options, and the official key stands.", "topic": "Beck's distortions: selective abstraction"}, {"q": "Which technique is NOT commonly used in psychoanalytic therapy?", "options": {"a": "Free association", "b": "Dream analysis", "c": "Cognitive restructuring", "d": "Transference interpretation"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is c: cognitive restructuring is not a technique of psychoanalytic therapy. Psychoanalysis was developed by the Viennese neurologist Sigmund Freud (1856 to 1939) from the 1890s, after he dropped hypnosis and the cathartic method he had shared with Josef Breuer. Its goal is insight: making unconscious conflicts conscious so that the ego can deal with them. The analyst works with a few classic tools. Free association, Freud's 'fundamental rule', asks the patient to say whatever comes to mind without censoring it. Dream analysis treats dreams as disguised wish fulfilments, decoding the manifest content to reach the latent content (The Interpretation of Dreams, 1900). Transference interpretation shows the patient how he relives feelings toward early figures, such as a parent, in the relationship with the analyst; interpreting resistance is another core tool. Cognitive restructuring, by contrast, comes from the cognitive therapies of Aaron Beck and Albert Ellis: identifying conscious automatic thoughts and distortions, testing them against evidence and replacing them with balanced thoughts. It assumes the problem lies in present conscious thinking, not in buried conflict. Example: a patient who keeps feeling that his analyst is about to reject him is helped to see that this fear echoes his experience with a cold father (transference), rather than being asked to fill in a thought record. Option c is right because cognitive restructuring belongs to cognitive and cognitive behavioural therapy, not psychoanalysis. Exam trap: in NOT questions, three options are genuine psychoanalytic tools; pick the one from a different school, here the CBT technique.", "topic": "Psychoanalytic techniques versus CBT"}, {"q": "In behavioral therapy, systematic desensitization is used primarily to treat:", "options": {"a": "Depression", "b": "Phobias", "c": "Schizophrenia", "d": "Bipolar disorder"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: systematic desensitisation is used primarily to treat phobias. The method was developed by the South African psychiatrist Joseph Wolpe (1915 to 1997) and set out in Psychotherapy by Reciprocal Inhibition (1958). It grew from Mary Cover Jones's 1924 treatment of Peter, a boy afraid of rabbits, and from Wolpe's own experiments with cats made anxious in a cage. The principle is reciprocal inhibition: a person cannot be deeply relaxed and highly anxious at the same time, so if relaxation is paired with a feared stimulus, the relaxation response gradually replaces the fear (counterconditioning). Treatment has three steps. First the client learns deep muscle relaxation, a shortened form of Edmund Jacobson's progressive muscle relaxation. Second, client and therapist build an anxiety hierarchy, ranking feared situations from least to most frightening using subjective units of disturbance (SUDS, 0 to 100). Third, the relaxed client imagines each scene in turn, moving up only when a step no longer causes anxiety. Example: a person with a fear of flying first imagines seeing a plane on television, then booking a ticket, then sitting at the gate, and finally take-off, staying relaxed at each step. Option b is right because systematic desensitisation was designed for, and works best with, specific phobias and other clear, cue-bound fears. Exam trap: it rests on reciprocal inhibition (classical counterconditioning), not on operant extinction or insight, and flooding differs because it presents the most feared stimulus at once, without a hierarchy or relaxation.", "topic": "Systematic desensitisation for phobias"}, {"q": "Which of the following therapies is most effective for treating PTSD?", "options": {"a": "Psychoanalysis", "b": "Trauma-Focused Cognitive Behavioral Therapy (TF-CBT)", "c": "Humanistic Therapy", "d": "Existential Therapy"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: trauma-focused cognitive behavioural therapy (TF-CBT) is the most effective of the listed therapies for post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD). PTSD follows exposure to actual or threatened death, serious injury or sexual violence and shows as intrusive memories and nightmares, avoidance of reminders, negative changes in thinking and mood, and hyperarousal. Trauma-focused CBT is a family of treatments that work directly on the trauma memory and its meaning. Prolonged exposure, developed by Edna B. Foa, combines imaginal exposure (repeatedly retelling the trauma) with in vivo exposure to safe but avoided reminders. Cognitive processing therapy, developed by Patricia Resick for rape survivors, challenges 'stuck points' about blame, safety and trust. For children, the TF-CBT model of Judith Cohen, Anthony Mannarino and Esther Deblinger teaches coping skills and then builds a trauma narrative. Along with eye movement desensitisation and reprocessing (EMDR), from Francine Shapiro, these are the first-line psychological treatments recommended in guidelines such as NICE's. Example: a survivor of a road accident who avoids driving retells the crash in detail in sessions, examines the belief 'it was all my fault', and then drives short distances in steps until the fear fades. Option b is right because trauma-focused CBT has the strongest evidence base for PTSD in both adults and children. Exam trap: the acronym TF-CBT strictly names the Cohen, Mannarino and Deblinger model for children, but in exam options 'trauma-focused CBT' stands for the whole group of trauma-focused cognitive behavioural treatments; supportive humanistic or existential work is not first line.", "topic": "First-line therapy for PTSD"}, {"q": "What is the main focus of interpersonal therapy (IPT)?", "options": {"a": "Resolving unconscious conflicts", "b": "Improving social and communication skills", "c": "Exploring past trauma", "d": "Developing mindfulness skills"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: the official key gives improving social and communication skills as the main focus of interpersonal therapy (IPT). IPT was developed by the American psychiatrist Gerald Klerman (1928 to 1992) and the epidemiologist Myrna Weissman, his wife, and published in Interpersonal Psychotherapy of Depression (1984). It draws on Adolf Meyer, Harry Stack Sullivan and John Bowlby, and holds that depression both follows and worsens problems in current relationships. IPT is brief (usually 12 to 16 weekly sessions) and present-focused; it treats depression as a medical illness, does not interpret transference and does not dig into childhood. After an interpersonal inventory, therapist and patient choose one or two of four problem areas: grief, interpersonal role disputes, role transitions (such as a new job or retirement) and interpersonal deficits (isolation or poor social skills). Techniques include communication analysis, role-play and encouraging the expression of feelings, all aimed at improving how the patient handles relationships so that mood lifts. Example: a woman who became depressed after moving to a new city for her husband's job (a role transition) role-plays telling him how lonely she feels and plans ways to make friends. Option b is right because, of the four choices, it is the only one about current interpersonal functioning, which is what IPT works on. Exam trap: IPT grew from Sullivan's interpersonal theory but is not psychodynamic, so unconscious conflicts and past trauma are wrong. Key note: IPT's precise focus is resolving current interpersonal problems in the four areas and linking them to mood; skills training is mainly a tactic for interpersonal deficits, so b is the closest wording rather than a perfect definition, and the official key stands.", "topic": "Interpersonal therapy: focus and problem areas"}, {"q": "Which of the following is a core skill taught in Dialectical Behavior Therapy (DBT)?", "options": {"a": "Mindfulness", "b": "Free association", "c": "Dream analysis", "d": "Aversion therapy"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: mindfulness is a core skill taught in Dialectical Behaviour Therapy (DBT). DBT was developed by Marsha M. Linehan (manuals published in 1993) for chronically suicidal clients with borderline personality disorder. Linehan combined behaviour therapy's techniques for change with acceptance practices drawn from Zen, which she practised, and with a dialectical stance that holds acceptance and change together. In the weekly skills group, clients learn four modules. Core mindfulness comes first and runs through all the others: observing, describing and participating in experience without judgement, one thing at a time, and finding 'wise mind', the balance between 'emotion mind' and 'reasonable mind'. Distress tolerance helps clients survive crises without making them worse (distraction, self-soothing, radical acceptance). Emotion regulation teaches naming emotions, reducing vulnerability and 'opposite action'. Interpersonal effectiveness teaches asking for what one needs and saying no while keeping relationships and self-respect. Linehan's trials (1991 and 2006) found fewer suicidal acts with DBT than with comparison treatments. Example: a client flooded with rage after a text from her partner pauses, observes the sensations in her body and labels 'he is abandoning me' as a thought, which gives her time to choose a skill instead of self-harming. Option a is right because mindfulness is named as the core module of DBT's skills training. Exam trap: mindfulness also appears in ACT, MBSR and MBCT, so students sometimes reject it as 'not DBT'; it is in fact DBT's foundation skill, while free association, dream analysis and aversion therapy belong to other schools.", "topic": "DBT core skill: mindfulness"}, {"q": "The primary goal of Rational Emotive Behavior Therapy (REBT) is to:", "options": {"a": "Modify irrational beliefs", "b": "Analyze childhood experiences", "c": "Encourage catharsis", "d": "Use dream interpretation"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: the primary goal of Rational Emotive Behaviour Therapy (REBT) is to modify irrational beliefs. REBT was founded by the American psychologist Albert Ellis (1913 to 2007), who left psychoanalysis because insight rarely helped his patients. Drawing on Stoic philosophy (Epictetus taught that people are disturbed not by things but by the views they take of them), he began rational therapy in 1955; it became rational-emotive therapy and, in 1993, REBT. It is usually called the first cognitive behavioural therapy. Its core is the ABC model: A, the activating event; B, the person's beliefs about it; C, the emotional and behavioural consequences. A does not cause C directly; B does. Irrational beliefs are rigid demands ('musts', which Ellis mocked as 'musturbation') plus awfulising, low frustration tolerance and global rating of oneself or others. Therapy adds D, disputing the beliefs by asking for logical, empirical and practical evidence, leading to E, an effective new philosophy. Homework, such as shame-attacking exercises, puts the new beliefs into action. Example: a man is rejected for a job (A), believes 'I must succeed, or I am worthless' (B) and becomes depressed (C). The therapist disputes: 'Where is the evidence that one rejection makes you worthless?' He moves to 'I would prefer to get the job, but I can cope and keep trying' (E) and feels disappointed rather than depressed. Option a is right because changing irrational beliefs into rational preferences is the explicit aim of REBT. Exam trap: in Ellis's ABC, B is beliefs and C is consequences; do not confuse it with the behavioural ABC (antecedent, behaviour, consequence) of functional analysis.", "topic": "REBT goal: modify irrational beliefs"}, {"q": "Which therapy is most effective in treating Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder (OCD)?", "options": {"a": "Psychoanalysis", "b": "Exposure and Response Prevention (ERP)", "c": "Gestalt Therapy", "d": "Humanistic Therapy"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: exposure and response prevention (ERP) is the most effective psychological treatment for obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD). OCD is driven by a vicious cycle: an obsession (an intrusive, unwanted thought, image or urge, such as 'I may have left the gas on') raises anxiety, and a compulsion (checking, washing, counting, seeking reassurance) brings quick relief. Because the relief negatively reinforces the ritual, the person never discovers that the feared disaster would not happen and that anxiety falls by itself. ERP, introduced by the British psychologist Victor Meyer in 1966 and developed further by Edna Foa and colleagues, breaks the cycle in two ways. Exposure means deliberately and repeatedly facing the feared cue, in real life or in imagination, working up a graded hierarchy. Response prevention means not carrying out the ritual, including hidden mental rituals and reassurance seeking. Anxiety rises, peaks and then falls through habituation and extinction, and the person learns that the feared outcome does not follow. Unlike Wolpe's systematic desensitisation, ERP needs no relaxation training. Example: a man who checks the stove 20 times before leaving home switches it off once, walks out and does not return, staying with the anxiety until it fades; within weeks his checking drops sharply. Option b is right because ERP, delivered on its own or as the core of CBT, is the first-line psychological treatment for OCD in guidelines such as NICE's, often combined with an SSRI. Exam trap: this is the paper's repeat of Q28; whether the option says 'ERP' or 'CBT with ERP', choose it over insight-based or humanistic therapies, which lack controlled evidence for OCD.", "topic": "ERP for obsessive-compulsive disorder"}, {"q": "Cognitive distortions are a key focus in which therapeutic approach?", "options": {"a": "Cognitive Behavioral Therapy (CBT)", "b": "Existential Therapy", "c": "Psychodynamic Therapy", "d": "Client-Centered Therapy"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: cognitive distortions are a key focus of cognitive behavioural therapy (CBT). The concept comes from the American psychiatrist Aaron T. Beck (1921 to 2021), who in the 1960s noticed that his depressed patients had quick, believable, negative automatic thoughts that came before their feelings. Beck described the cognitive triad (negative views of the self, the world and the future) and the systematic errors in thinking that keep it going: arbitrary inference, selective abstraction, overgeneralisation, magnification and minimisation, personalisation and dichotomous (all-or-nothing) thinking. David Burns added further names such as mind reading, fortune telling, emotional reasoning and should statements in Feeling Good (1980). In CBT the therapist and client treat these thoughts as hypotheses: they identify the distortion, examine the evidence through Socratic questioning and thought records, and test predictions with behavioural experiments, so that emotion and behaviour change as thinking becomes more balanced. Albert Ellis's REBT shares this focus on faulty beliefs. Example: a man whose friend does not return a call concludes, 'He is angry with me' (arbitrary inference). In CBT he lists other explanations (the friend is busy, or his phone is off), then texts the friend and learns he was travelling. Option a is right because identifying and correcting cognitive distortions is the defining work of cognitive and cognitive behavioural therapy. Exam trap: client-centred and existential therapies also care about the client's view of the world, but neither labels or challenges thinking errors; that is specific to CBT.", "topic": "Cognitive distortions and CBT"}, {"q": "In family therapy, the term \"triangulation\" refers to:", "options": {"a": "Strengthening communication between two family members", "b": "A third party being drawn into a conflict", "c": "Establishing clear boundaries", "d": "Reducing stress through role-playing"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: in family therapy, triangulation means a third party, often a child, being drawn into a conflict between two others. The term comes mainly from the American psychiatrist Murray Bowen (1913 to 1990), founder of the transgenerational family systems school, whose master concept is differentiation of self. Bowen saw the triangle as the smallest stable relationship system: when tension rises between two people, such as a husband and wife, one or both pull in a third person to lower their own anxiety, for example by confiding in a child, uniting against a 'problem child', or using the child as a messenger. The two-person conflict is then detoured rather than resolved, and the child may become symptomatic. Salvador Minuchin's structural family therapy (Families and Family Therapy, 1974) listed triangulation among the rigid triads, along with detouring and stable coalitions, which cross the boundary between the parental and child subsystems. Treatment aims to detriangulate: Bowen worked as a calm coach who stayed out of the family's triangles and helped members deal with each other directly, while Minuchin strengthened the parental subsystem. Example: parents who no longer speak to each other each complain to their teenage daughter about the other, and she develops headaches and stops going to school. Option b is right because triangulation is defined by a third person being pulled into a two-person conflict. Exam trap: triangulation in qualitative research (Norman Denzin's use of several data sources, methods, investigators or theories to cross-check findings) is a completely different concept with the same name, and this paper asks about it separately.", "topic": "Triangulation in family therapy"}, {"q": "Which of the following therapies is best suited for treating Borderline Personality Disorder (BPD)?", "options": {"a": "Cognitive Therapy", "b": "Dialectical Behavior Therapy (DBT)", "c": "Psychoanalysis", "d": "Behavioral Activation"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: Dialectical Behaviour Therapy (DBT) is the therapy best suited to borderline personality disorder (BPD). Marsha M. Linehan developed DBT, with treatment manuals in 1993, for chronically suicidal women, most of whom met criteria for BPD, a disorder marked by unstable relationships, identity disturbance, impulsivity, intense anger, chronic emptiness and recurrent self-harm. Linehan's biosocial theory holds that BPD arises when a child with a biological emotional vulnerability (high sensitivity, strong reactions and a slow return to baseline) grows up in an invalidating environment that dismisses or punishes emotions. DBT therefore balances acceptance (validation and mindfulness, drawn from Zen) with change (behaviour therapy). It runs in four modes: individual therapy, a weekly skills group, telephone coaching for crises and a consultation team for therapists. Skills cover mindfulness, distress tolerance, emotion regulation and interpersonal effectiveness, and individual sessions target life-threatening behaviour first. In Linehan's 1991 trial, women given a year of DBT made fewer and less medically serious suicidal acts and spent fewer days in hospital than those given usual treatment. Example: a client who cuts herself after arguments learns to rate the urge, use distress tolerance skills and phone her therapist for coaching before acting. Option b is right because DBT was designed for BPD and has the earliest and strongest controlled evidence in it. Exam trap: other therapies with evidence for BPD exist (Anthony Bateman and Peter Fonagy's mentalisation-based treatment, Jeffrey Young's schema therapy, Otto Kernberg's transference-focused psychotherapy), but none is offered here, and plain cognitive therapy or classical psychoanalysis is not the specialist choice.", "topic": "DBT for borderline personality disorder"}, {"q": "Which therapeutic approach was developed by Aaron Beck?", "options": {"a": "Gestalt Therapy", "b": "Cognitive Therapy", "c": "Existential Therapy", "d": "Narrative Therapy"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: Aaron Beck developed cognitive therapy. Aaron Temkin Beck (1921 to 2021) was a psychoanalytically trained American psychiatrist at the University of Pennsylvania. In the late 1950s and 1960s he set out to test Freud's idea that depression is anger turned inward, but found that his depressed patients' dreams and thoughts were full of loss and defeat instead. He also noticed a stream of quick, believable, negative automatic thoughts that came before their feelings. He set out the model in Depression (1967) and the treatment in Cognitive Therapy of Depression (1979, with colleagues). The model has three levels: core beliefs or schemas formed early in life, intermediate rules and assumptions, and automatic thoughts. Depression shows the cognitive triad (negative views of the self, the world and the future), kept going by cognitive distortions such as arbitrary inference and overgeneralisation. Therapy is brief, structured and collaborative: through collaborative empiricism, Socratic questioning, thought records and behavioural experiments, the client learns to test and correct distorted thinking. Beck also built widely used scales such as the Beck Depression Inventory (1961), and his daughter Judith S. Beck continues the work. Example: a depressed student thinks, 'I will never make friends'; therapist and client treat this as a hypothesis, list counter-evidence, plan to join one club and then review what happened. Option b is right because Beck is the founder of cognitive therapy. Exam trap: Albert Ellis's REBT (1955) came first and shares the aim of changing thinking, so students mix the two; the cognitive triad and the thought record are Beck's, while the ABC model and disputing are Ellis's.", "topic": "Founder of cognitive therapy: Beck"}, {"q": "The \"empty chair\" technique is commonly used in:", "options": {"a": "Rational Emotive Behavior Therapy", "b": "Gestalt Therapy", "c": "Psychoanalysis", "d": "Behavioral Therapy"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: the empty chair technique is commonly used in Gestalt therapy. Gestalt therapy was developed by Fritz Perls (1893 to 1970) with Laura Perls and Paul Goodman (Gestalt Therapy, 1951). It is an experiential, here-and-now therapy that aims to increase awareness and help clients finish unfinished business, feelings from past relationships that were never expressed and still shape present life. In the empty chair technique, the client imagines a person (often a parent, a former partner or someone who has died) or a disowned part of the self sitting in an empty chair and speaks to it directly, in the present tense. The client may then switch chairs and answer as the other person or part. This turns talk about feelings into direct experience of them, so that anger, grief or longing can be expressed and integrated. A related form is the two-chair dialogue between the 'topdog' (the critical 'you should' voice) and the 'underdog' (the apologetic voice that wins by stalling). Example: a man who never told his late father how hurt he felt by his criticism speaks to the empty chair, then moves into it and answers as his father; he ends the session tearful but relieved. Option b is right because the empty chair is the best-known signature technique of Gestalt therapy. Exam trap: emotion-focused therapy (Leslie Greenberg) and psychodrama (Jacob Moreno) also use chair work, but in exam options the empty chair points to Gestalt therapy, never to REBT, psychoanalysis or behaviour therapy.", "topic": "Empty chair technique: Gestalt therapy"}, {"q": "A key characteristic of Client-Centered Therapy is:", "options": {"a": "The therapist providing unconditional positive regard", "b": "The focus on past unconscious conflicts", "c": "Direct interpretation of dreams", "d": "Challenging cognitive distortions"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: a key characteristic of client-centred therapy is the therapist providing unconditional positive regard. Client-centred (now person-centred) therapy was developed by Carl Rogers (1902 to 1987) in the 1940s and 1950s, with Client-Centered Therapy published in 1951. Rogers believed that people have an actualising tendency, an inborn drive toward growth, and that distress comes from incongruence: a gap between the self-concept and actual experience, created when love was given only under conditions of worth. The therapist does not diagnose, interpret or advise. Instead, as Rogers argued in his 1957 paper on the necessary and sufficient conditions of therapeutic change, the therapist offers three core conditions: congruence or genuineness, unconditional positive regard (warm, non-judgemental acceptance of the client as a person) and accurate empathic understanding, communicated through reflection of feelings. In that climate the client stops defending the self, owns denied feelings and moves toward becoming a 'fully functioning person'. Example: a student confesses that she has failed her exams and lied to her parents about it. The therapist conveys acceptance of her as a person and reflects, 'You feel ashamed and trapped between telling the truth and keeping their approval', and she begins to explore what she really wants. Option a is right because unconditional positive regard is one of Rogers's core conditions and defines the client-centred relationship. Exam trap: the other three options each name a different school (psychoanalysis, dream interpretation, CBT); in Rogers's approach the client, not the therapist, leads, and the therapist never interprets or disputes.", "topic": "Client-centred therapy: core conditions"}, {"q": "Which therapy focuses on helping clients find meaning in life despite suffering?", "options": {"a": "Existential Therapy", "b": "Behavioral Therapy", "c": "Psychoanalysis", "d": "Cognitive Therapy"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: existential therapy focuses on helping clients find meaning in life despite suffering. Existential therapy is less a set of techniques than a philosophical view of human life, drawing on Søren Kierkegaard, Martin Heidegger (Being and Time, 1927) and Jean-Paul Sartre. It sees people as free and responsible for creating their own meaning. Irvin Yalom, in Existential Psychotherapy (1980), named four ultimate concerns: death, freedom, isolation and meaninglessness; anxiety about them is normal, and problems arise when people flee it into inauthentic living. Rollo May brought these ideas into American psychology. The form most tied to meaning is logotherapy, founded by the Viennese psychiatrist Viktor Frankl (1905 to 1997), who survived the Nazi concentration camps and described the experience in Man's Search for Meaning. Frankl held that the will to meaning is the primary human motive; when it is frustrated, the result is an existential vacuum or a noogenic neurosis. Meaning can be found by creating a work, by experiencing love or beauty, and through the attitude taken toward unavoidable suffering. Example: Frankl described an elderly doctor who could not get over his wife's death. Frankl asked what would have happened if he had died first; the man said she would have suffered terribly, and Frankl pointed out that by surviving her he had spared her that pain. The suffering gained a meaning and became bearable. Option a is right because the search for meaning in the face of suffering is the defining theme of existential therapy and logotherapy. Exam trap: humanistic therapies (Rogers) also value growth, but 'meaning despite suffering' is the specific signature of Frankl and the existential school.", "topic": "Existential therapy and meaning"}, {"q": "In Behavior Therapy, reinforcement is used to:", "options": {"a": "Encourage desired behaviors", "b": "Identify unconscious motives", "c": "Modify cognitive distortions", "d": "Develop insight into past trauma"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: in behaviour therapy, reinforcement is used to encourage desired behaviours. Behaviour therapy treats problems as learned habits that can be relearned, and reinforcement is its main tool for building behaviour. The idea comes from Edward Thorndike's law of effect (responses followed by satisfying consequences are 'stamped in') and from B. F. Skinner's operant conditioning, set out in The Behavior of Organisms (1938). A reinforcer is any consequence that makes the behaviour it follows more likely. Positive reinforcement adds something pleasant (praise, a token, a privilege); negative reinforcement removes something unpleasant (a nagging parent stops once homework starts). Both increase behaviour; punishment, by contrast, decreases it. In therapy the reinforcer is given immediately after the target behaviour, every time while the behaviour is new, then intermittently so that it lasts. Techniques built on it include shaping (reinforcing successive approximations), the Premack principle and the token economy of Teodoro Ayllon and Nathan Azrin (1968). Example: a child with autism earns a sticker each time he makes eye contact, and later only on some occasions, until eye contact becomes part of his everyday behaviour. Option a is right because reinforcement exists to increase the frequency of a chosen, desirable behaviour. Exam trap: negative reinforcement is not punishment; it also strengthens behaviour, by taking away something aversive, and the other three options describe psychodynamic and cognitive goals, not behavioural ones.", "topic": "Reinforcement in behaviour therapy"}, {"q": "Which therapy technique involves gradual exposure to a feared stimulus while practicing relaxation?", "options": {"a": "Systematic Desensitization", "b": "Free Association", "c": "Thought Stopping", "d": "Psychoeducation"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: systematic desensitisation is the technique of gradual exposure to a feared stimulus while practising relaxation. It was developed by the South African psychiatrist Joseph Wolpe and set out in Psychotherapy by Reciprocal Inhibition (1958). Its forerunner was Mary Cover Jones's 1924 treatment of Peter, a small boy afraid of rabbits, who was fed while a rabbit was brought a little closer each day. Wolpe's principle, reciprocal inhibition, holds that two incompatible responses cannot occur together, so a deeply relaxed body cannot also be anxious. Treatment has three steps. First, the client learns Edmund Jacobson's progressive muscle relaxation. Second, client and therapist build an anxiety hierarchy of 10 to 20 situations, each rated on Wolpe's subjective units of distress scale (SUDS, 0 to 100). Third, while relaxed, the client imagines (or meets) the lowest item and moves up only when it no longer causes anxiety. Example: a woman afraid of dogs begins by picturing a photograph of a puppy and, many sessions later, pats a calm dog on a lead without distress. Option a is right because the stem names both defining ingredients, graded exposure and relaxation, which together make systematic desensitisation. Exam trap: flooding also uses exposure, but it starts at the top of the hierarchy with no relaxation and no gradual steps; 'gradual plus relaxation' always means systematic desensitisation.", "topic": "Systematic desensitisation"}, {"q": "Motivational Interviewing is most commonly used to treat:", "options": {"a": "Substance Use Disorders", "b": "Schizophrenia", "c": "Autism Spectrum Disorder", "d": "ADHD"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: motivational interviewing (MI) is most commonly used to treat substance use disorders. William R. Miller first described it in 1983 in work with problem drinkers and developed it with Stephen Rollnick in Motivational Interviewing (1991; later editions 2002 and 2013). MI is a collaborative, person-centred but goal-directed conversation that strengthens a person's own motivation to change by resolving ambivalence, the state of wanting and not wanting change at the same time. Arguing for change only makes the client defend the status quo, so the counsellor instead uses the OARS skills (open questions, affirmations, reflections and summaries), expresses empathy, develops discrepancy between behaviour and the client's own goals, rolls with resistance and supports self-efficacy, drawing out the client's own 'change talk'. It is often paired with Prochaska and DiClemente's stages of change, and is especially useful for people in precontemplation and contemplation. Example: a man who drinks heavily says he enjoys alcohol but hates missing his daughter's school events; the counsellor reflects both sides and asks what he would like to be different, letting him voice his own reasons to cut down. Option a is right because addiction is MI's original and best-supported field, where ambivalence about change is the central clinical problem. Exam trap: MI was created by Miller and Rollnick, not by Prochaska and DiClemente, who built the transtheoretical (stages of change) model that MI is often combined with.", "topic": "Motivational interviewing: main use"}, {"q": "Which of the following is a key component of Acceptance and Commitment Therapy (ACT)?", "options": {"a": "Cognitive Defusion", "b": "Interpretation of dreams", "c": "Hypnosis", "d": "Free Association"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: cognitive defusion is a key component of acceptance and commitment therapy (ACT). ACT (said as the word 'act') was developed by Steven C. Hayes with Kirk Strosahl and Kelly Wilson; their book appeared in 1999. It belongs to the 'third wave' of behaviour therapy and rests on relational frame theory, Hayes's account of how language lets a thought carry the sting of what it stands for. Trying to escape unwanted thoughts and feelings (experiential avoidance) backfires and narrows life, so ACT aims at psychological flexibility rather than symptom removal. Flexibility is built through six processes, the hexaflex: acceptance, cognitive defusion, contact with the present moment, self-as-context, values and committed action. Cognitive defusion means stepping back from thoughts and seeing them as words and pictures passing through the mind, not literal truths or orders. Its opposite is cognitive fusion, being caught up in a thought as if it were reality. Example: instead of 'I am a failure', the client is taught to say 'I am having the thought that I am a failure', or to repeat the word 'failure' aloud until it loses its grip. Option a is right because defusion is one of the six named core processes of ACT. Exam trap: defusion does not dispute the content of a thought, as Beck's cognitive restructuring does; it changes the person's relationship to the thought while leaving the thought itself alone.", "topic": "ACT: cognitive defusion"}, {"q": "In Structural Family Therapy, the therapist aims to:", "options": {"a": "Reorganize family hierarchies and boundaries", "b": "Explore early childhood experiences", "c": "Focus solely on individual behavior", "d": "Encourage dream analysis"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: in structural family therapy the therapist aims to reorganise family hierarchies and boundaries. The approach was developed by Salvador Minuchin, who worked first with poor families of delinquent boys (Families of the Slums, 1967) and then at the Philadelphia Child Guidance Clinic (Families and Family Therapy, 1974). Minuchin looked at the family's present structure: its subsystems (spousal, parental, sibling), a hierarchy with parents in charge, and the boundaries between members. Healthy boundaries are clear, firm yet permeable. Diffuse boundaries produce enmeshment (over-involvement, intrusion, little autonomy); rigid boundaries produce disengagement (distance and little support). Symptoms are seen as signs of a faulty structure, so changing the structure relieves them. The therapist first joins the family by accommodating to its style, then draws a structural map and asks for an enactment, the conflict acted out in the session rather than described. Unbalancing, boundary making and reframing then restructure the system. Example: a mother and teenage son are allied against a father who has no say; the therapist sides with the father during an enactment and asks both parents to agree a curfew together, restoring the parental subsystem. Option a is right because restructuring hierarchy and boundaries is the defining goal of Minuchin's method. Exam trap: structural is Minuchin, strategic is Jay Haley, multigenerational (family systems) is Murray Bowen and experiential is Virginia Satir; enmeshment means diffuse boundaries, while rigid boundaries mean disengagement.", "topic": "Structural family therapy aims"}, {"q": "Which therapy primarily focuses on increasing awareness of the present moment?", "options": {"a": "Mindfulness-Based Cognitive Therapy (MBCT)", "b": "Psychoanalysis", "c": "Existential Therapy", "d": "Solution-Focused Therapy"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: mindfulness-based cognitive therapy (MBCT) primarily focuses on increasing awareness of the present moment. Mindfulness, which Jon Kabat-Zinn defined as paying attention on purpose, in the present moment and non-judgementally, entered medicine through his mindfulness-based stress reduction (MBSR), founded in 1979 at the University of Massachusetts Medical School. Zindel Segal, Mark Williams and John Teasdale combined MBSR with Beck's cognitive therapy to create MBCT (their book appeared in 2002), an eight-week group programme for people who have recovered from recurrent depression. Through the body scan, sitting meditation and the three-minute breathing space, participants learn decentring: noticing thoughts as passing mental events rather than facts, so that a dip in mood does not spiral into rumination and relapse. In the trial of Teasdale and colleagues (2000), relapse among patients with three or more past episodes fell from 66 to 37 percent. Example: a woman who once slid into depression after every criticism learns to notice 'here is the thought that I am useless', feel her breath and return to the task, instead of brooding for days. Option a is right because present-moment, non-judgemental awareness is the core skill MBCT trains. Exam trap: MBCT is a relapse-prevention treatment for people who are currently well, not a treatment for acute depression; do not confuse it with MBSR (Kabat-Zinn, for stress and pain) or with ACT, which includes present-moment contact as only one of six processes.", "topic": "MBCT and present-moment awareness"}, {"q": "Type of thinking in which a person starts from one and comes up with many different ideas is an example of", "options": {"a": "Divergent thinking", "b": "Convergent thinking", "c": "Critical analysis", "d": "Decision"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: thinking that starts from one point and produces many different ideas is divergent thinking. The distinction comes from J. P. (Joy Paul) Guilford, whose 1950 American Psychological Association presidential address launched the modern study of creativity. In his structure of intellect model (The Nature of Human Intelligence, 1967), divergent production and convergent production are separate mental operations. Divergent thinking fans out from one starting point to many possible answers; convergent thinking narrows many pieces of information down to the single correct answer. Guilford scored divergent production on four dimensions: fluency (number of ideas), flexibility (number of different categories), originality (statistical rarity) and elaboration (detail). Tasks such as alternative uses and the Torrance Tests of Creative Thinking measure it. Example: asked 'How many uses can you think of for a brick?', one person lists a wall, a doorstop, a paperweight, a weapon and red powder for paint; that spread of ideas from one stimulus is divergent thinking. Solving 'seven times eight' needs convergent thinking because there is only one right answer. Option a is right because the stem describes exactly the one-to-many movement that defines divergent thinking. Exam trap: divergent thinking is closely tied to creativity but is not the same as intelligence; ordinary IQ tests mostly reward convergent thinking, which is why a highly intelligent person may still score modestly on divergent tasks.", "topic": "Divergent versus convergent thinking"}, {"q": "Behavioral Activation is primarily used to treat:", "options": {"a": "Depression", "b": "Schizophrenia", "c": "PTSD", "d": "Phobias"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: behavioural activation (BA) is primarily used to treat depression. Its roots lie in Peter Lewinsohn's 1970s theory that depression follows a loss of response-contingent positive reinforcement: when fewer of a person's actions are rewarded, he withdraws, which removes even more reward, and the spiral deepens. In 1996 Neil Jacobson and colleagues found that the activation component of Beck's cognitive therapy worked as well as the full package, and Jacobson, Christopher Martell and Michael Addis then developed BA as a treatment in its own right. In the trial of Sona Dimidjian and colleagues (2006), BA matched antidepressant medication for more severely depressed patients. BA works from the outside in: rather than waiting to feel motivated, the client acts first, and mood follows. Steps include activity monitoring, rating activities for mastery and pleasure, scheduling valued and enjoyable activities, breaking tasks into small steps and tackling avoidance and rumination. Example: a student who has stopped leaving her room records her day, notices her mood lifts slightly after a walk, and schedules a short walk and a call to a friend each day, gradually rebuilding the rewarding routine depression took away. Option a is right because BA was built from a reinforcement model of depression and has its strongest evidence there. Exam trap: BA means engaging in meaningful, rewarding activity; it is not relaxation, hypnosis or analysis of childhood, and it is not exposure therapy, the core method for anxiety disorders.", "topic": "Behavioural activation for depression"}, {"q": "The core principle of Exposure Therapy is:", "options": {"a": "Repeated confrontation with feared stimuli", "b": "Challenging irrational thoughts", "c": "Exploring early childhood trauma", "d": "Encouraging positive affirmations"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: the core principle of exposure therapy is repeated confrontation with feared stimuli. Exposure therapy is repeated or prolonged contact with a feared object, situation, memory or bodily sensation, without escape or avoidance, until the fear subsides. Its roots lie in learning theory: Mary Cover Jones's 1924 work with Peter and Joseph Wolpe's systematic desensitisation (1958) showed that fear can be unlearned. Two processes explain it. Habituation is the fall in anxiety with repeated or prolonged contact with a stimulus; extinction occurs as the stimulus keeps appearing without the feared outcome, so the conditioned link weakens. Michelle Craske's inhibitory learning view adds that the client learns a new, competing memory ('the dog did not bite'). Avoidance, by contrast, keeps fear alive because the relief it brings negatively reinforces it. Exposure can be graded or intense (flooding), in vivo or imaginal, interoceptive (spinning or overbreathing for panic) or in virtual reality. In obsessive-compulsive disorder it is combined with response prevention (ERP), described by Victor Meyer in 1966; for PTSD Edna Foa built prolonged exposure. Example: a man afraid of lifts rides one floor repeatedly until his anxiety falls, then two floors, then ten. Option a is right because the confrontation itself, repeated without escape, is the active ingredient. Exam trap: exposure must last long enough for anxiety to fall and must block escape and safety behaviours; leaving at the peak of fear teaches that escape works and makes the fear stronger.", "topic": "Core principle of exposure therapy"}, {"q": "Narrative Therapy helps clients by:", "options": {"a": "Reconstructing personal stories in a meaningful way", "b": "Analyzing unconscious motives", "c": "Engaging in mindfulness exercises", "d": "Using systematic desensitization"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: narrative therapy helps clients by reconstructing their personal stories in a meaningful way. It was created by the Australian social worker Michael White in Adelaide and David Epston in New Zealand, and set out in Narrative Means to Therapeutic Ends (1990). It grew from family therapy and social constructionism: people make sense of their lives through stories, and no single account is the true one. Clients often arrive with a thin, 'problem-saturated' story in which the problem defines who they are. The therapist first externalises the problem ('the person is not the problem; the problem is the problem'), giving it a name such as 'the Anger' or White's famous 'Sneaky Poo', so that person and problem can be seen as separate. Next comes the search for unique outcomes, moments when the problem did not win. Finally the therapist helps the client re-author a richer, preferred story around those exceptions, often supported by therapeutic letters, certificates and an audience of significant others. Example: a teenager who calls herself 'a depressed person' begins to speak of 'the Gloom' that visits her, recalls the week she went to a concert despite it, and builds a story of herself as someone who stands up to the Gloom. Option a is right because re-authoring the client's life story is the defining aim of narrative therapy. Exam trap: externalisation belongs to narrative therapy, exception and miracle questions to solution-focused brief therapy, and prescribing the symptom to strategic family therapy.", "topic": "Narrative therapy: re-authoring stories"}, {"q": "Which of the following best describes delusion?", "options": {"a": "A perceptual disturbance", "b": "A fixed false belief not amenable to reason", "c": "A mood disorder", "d": "An obsession"}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b: a delusion is a fixed false belief not amenable to reason. In descriptive psychopathology a delusion is a false, firmly held belief that is not shaken by argument or contrary evidence and is out of keeping with the person's cultural, religious and educational background. Karl Jaspers, in General Psychopathology (1913), named its three external marks: subjective certainty, incorrigibility (it cannot be corrected by experience or reasoning) and impossibility or falsity of content. The cultural clause matters: a community's shared belief in the evil eye or in spirits is not a delusion, however strange it seems to an outsider. Delusions are disorders of thought content, not of perception. They can be primary (not understandable from any other experience) or secondary (understandable from a mood or hallucination), and they are classified by theme: persecutory (the commonest), reference, grandiose, nihilistic, guilt, jealousy, erotomania and somatic. Together with hallucinations and disorganised speech or behaviour, delusions mark psychosis. Example: a clerk is utterly convinced that his neighbours have planted cameras in his walls to steal his ideas; when shown that no cameras exist, he says they have been cleverly hidden. Option b is right because fixity and imperviousness to reason are the defining features of a delusion. Exam trap: an overvalued idea (Carl Wernicke's term) is also a strongly held belief, but it is understandable and lacks absolute fixity, as in the anorexic patient's conviction that she is fat; an obsession is recognised as one's own and resisted.", "topic": "Definition of delusion"}, {"q": "A primary delusion is:", "options": {"a": "Secondary to hallucination", "b": "Arising suddenly without cause", "c": "Based on mood", "d": "Induced by another"}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b: a primary delusion is one arising suddenly without cause, meaning without any understandable psychological antecedent. The concept comes from Karl Jaspers, who in General Psychopathology (1913) divided delusions into two kinds. A secondary delusion (a delusion-like idea) can be understood as growing out of some other experience: guilt from deeply depressed mood, persecution to explain hostile voices, grandiosity from manic elation. A primary or autochthonous ('self-born') delusion cannot be traced back to anything else; it arrives fully formed, with immediate conviction, and empathic understanding cannot get behind it. Jaspers held that this ununderstandability was the mark of a true psychotic process, typically schizophrenia. Primary delusions appear as delusional mood (a sense that something sinister is going on), delusional perception, delusional memory and the sudden delusional idea. Delusional perception, in which a normal perception suddenly carries a private, delusional meaning, is one of Kurt Schneider's first-rank symptoms. Example: a young man sees a red traffic light and instantly 'knows' that he has been chosen to save the world; nothing in his mood or other experiences explains the link. Option b is right because sudden onset without an understandable cause in other experience is exactly what makes a delusion primary. Exam trap: options a and c are the textbook examples of secondary delusions, and a delusion based on mood or hallucination is never primary, however bizarre it is. Key note: 'without cause' is loose wording; strictly, a primary delusion has no psychological antecedent that explains it, though it may still have a biological cause, and a phase of delusional mood can precede it.", "topic": "Primary (autochthonous) delusion"}, {"q": "Which term describes a false sensory perception without external stimulus?", "options": {"a": "Delusion", "b": "Hallucination", "c": "Illusion", "d": "Obsession"}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b: a hallucination is a false sensory perception without an external stimulus. The French psychiatrist Jean-Étienne Esquirol gave the term its modern meaning in the early nineteenth century, separating it from the illusion, which needs a real object. In descriptive psychopathology a true hallucination has three features: it arises with no external stimulus, it is as vivid and real as a normal perception, and it is located in outer, objective space (the voice comes from the corridor). A pseudohallucination, by contrast, is experienced in inner space ('a voice inside my head') or is recognised as unreal. Hallucinations can occur in any sense. Auditory ones are the commonest in schizophrenia, and third-person voices, running commentary and thought echo are Schneider's first-rank symptoms; visual hallucinations suggest an organic cause such as delirium; tactile formication suggests cocaine use or alcohol withdrawal; olfactory ones suggest temporal lobe epilepsy. Hypnagogic (falling asleep) and hypnopompic (waking) hallucinations occur in healthy people. Example: alone in a silent room, a woman clearly hears a man's voice outside her window calling her a thief, though no one is there. Option b is right because perception in the absence of any stimulus is the definition of a hallucination. Exam trap: the distinction is the stimulus. An illusion is a misperception of something real (a coat on a hook seen as an intruder); a hallucination needs no stimulus at all; a delusion is a belief, not a perception.", "topic": "Hallucination versus illusion and delusion"}, {"q": "An individual believing the TV is sending them messages is experiencing:", "options": {"a": "Referential delusion", "b": "Nihilistic delusion", "c": "Persecutory delusion", "d": "Somatic delusion"}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: believing that the television is sending one messages is a referential delusion, or delusion of reference. In a delusion of reference, neutral events, objects or other people's words are believed to carry a special, personal meaning for the patient: a newsreader is speaking directly to him, a song on the radio was chosen for him, strangers' laughter is about him, a newspaper headline is a coded instruction. The belief has the usual marks of a delusion described by Karl Jaspers: certainty, incorrigibility and false content. It should be separated from an idea of reference, in which the person has the same self-referring thought but without full conviction ('I wonder whether they were laughing at me'), which is common in social anxiety and in schizotypal or paranoid personality. Referential delusions are frequent in schizophrenia and often travel with persecutory ones, since the broadcasts may be taken as warnings or threats. Example: a young woman says the weather forecaster's gestures each evening are instructions telling her which streets she may walk on, and no argument dislodges the idea. Option a is right because the core of the experience is that an ordinary, impersonal event is taken as a message aimed at her. Exam trap: delusions of reference are not one of Schneider's first-rank symptoms, whereas delusional perception is; and an idea of reference lacks the fixity of a true delusion.", "topic": "Delusion of reference"}, {"q": "Changes in consciousness are likely seen in:", "options": {"a": "Schizophrenia", "b": "Depression", "c": "Dementia", "d": "Delirium"}, "correct": "d", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is d: changes in consciousness are characteristically seen in delirium. Delirium is an acute brain failure. DSM-5-TR defines it as a disturbance of attention (difficulty directing, focusing, sustaining or shifting it) and awareness (reduced orientation to the environment) that develops over a short period, usually hours to a few days, fluctuates in severity during the day, and brings an additional cognitive change such as disorientation, memory loss or perceptual disturbance, with evidence of a physical cause. Older texts called this clouding of consciousness, the mildest lowering of awareness. Causes include infection, metabolic or electrolyte upset, hypoxia, drugs, intoxication or withdrawal (delirium tremens) and surgery. Typical features are visual hallucinations and illusions, a reversed sleep-wake cycle, worsening in the evening (sundowning) and diffuse slowing on the EEG. Subtypes are hyperactive, hypoactive (quiet and easily missed) and mixed. Example: an 80-year-old woman two days after hip surgery is lucid in the morning but at night is drowsy, cannot follow a conversation, thinks she is at home and picks at the bedclothes; a urinary infection is found, and she clears when it is treated. Option d is right because disturbed consciousness and attention, with acute onset and fluctuating course, is the defining feature of delirium. Exam trap: in schizophrenia, depression and early dementia consciousness is clear; the most reliable clue separating delirium from dementia is disturbed attention or consciousness with acute onset, not memory loss or hallucinations, which occur in both.", "topic": "Disturbed consciousness in delirium"}, {"q": "Which symptom is considered a disorder of possession of thought?", "options": {"a": "Thought insertion", "b": "Circumstantiality", "c": "Flight of ideas", "d": "Tangentiality"}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: thought insertion is a disorder of the possession of thought. British descriptive psychopathology, especially Frank Fish's Clinical Psychopathology, which many Indian syllabuses follow, describes thought under four headings: stream (the speed and flow of thinking), form (how ideas are linked), content (what is thought, such as delusions) and possession (the sense that one's thoughts are one's own). Normally we take for granted that our thoughts belong to us and are under our control. In disorders of possession this ownership breaks down. Thought alienation has three classic forms: thought insertion (thoughts are put into the mind by an outside agency), thought withdrawal (thoughts are taken out) and thought broadcasting (thoughts pass out of the mind so that others know them). All three are first-rank symptoms of Kurt Schneider and strongly suggest schizophrenia in the absence of organic disease. Many Indian texts also place obsessions under possession, since the thought is known to be one's own yet cannot be controlled. Example: a patient says, 'These thoughts about killing are not mine; my neighbour beams them into my head with a machine.' The thoughts are experienced as foreign, not merely unwanted. Option a is right because thought insertion is a loss of ownership of thought, the defining feature of a possession disorder. Exam trap: circumstantiality, flight of ideas and tangentiality concern how thoughts flow and connect, so they are disorders of the stream or form of thought; and an obsession, unlike an inserted thought, is recognised as coming from one's own mind.", "topic": "Thought insertion: possession of thought"}, {"q": "First rank symptoms are characteristic of which condition?", "options": {"a": "Depression", "b": "Schizophrenia", "c": "OCD", "d": "Anxiety"}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is b: first rank symptoms are characteristic of schizophrenia. They were listed by the German psychiatrist Kurt Schneider (1887 to 1967), whose Clinical Psychopathology appeared in English in 1959. Eugen Bleuler's fundamental symptoms (the four As) were hard to rate reliably, so Schneider picked out experiences that, in the absence of organic brain disease, strongly suggest schizophrenia. Their shared theme is a breakdown of the boundary between self and world. The usual count is eleven: three kinds of auditory hallucination (thought echo, voices arguing or discussing the patient in the third person, and a running commentary on his actions); thought alienation (thought insertion, withdrawal and broadcasting); passivity experiences (made feelings, made impulses, made volitional acts and somatic passivity); and delusional perception. His second-rank symptoms included other hallucinations, perplexity, sudden delusional ideas, mood changes and emotional blunting. Example: a 22-year-old student says that a voice keeps describing everything he does ('now he is opening the door') and that his arm is moved by a force from outside; both are first-rank symptoms. Option b is right because Schneider defined these symptoms specifically as the markers of schizophrenia. Exam trap: first rank symptoms are characteristic but not specific; they also occur in mania and other psychoses, so DSM-5 dropped their special weight; and delusions of reference, depersonalisation, negative symptoms and Bleuler's loosening of associations are not first rank.", "topic": "Schneider's first rank symptoms"}, {"q": "Which of the following is used to compare means of two or more groups?", "options": {"a": "ANOVA", "b": "t-test", "c": "Multiple Regression", "d": "Chi Square"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: analysis of variance (ANOVA) is used to compare the means of two or more groups. ANOVA was developed by Ronald A. Fisher in the 1920s, notably in Statistical Methods for Research Workers (1925); George Snedecor later named its statistic F in Fisher's honour. It compares means by analysing variances. The total sum of squares is split into variation between the group means and variation within the groups (error). The F ratio is the between-groups mean square divided by the within-groups mean square: if the groups differ only by chance, F is close to 1; a large F means the group means differ more than chance allows. A significant F says that at least one mean differs, and post hoc tests such as Tukey's then locate the difference. ANOVA assumes a continuous dependent variable, independent observations, roughly normal distributions and equal variances. It is used for three or more means because running many t-tests would inflate the familywise Type I error rate, but it also works for two groups, where F = t squared and both tests give the same p-value. Example: a researcher compares anxiety scores after CBT, medication and a waiting list; a one-way ANOVA tests all three means in one step. Option a is right because only ANOVA handles both two and more than two group means. Exam trap: the t-test compares at most two means, so it fails the 'or more' part of the stem; chi-square compares frequencies, not means.", "topic": "ANOVA for comparing group means"}, {"q": "Which of the following is a negative symptom in schizophrenia?", "options": {"a": "Hallucination", "b": "Delusion", "c": "Avolition", "d": "Catatonia"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: avolition is a negative symptom of schizophrenia. Symptoms of schizophrenia are grouped as positive, negative and cognitive. Positive symptoms are the presence of something abnormal, an excess or distortion of normal function: delusions, hallucinations, disorganised speech and grossly disorganised behaviour. Negative symptoms are the absence or reduction of something normal. The usual five are affective flattening (reduced emotional expression), alogia (poverty of speech), avolition (lack of self-initiated, purposeful activity), anhedonia (reduced pleasure) and asociality; ICD-10 lists marked apathy, paucity of speech and blunted or incongruous emotional responses. Timothy Crow (1980) linked negative symptoms to his Type II schizophrenia, with a chronic course, poor response to antipsychotics and enlarged ventricles, and Nancy Andreasen built the Scale for the Assessment of Negative Symptoms (SANS) to rate them. Negative symptoms predict poor social and occupational outcome more strongly than positive ones. Example: a 30-year-old man with schizophrenia, whose voices are controlled by medication, still lies in bed most of the day, does not wash unless reminded and has abandoned all plans to find work; he is not sad, he simply cannot start or sustain goal-directed activity. Option c is right because avolition is a loss of normal drive, the very definition of a negative symptom. Exam trap: positive and negative refer to the presence or absence of a function, not to whether a symptom is pleasant or harmful; hallucinations, delusions and disorganised behaviour are always positive, and Bleuler's ambivalence is not a negative symptom.", "topic": "Negative symptoms of schizophrenia"}, {"q": "In depressive pseudodementia, the cognitive deficits are typically:", "options": {"a": "Variable and inconsistent", "b": "Progressive and irreversible", "c": "Consistent with cortical dementia", "d": "Accompanied by agnosia"}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: in depressive pseudodementia the cognitive deficits are typically variable and inconsistent. Pseudodementia is cognitive impairment caused by a psychiatric illness, usually depression in an older adult, that looks like dementia; Leslie Kiloh drew attention to it in a 1961 paper. The deficits come from poor concentration, slowed thinking and low motivation rather than loss of brain tissue, so performance varies from task to task and day to day, and effort is poor. In pseudodementia onset is fairly abrupt and can be dated, often after a loss; the patient complains loudly of poor memory and highlights failures; answers are often 'I don't know' rather than near misses; performance on tasks of similar difficulty is inconsistent; depressed mood, past episodes and sleep or appetite change are present; and the deficits improve with antidepressant treatment. In true dementia onset is insidious, the patient minimises or hides deficits and makes near misses, decline is consistent and progressive, and cortical signs such as aphasia, apraxia and agnosia appear. Example: a 70-year-old widower says 'My memory is finished' and answers most test questions with 'I don't know', yet recalls every detail of his pension dispute; after eight weeks of antidepressants his scores return to normal. Option a is right because fluctuating, effort-dependent, inconsistent performance is the hallmark of depressive pseudodementia. Exam trap: in dementia the patient conceals the problem and the family complains; in pseudodementia the patient complains, often more than the family does.", "topic": "Depressive pseudodementia versus dementia"}, {"q": "Which of the following is a hallmark of qualitative research?", "options": {"a": "Randomized control", "b": "Statistical generalization", "c": "Subjective experience", "d": "Large sample size"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: a hallmark of qualitative research is its focus on subjective experience. Qualitative research studies meaning, experience, process and context through words, images and observed action, and analyses them by interpretation rather than measurement. It seeks the insider's view: how people themselves understand and feel about what happens to them. Its roots include Verstehen (interpretive understanding, associated with Wilhelm Dilthey and Max Weber), Edmund Husserl's phenomenology and symbolic interactionism, the name Herbert Blumer gave in 1937 to George Herbert Mead's ideas. Its logic is inductive and bottom-up; samples are small and purposive; settings are natural; and the researcher is the instrument, which calls for reflexivity. Major traditions include phenomenology and interpretative phenomenological analysis (Jonathan Smith), grounded theory (Glaser and Strauss, 1967), ethnography, case study and narrative research. Quality is judged by trustworthiness (Lincoln and Guba, 1985) rather than by statistical validity and reliability. Example: instead of measuring caregiver burden on a scale, a researcher holds in-depth interviews with ten mothers of children with autism and draws out themes such as 'living with judgement' and 'finding a new normal'. Option c is right because capturing participants' own subjective experience and meanings is what qualitative research is designed to do. Exam trap: the other three options are hallmarks of quantitative research; in 'odd one out' questions, whatever implies objective measurement, probability samples, control or statistical generalisation is the non-qualitative feature.", "topic": "Hallmark of qualitative research"}, {"q": "Grounded theory is used to:", "options": {"a": "Prove a hypothesis", "b": "Develop a theory from data", "c": "Measure statistical correlation", "d": "Test causality"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: grounded theory is used to develop a theory from data. It was launched by the sociologists Barney Glaser and Anselm Strauss in The Discovery of Grounded Theory (1967), after their studies of dying patients in hospitals. They objected to research that only tested grand theories handed down from above; instead, theory should be 'grounded' in, and built up from, systematically gathered data. The method is inductive. Its key procedures are constant comparison (each new incident is compared with earlier ones and with emerging categories), theoretical sampling (choosing whom to study next according to what the developing theory needs), memo writing (recording the analyst's ideas as they form) and theoretical saturation (stopping when new data add nothing new to the categories). Strauss and Juliet Corbin (1990) set out three coding stages: open coding breaks the data apart and labels concepts; axial coding relates the categories to one another; selective coding integrates everything around one core category. Kathy Charmaz later developed a constructivist version. Example: a researcher interviews young adults recovering from a first episode of psychosis, codes the transcripts line by line, and builds a model in which 'reclaiming identity' is the core category linking stigma, family support and work. Option b is right because generating theory from the data, rather than testing a prior theory, is the defining purpose of grounded theory. Exam trap: grounded theory begins without a hypothesis; proving hypotheses, measuring correlation and testing causality are deductive, quantitative aims.", "topic": "Purpose of grounded theory"}, {"q": "Which method is not used in quantitative research?", "options": {"a": "t-test", "b": "Thematic analysis", "c": "Chi-square", "d": "Regression"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: thematic analysis is not used in quantitative research; it is a qualitative method. Quantitative research measures variables as numbers and tests hypotheses statistically, while qualitative research analyses words, meanings and experiences by interpretation. Thematic analysis is a method for identifying, analysing and reporting patterns of meaning (themes) across a qualitative data set such as interview transcripts or focus-group discussions. Virginia Braun and Victoria Clarke set out its widely used form in 2006, in six phases: familiarising oneself with the data, generating initial codes, searching for themes, reviewing themes, defining and naming themes, and writing the report. It is flexible because it is not tied to one theory, which is why it is often the first qualitative method students learn. The other three options are all inferential statistics: the t-test compares two means, chi-square compares frequencies of categorical data, and regression predicts an outcome from one or more predictors. Example: after interviewing twelve nurses about burnout, a researcher codes the transcripts and identifies the themes 'emotional exhaustion', 'feeling unseen' and 'peer support as protection'; no numbers or significance tests are involved. Option b is right because thematic analysis works with textual meaning, not numerical data, and so belongs to qualitative research. Exam trap: content analysis can be quantitative (counting how often categories occur), but thematic analysis, grounded theory coding, interpretative phenomenological analysis and discourse analysis are qualitative methods.", "topic": "Thematic analysis is qualitative"}, {"q": "Triangulation in qualitative research refers to:", "options": {"a": "Using three variables", "b": "Repeating measures", "c": "Using multiple methods to ensure credibility", "d": "Combining three data sets"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: triangulation in qualitative research refers to using multiple methods (and other multiple perspectives) to ensure credibility. The word is borrowed from surveying and navigation, where a position is fixed by taking bearings from two or more known points. Norman Denzin, in The Research Act (1970; the 1978 edition is the one most often cited), named four types: data triangulation (several sources, times, places or people), investigator triangulation (several researchers or analysts), theory triangulation (several theoretical lenses) and methodological triangulation (several methods, such as interviews, observation and documents, or qualitative with quantitative data). The logic is that each method has its own weaknesses, so if different approaches converge on the same finding, confidence in it grows; where they disagree, the difference itself is informative. Yvonna Lincoln and Egon Guba (1985) listed triangulation, with prolonged engagement, peer debriefing and member checking, as a route to credibility, the qualitative counterpart of internal validity. Example: to study stigma in a psychiatric outpatient clinic, a researcher interviews patients, observes waiting-room interactions and reviews case notes; when all three point to the same pattern, the finding is more credible. Option c is right because cross-checking a finding through several methods or sources is what triangulation means. Exam trap: the 'tri' does not mean three; triangulation is about multiple, converging perspectives, and this research sense is different from family-therapy triangulation, in which a child is drawn into the parents' conflict.", "topic": "Triangulation in qualitative research"}, {"q": "Member checking is used to:", "options": {"a": "Test reliability", "b": "Confirm validity with participants", "c": "Compare two groups", "d": "Analyze variance"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: member checking is used to confirm validity with participants. Also called respondent validation, member checking means taking data, interpretations or draft findings back to the people who provided them and asking whether they recognise them as an accurate account of their experience. Yvonna Lincoln and Egon Guba, in Naturalistic Inquiry (1985), called it the most crucial technique for establishing credibility. Their trustworthiness framework replaces the quantitative criteria with four parallels: credibility (for internal validity), transferability (for external validity), dependability (for reliability) and confirmability (for objectivity). Credibility is built through prolonged engagement, peer debriefing, triangulation and member checking. Member checking can be done by returning interview transcripts, a summary of the themes or the final report, individually or in a group. Participants may confirm, correct or add to the interpretation; disagreement is not simply an error but new data to consider, since people's views change over time. Example: after analysing interviews with women who survived suicide attempts, a researcher meets several participants and shares the emerging theme 'being unheard before the crisis'; they confirm it but suggest that 'unseen' captures it better. Option b is right because the purpose of member checking is to establish the credibility, the qualitative form of validity, of findings by checking them with the participants themselves. Exam trap: member checking concerns validity (credibility), not reliability; the qualitative parallel of reliability is dependability, shown through an audit trail.", "topic": "Member checking and credibility"}, {"q": "Which of the following is a type of ordinal data?", "options": {"a": "Blood pressure reading", "b": "IQ score", "c": "Interview transcript", "d": "Reaction time"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: an IQ score is treated in this paper as ordinal data. Stanley Smith Stevens (1946) sorted measurement into four levels, each adding a property: nominal (labels only, such as blood group), ordinal (rank order, with unknown distances between ranks), interval (equal units but an arbitrary zero, such as Celsius temperature) and ratio (a true zero, such as weight). IQ began with Alfred Binet's mental age (1905) and William Stern's ratio IQ (1912); David Wechsler replaced it with the deviation IQ (1939), which converts a person's rank among same-age peers into a scale with mean 100 and SD 15. The score shows where someone stands among peers, but nobody has shown that the 10 points between 70 and 80 equal the 10 points between 130 and 140 in real ability, and an IQ of 0 does not mean no intelligence. Example: Riya scores 120 and Arjun 110; we can safely say Riya ranks higher, but not that 120 is twice 60. Option b is right because, of the four, only IQ is a psychological score whose numbers rank people without guaranteed equal intervals. Exam trap: blood pressure and reaction time look like scores too, but both have a true zero (no pressure, no time), so they are ratio data. Key note: most statistics and psychometrics texts, including the Final Textbook, class IQ as interval data because deviation IQs are built to have equal units; strict measurement theorists reply that equal intervals in the underlying trait are assumed, not shown, so IQ is really ordinal. The official key takes the strict view, and among these options it is the only defensible ordinal choice.", "topic": "Levels of measurement: IQ as ordinal"}, {"q": "Phenomenology focuses on:", "options": {"a": "Cultural interpretation", "b": "Lived experiences", "c": "Experimental conditions", "d": "Survey responses"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: phenomenology focuses on lived experience. Phenomenology began as a philosophy with Edmund Husserl (1859 to 1938), whose aim was to go 'to the things themselves': describe experience exactly as it appears to the person before theorising about it. To do this the researcher practises bracketing (epoche), setting aside her own assumptions. Husserl's former assistant Martin Heidegger (Being and Time, 1927) argued that we can never step outside our world, so all understanding is interpretation; this gave hermeneutic or interpretive phenomenology. In psychology the best known method today is Jonathan Smith's interpretative phenomenological analysis (IPA, 1996), in which the researcher makes sense of participants making sense of their experience. A phenomenological study usually takes a small, purposive sample of people who have all lived through the phenomenon, uses long open interviews, and moves from significant statements to themes and finally to the essence of the experience. Example: a researcher interviews eight mothers of children with autism about what the day of diagnosis felt like, and distils themes such as shock, relief at having a name, and grief for an imagined future. Option b is right because the defining aim of phenomenology is to capture what an experience is like from the inside, in the participant's own terms. Exam trap: 'cultural interpretation' sounds close because phenomenology is interpretive, but studying a culture or group through long immersion is ethnography; phenomenology studies an experience, not a culture.", "topic": "Phenomenology in qualitative research"}, {"q": "What is a key limitation of qualitative research?", "options": {"a": "Lacks depth", "b": "Cannot explain human experience", "c": "Limited generalizability", "d": "Lacks ethical concerns"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: a key limitation of qualitative research is limited generalisability. Qualitative research studies meaning, experience and process through words and observation, using small, purposively chosen samples and interpretive analysis (grounded theory from Barney Glaser and Anselm Strauss, 1967; phenomenology; ethnography; thematic analysis). Its strength is depth: it shows how and why people experience something. The price is breadth. With eight or twelve participants chosen because they are information-rich, not at random, the findings cannot be statistically projected to a whole population, and because the researcher is the instrument, another researcher might read the data differently. Yvonna Lincoln and Egon Guba (1985) answered this by replacing external validity with transferability: the researcher gives a thick description of setting and participants, and the reader judges whether the findings fit another context. Example: interviews with ten caregivers of people with schizophrenia in one Chandigarh hospital may reveal powerful themes of stigma, but you cannot claim from them that 70 per cent of Indian caregivers feel this way. Option c is right because small, non-random, context-bound samples are exactly what stop qualitative findings being generalised in the statistical sense. Exam trap: options a and b list supposed weaknesses that are really qualitative research's strengths; depth and the explanation of human experience are what it does best, so a question asking for a limitation wants generalisability (or subjectivity and time cost), never lack of depth.", "topic": "Limitations of qualitative research"}, {"q": "Purposive sampling is:", "options": {"a": "Random", "b": "Representative of population", "c": "Based on characteristics of interest", "d": "Unbiased"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: purposive sampling is based on characteristics of interest. Sampling methods fall into two families. In probability sampling (simple random, systematic, stratified, cluster) every member of the population has a known, non-zero chance of selection, which lets the researcher calculate sampling error and generalise. In non-probability sampling (convenience, purposive, quota, snowball) the chance of selection is unknown. Purposive sampling, also called judgemental sampling, is the method in which the researcher deliberately hand-picks cases because they have the features the study needs: they are typical, expert, extreme or simply information-rich. Michael Quinn Patton described many purposeful strategies for qualitative work, such as maximum variation, typical case, extreme or deviant case and criterion sampling. It is the standard sampling method of qualitative research, because a phenomenological or grounded theory study needs people who have actually lived the experience being studied. Example: to study coping after a first psychotic episode, a researcher recruits twelve outpatients who had a first episode within the past year and can talk about it, rather than drawing names at random from the city. Option c is right because the defining feature of purposive sampling is selection by the researcher's judgement of who has the characteristics of interest. Exam trap: purposive sampling is not quota sampling; quota sampling fixes numbers per subgroup to mirror population proportions and then fills them non-randomly, whereas purposive sampling chooses individuals for their relevance, with no attempt at proportions.", "topic": "Purposive (judgemental) sampling"}, {"q": "Coding in qualitative research involves:", "options": {"a": "Assigning numbers to data", "b": "Summarizing statistical findings", "c": "Labeling themes or patterns", "d": "Programming a model"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: coding in qualitative research involves labelling themes or patterns. A code is a short word or phrase the researcher attaches to a segment of an interview, field note or document to capture what it is about. Coding is the first step in turning pages of raw text into findings. In grounded theory, Anselm Strauss and Juliet Corbin (1990) described three stages: open coding breaks the data apart and labels concepts line by line, axial coding links the codes into categories, and selective coding integrates everything around one core category. In thematic analysis, Virginia Braun and Victoria Clarke (2006) set out six phases: familiarising with the data, generating initial codes, searching for themes, reviewing themes, defining and naming themes, and writing the report. Codes can be descriptive (what is said) or interpretive (what it means), and researchers often manage them in software such as NVivo or ATLAS.ti, though the software does not do the thinking. Example: a patient says, 'After the diagnosis my relatives stopped inviting us to weddings'; the researcher codes this 'social exclusion', and when similar codes recur across interviews they are grouped into the theme 'stigma'. Option c is right because qualitative coding is the interpretive labelling of meaningful segments so that patterns and themes can be identified. Exam trap: in quantitative research coding does mean assigning numbers to responses (male = 1, female = 2) for data entry, which is why option a tempts; the stem says qualitative research, where codes are labels of meaning, not numbers.", "topic": "Coding in qualitative analysis"}, {"q": "Who proposed the concept of 'schemas' in cognitive development?", "options": {"a": "Skinner", "b": "Freud", "c": "Piaget", "d": "Maslow"}, "correct": "c", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is c: Jean Piaget proposed the concept of schemas in cognitive development. Piaget (1896 to 1980), a Swiss biologist turned developmental psychologist, studied how children's thinking changes by observing his own three children and by questioning children with his clinical method. For him the unit of knowledge is the scheme (usually written schema in English): an organised pattern of action or thought for dealing with a class of experiences. An infant's schemes are actions such as sucking and grasping; an older child's are mental operations such as adding or classifying. Schemes change through adaptation, which has two processes. Assimilation fits new experience into an existing scheme; accommodation changes the scheme when the experience will not fit. A mismatch creates disequilibrium, and resolving it, equilibration, carries the child through the four stages: sensorimotor, preoperational, concrete operational and formal operational. Example: a two-year-old whose 'doggie' schema means 'four-legged animal' calls a cow 'doggie' (assimilation); when told it is a cow that moos, the child splits the schema into dog and cow (accommodation). Option c is right because schemas, with assimilation and accommodation, are the building blocks of Piaget's theory of cognitive development. Exam trap: the word schema is also used by Frederic Bartlett (Remembering, 1932) for the reconstruction of memory and by Aaron Beck for core beliefs in depression; when the question says cognitive development, the answer is Piaget.", "topic": "Piaget's schemas"}, {"q": "Which of the following best explains the participatory research from more traditional research paradigms?", "options": {"a": "It prioritizes double-blind procedures to eliminate bias from participant expectations.", "b": "It maintains strict researcher objectivity and avoids any collaboration with participants.", "c": "It involves stakeholders as co-researchers throughout the research process, including question formulation and data interpretation.", "d": "It uses random sampling to ensure the generalizability of findings to the broader population."}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: participatory research involves stakeholders as co-researchers throughout the research process, including question formulation and data interpretation. Traditional, positivist research treats participants as subjects: the expert researcher designs the study, collects data from people and interprets it alone, aiming at objectivity and distance. Participatory research reverses this power relationship. Its roots include Kurt Lewin's action research (1946), with its cycles of plan, act, observe and reflect; the Brazilian educator Paulo Freire's Pedagogy of the Oppressed (English edition 1970), which argued that oppressed people should create knowledge about their own lives; and the participatory action research of the Colombian sociologist Orlando Fals-Borda. John Heron's co-operative inquiry goes further, making every participant both co-researcher and co-subject. In health, community-based participatory research partners researchers with patients, families and communities. The goals are relevance, empowerment and change, not knowledge alone. Example: instead of designing a questionnaire on service gaps herself, a psychologist forms a team with parents of children with intellectual disability; together they choose the questions, run focus groups, interpret the findings and present them to the district health office to change services. Option c is right because shared ownership of every stage, including framing the question and interpreting the data, is what distinguishes participatory research from traditional paradigms. Exam trap: participatory research does not just mean that people 'participate' by giving data; everyone who answers a questionnaire is a participant, but only participatory research makes them partners in deciding what is studied and what the findings mean.", "topic": "Participatory versus traditional research"}, {"q": "Cognitive dissonance theory was developed by:", "options": {"a": "Festinger", "b": "Bandura", "c": "Ellis", "d": "Piaget"}, "correct": "a", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is a: cognitive dissonance theory was developed by Leon Festinger. Festinger (1919 to 1989), an American social psychologist and a student of Kurt Lewin, proposed it in A Theory of Cognitive Dissonance (1957). Dissonance is the uncomfortable tension of holding two inconsistent cognitions, especially when behaviour contradicts an attitude. Because the tension is unpleasant, people try to reduce it: by changing the attitude, changing the behaviour, adding consonant cognitions or trivialising the inconsistency. Dissonance grows with the importance of the cognitions and the ratio of dissonant to consonant ones. The classic test is Festinger and James Carlsmith's (1959) study: students did a boring task, then were paid either $1 or $20 to tell the next participant it was fun. The $1 group later rated the task as more enjoyable, because $1 was too little to justify lying, so they changed their attitude to fit their behaviour; the $20 group had an external justification. Other paradigms include effort justification (Elliot Aronson and Judson Mills, 1959) and post-decision dissonance (Jack Brehm, 1956). Example: a smoker who knows smoking causes cancer tells himself 'my grandfather smoked and lived to ninety'. Option a is right because Festinger named and formulated cognitive dissonance theory. Exam trap: Daryl Bem's self-perception theory (1967) explains the same $1 and $20 result without any tension, so if a question asks for the rival explanation of dissonance findings, the answer is Bem, not Festinger.", "topic": "Festinger's cognitive dissonance theory"}, {"q": "Which concept refers to mental shortcuts in thinking?", "options": {"a": "Insight", "b": "Heuristics", "c": "Intelligence", "d": "Schema"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: heuristics are mental shortcuts in thinking. A heuristic is a rule of thumb that lets us judge or decide quickly with little effort, at the cost of occasional systematic error. It contrasts with an algorithm, a step-by-step procedure that guarantees a correct answer if followed but may be slow. Herbert Simon's idea of bounded rationality explained why we need shortcuts: limited time, information and mental capacity mean we satisfice rather than optimise. Amos Tversky and Daniel Kahneman catalogued the main heuristics in their 1974 Science paper 'Judgment under Uncertainty: Heuristics and Biases'. The availability heuristic judges frequency by how easily examples come to mind; the representativeness heuristic judges probability by similarity to a typical case, producing base-rate neglect and the conjunction fallacy (the Linda problem); anchoring and adjustment starts from an initial value and adjusts too little. Gerd Gigerenzer later argued that fast and frugal heuristics are often ecologically rational, not merely error-prone. Kahneman received the Nobel Prize in economics in 2002. Example: after news of two plane crashes, a person judges flying more dangerous than driving because crashes are easy to recall (availability), although road deaths are far more common. Option b is right because 'mental shortcut' is the textbook definition of a heuristic. Exam trap: a schema (d) also speeds up thinking, but a schema is an organised store of knowledge about a category, whereas a heuristic is a strategy for reaching a judgement; do not confuse the knowledge structure with the shortcut rule.", "topic": "Heuristics as mental shortcuts"}, {"q": "Lashley's \"mass action\" principle primarily challenged which of the following beliefs?", "options": {"a": "That specific brain areas control motor movements", "b": "That brain lesions have no impact on learning", "c": "That memories are stored diffusely across the cortex", "d": "That learning is localized in discrete brain regions"}, "correct": "d", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is d: Lashley's mass action principle challenged the belief that learning is localised in discrete brain regions. Karl Spencer Lashley (1890 to 1958) spent decades hunting the engram, the physical trace of a memory, a term coined by Richard Semon (1904). He trained rats to run mazes, then removed different parts of their cortex, expecting that some particular lesion would wipe out the maze habit. None did. In Brain Mechanisms and Intelligence (1929) he stated two principles. Mass action: how badly learning was impaired depended on how much cortex was removed, not where. Equipotentiality: within a functional area, any part of the cortex could take over the work of any other. His review 'In Search of the Engram' (1950) concluded, half in jest, that learning should be impossible, since he could find memory nowhere in particular; it must be distributed. This opposed the localisationist view, strengthened since Paul Broca's speech area (1861), that each function, including learned habits, sits in a specific spot. His student Donald Hebb explained the result with cell assemblies spread across the cortex (1949). Example: in Lashley's pattern, a rat that lost a little cortex made few extra maze errors, while one that lost half its cortex made many more, whichever area was removed. Option d is right because mass action argued against discrete localisation of learning, replacing it with a distributed, amount-dependent view. Exam trap: option c, memories stored diffusely across the cortex, is not what Lashley challenged; it is Lashley's own conclusion, so choosing it reverses the question.", "topic": "Lashley's mass action and equipotentiality"}, {"q": "Cognitive triad in depression includes:", "options": {"a": "Self, world, future", "b": "Id, ego, superego", "c": "Past, present, future", "d": "Thoughts, emotions, behaviours"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: the cognitive triad in depression consists of the self, the world and the future. Aaron Temkin Beck (1921 to 2021), a psychoanalytically trained psychiatrist at the University of Pennsylvania, noticed in the late 1950s that depressed patients had streams of quick, self-critical automatic thoughts that came before their low mood. In Depression (1967) he proposed that depression is maintained by a negative cognitive triad: a negative view of the self ('I am worthless'), of the world or current experience ('nothing goes right, people are against me') and of the future ('it will never get better'). These views grow from negative core beliefs, or schemas, laid down in childhood and activated by stress, and they are kept alive by cognitive distortions such as overgeneralisation and selective abstraction. Cognitive therapy (Cognitive Therapy of Depression, 1979, with colleagues) teaches the patient to catch, test and restructure such thoughts. The negative view of the future, hopelessness, is especially linked to suicide risk; Beck also built the Beck Depression Inventory (1961) and the Beck Hopelessness Scale. Example: after failing one test a student thinks 'I am stupid' (self), 'teachers always mark me down' (world) and 'I will never get into an M.Phil' (future). Option a is right because self, world and future are the three targets of negative thinking in Beck's triad. Exam trap: this same paper asked the triad at Q3 as a NOT question, with 'negative view of past' as the odd one out; Beck's triad has no past, so 'past, present, future' (c) is the bait both times.", "topic": "Beck's cognitive triad"}, {"q": "What is a core concept of Bandura's cognitive theory?", "options": {"a": "Classical conditioning", "b": "Observational learning", "c": "Psychoanalysis", "d": "Moral reasoning"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: observational learning is a core concept of Bandura's social cognitive theory. Albert Bandura (1925 to 2021), a Canadian-born psychologist at Stanford, argued that people learn a great deal simply by watching others, called models, and the consequences of their behaviour, without performing the behaviour or being reinforced themselves. In the Bobo doll experiment (Bandura, Dorothea Ross and Sheila Ross, 1961), nursery-school children who watched an adult punch and kick an inflatable doll later copied the model's specific acts and words, while children who saw a calm model did not. In 1965, children who saw the model punished imitated less, until offered a reward to show what they had seen, when they reproduced it fully: reinforcement affects performance, not learning. Bandura named four processes: attention, retention, motor reproduction and motivation. With Richard Walters (1963) he developed social learning theory, which he renamed social cognitive theory in Social Foundations of Thought and Action (1986). Its other pillars are reciprocal determinism (person, behaviour and environment influence one another) and self-efficacy (1977), belief in one's capacity to perform a task. Example: a child watches an older sibling being praised for tying shoelaces, remembers the steps, and later ties her own when she wants praise too. Option b is right because learning by observing models is the defining idea of Bandura's theory. Exam trap: do not confuse Bandura with Julian Rotter, whose separate social learning theory (1954) is about expectancy, reinforcement value and locus of control; observational learning and the Bobo doll belong to Bandura.", "topic": "Bandura's observational learning"}, {"q": "According to Piaget, which stage involves abstract thinking?", "options": {"a": "Sensorimotor", "b": "Preoperational", "c": "Concrete operational", "d": "Formal operational"}, "correct": "d", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is d: according to Piaget, abstract thinking appears in the formal operational stage. Jean Piaget (1896 to 1980) described four invariant stages of cognitive development, each a qualitatively different way of thinking: sensorimotor (birth to about 2 years), preoperational (about 2 to 7), concrete operational (about 7 to 11) and formal operational (from about 11 or 12 onward). In the formal stage thought is freed from concrete, present objects. Adolescents can reason about abstractions such as justice or infinity, about the possible as well as the real, and about propositions whose conclusions follow from their form. The hallmark is hypothetico-deductive reasoning: forming hypotheses and testing them systematically, one variable at a time. Barbel Inhelder and Piaget showed this in The Growth of Logical Thinking from Childhood to Adolescence (1958) with the pendulum problem: a concrete thinker changes length, weight and push together, while a formal thinker varies one factor at a time and finds that only string length matters. Formal thought also brings idealism and adolescent egocentrism (David Elkind's imaginary audience and personal fable), and not everyone reaches it; many adults reason formally only in familiar domains. Example: a 14-year-old asked 'If all dogs were purple and Bruno is a dog, what colour is Bruno?' answers 'purple', accepting the premise, while a 7-year-old protests that dogs are not purple. Option d is right because reasoning about abstract and hypothetical ideas defines the formal operational stage. Exam trap: concrete operational children do think logically (they master conservation and classification), but only about real, tangible objects; logical is not the same as abstract.", "topic": "Piaget's formal operational stage"}, {"q": "Which is an example of a cognitive distortion?", "options": {"a": "Observational learning", "b": "Mind reading", "c": "Reinforcement", "d": "Free association"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: mind reading is an example of a cognitive distortion. Cognitive distortions are systematic errors in processing information that keep negative emotions going. Aaron Beck identified the first set in his work on depression in the 1960s: arbitrary inference, selective abstraction, overgeneralisation, magnification and minimisation, personalisation and dichotomous (all-or-nothing) thinking. The psychiatrist David Burns popularised a longer list in Feeling Good (1980), where mind reading and fortune telling are the two forms of 'jumping to conclusions'. Mind reading means assuming you know what other people are thinking, usually something negative about you, without checking. Fortune telling is predicting that things will turn out badly. Other distortions include emotional reasoning ('I feel useless, so I am useless'), should statements, labelling and catastrophising. In cognitive therapy the patient records automatic thoughts, names the distortion, weighs the evidence for and against the thought, and writes a more balanced alternative. Example: during her case presentation a trainee sees her supervisor frowning and concludes 'she thinks I am incompetent'; in therapy she would note that the frown might equally mean concentration or a headache. Option b is right because mind reading is a standard listed cognitive distortion, an unsupported conclusion about what others think. Exam trap: mind reading is a specific form of Beck's broader arbitrary inference (a conclusion drawn without evidence), so when another question describes a conclusion with no evidence and offers 'arbitrary inference', that is the Beck-era name for the same family of error.", "topic": "Cognitive distortions: mind reading"}, {"q": "Cognitive appraisal refers to:", "options": {"a": "Judging others", "b": "Evaluating environmental stressors", "c": "Unconscious motives", "d": "Genetic traits"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: cognitive appraisal refers to evaluating environmental stressors. Richard Lazarus (1922 to 2002) argued that stress and emotion depend less on events themselves than on how we interpret them. He set out the idea in Psychological Stress and the Coping Process (1966), and with Susan Folkman built the transactional model of stress (Stress, Appraisal, and Coping, 1984), in which stress is a relationship between person and environment that the person appraises as taxing or exceeding their resources. Primary appraisal asks 'Does this matter to me, and how?': the event is judged irrelevant, benign-positive or stressful, and if stressful, as harm-loss, threat or challenge. Secondary appraisal asks 'What can I do about it?', weighing coping resources and options. Reappraisal revises both as the situation and coping unfold. Because appraisal sits between event and response, two people facing the same stressor can react very differently, and treatments such as stress inoculation and cognitive restructuring work by changing appraisals. Example: two candidates face the same entrance exam; one appraises it as a threat and her preparation as thin, and panics, while the other sees a challenge, trusts her practice and feels focused. Option b is right because cognitive appraisal is exactly this evaluation of a potential stressor and of one's ability to cope with it. Exam trap: primary appraisal judges the event and secondary appraisal judges the coping resources; papers often swap them, and 'secondary' does not mean less important or a later reaction to stress.", "topic": "Lazarus's cognitive appraisal of stress"}, {"q": "Which school of psychology emphasized introspection?", "options": {"a": "Behaviorism", "b": "Structuralism", "c": "Humanism", "d": "Psychoanalysis"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: structuralism is the school of psychology that emphasised introspection. Wilhelm Wundt (1832 to 1920) founded experimental psychology at Leipzig in 1879 and studied immediate conscious experience by controlled introspection: trained observers gave short reports of their sensations and feelings in response to carefully controlled stimuli. His student Edward Bradford Titchener (1867 to 1927) took a stricter version to Cornell University and named it structuralism (1898). Its goal was to analyse consciousness into basic elements, sensations, images and affections, much as chemists built a periodic table, and to find how they combine. Introspection was its main method: observers had to describe raw experience and avoid the 'stimulus error' of naming the object instead. Structuralism faded after Titchener's death in 1927 because introspective reports could not be checked or settle disputes between laboratories, and because it excluded children, animals and applied problems. Functionalism (William James, John Dewey) asked what the mind is for, and behaviourism rejected introspection outright. Example: shown an apple, a structuralist observer should not say 'apple' but report 'a glossy red patch, roundish, cool to the touch, mildly pleasant'. Option b is right because introspection was the defining method of structuralism. Exam trap: Indian question papers often call Wundt himself a structuralist, but strictly Titchener named and led structuralism; if both appear, Titchener is the founder of structuralism and Wundt the father of experimental psychology, and both used introspection.", "topic": "Structuralism and introspection"}, {"q": "Who is considered the father of experimental psychology?", "options": {"a": "Freud", "b": "Wundt", "c": "Watson", "d": "Skinner"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: Wilhelm Wundt is considered the father of experimental psychology. Wundt (1832 to 1920), a German physician who had assisted Hermann von Helmholtz, argued that the mind could be studied with the experimental methods of physiology. His Principles of Physiological Psychology (1873 to 1874) announced a new science, and in 1879 at the University of Leipzig his students began original research in what is conventionally called the first psychological laboratory; that year is taken as the birth of psychology as an independent science. He founded the journal Philosophische Studien (1881) and trained many pioneers, including Edward Titchener, James McKeen Cattell, Emil Kraepelin and Charles Spearman. His method was controlled introspection: trained observers reacted to precise stimuli, such as a metronome beat or a flash of light, and reported their immediate experience while reaction times were measured. He also wrote the ten-volume Volkerpsychologie (folk psychology) on language, myth and custom, which he thought needed non-experimental methods. Example: in a Leipzig reaction-time study an observer pressed a key as soon as he heard a click, and Wundt compared this simple reaction with one that required recognising the sound first. Option b is right because Wundt gave psychology its first laboratory, journal and trained students, the marks of an experimental science. Exam trap: read the qualifier: Gustav Fechner (Elements of Psychophysics, 1860) is the father of psychophysics, William James of American psychology, John B. Watson of behaviourism and Sigmund Freud of psychoanalysis; only experimental psychology belongs to Wundt.", "topic": "Wundt, father of experimental psychology"}, {"q": "Operant conditioning was proposed by:", "options": {"a": "Bandura", "b": "Skinner", "c": "Pavlov", "d": "Thorndike"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: operant conditioning was proposed by B. F. Skinner. Burrhus Frederic Skinner (1904 to 1990), a radical behaviourist at Harvard, set out the idea in The Behavior of Organisms (1938). He separated respondent behaviour, elicited by a stimulus as in Pavlov's work, from operant behaviour, which the organism emits freely and which 'operates' on the environment. In operant conditioning the probability of a response is changed by its consequences: reinforcement (positive or negative) makes it more likely, and punishment less likely. Skinner built the operant chamber, or Skinner box, in which a rat could press a lever at any time while a cumulative recorder plotted its response rate, and with Charles Ferster (1957) he mapped schedules of reinforcement; he also studied shaping by successive approximations and extinction. His work underlies applied behaviour analysis, token economies and programmed instruction. Example: a child who gets a star each time she finishes homework without reminders does so more often; the star is positive reinforcement. Option b is right because Skinner coined the term operant and developed operant conditioning as a system. Exam trap: Edward Thorndike came first: his puzzle-box studies with cats (1898) and his law of effect (1911) described learning by consequences, now called instrumental learning, and Skinner built on them; if asked who first showed learning from consequences, think Thorndike, and if asked who named operant conditioning, choose Skinner. Key note: some texts credit Thorndike as the originator of this kind of learning and call Skinner its systematiser, so d has historical support, but the term and theory of operant conditioning are Skinner's, and the official key b stands.", "topic": "Skinner and operant conditioning"}, {"q": "Which type of memory lasts for only a few seconds?", "options": {"a": "Long-term memory", "b": "Semantic memory", "c": "Sensory memory", "d": "Working memory"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: sensory memory lasts for only a few seconds. In Richard Atkinson and Richard Shiffrin's multi-store model (1968), information passes from sensory memory to short-term memory if attended, and on to long-term memory if rehearsed. Sensory memory, the sensory register, holds a large amount of raw information from each sense very briefly, just long enough for attention to select what matters. Ulric Neisser (1967) named the visual store iconic memory and the auditory store echoic memory. George Sperling (1960) flashed twelve letters for 50 milliseconds; people reported only four or five in all, but when a tone after the flash cued one row, they gave about three of its four letters, so most of the display had briefly been available. Delaying the tone by about a second removed the advantage: the icon fades within roughly a quarter to half a second. Echoic memory lasts about 2 to 4 seconds. Short-term memory lasts about 18 to 30 seconds unrehearsed (Lloyd and Margaret Peterson, 1959), and long-term memory from minutes to a lifetime. Example: when a friend asks 'What did I just say?' while you were reading, you can often recover the last few words from your echoic store. Option c is right because sensory memory is the store whose whole lifespan is a few seconds or less. Exam trap: working memory (d) is also brief, but it lasts tens of seconds unrehearsed and can be held indefinitely by rehearsal. Key note: some introductory books loosely say short-term memory lasts 'a few seconds', which makes d tempting, but sensory memory is the standard answer and the official key c stands.", "topic": "Duration of sensory memory"}, {"q": "Which theory emphasizes self-actualization?", "options": {"a": "Psychoanalytic", "b": "Behaviorist", "c": "Humanistic", "d": "Cognitive"}, "correct": "c", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is c: the humanistic theory emphasises self-actualisation. Humanistic psychology, which Abraham Maslow called the third force, arose in the 1950s and 1960s as a reaction against psychoanalysis (the first force), seen as pessimistic and focused on illness, and behaviourism (the second force), seen as mechanical. It holds that people are basically good, have free will and are motivated to grow. Self-actualisation is the striving to fulfil one's potential, to become everything one is capable of becoming. The neurologist Kurt Goldstein coined the term in The Organism (English edition 1939); Maslow (1908 to 1970) placed it at the top of his hierarchy of needs (1943; Motivation and Personality, 1954), above physiological, safety, belonging and esteem needs. He studied people he judged self-actualised, such as Abraham Lincoln and Eleanor Roosevelt, described their acceptance, spontaneity, autonomy and peak experiences, and estimated that 1 per cent of people or fewer reach this level. Carl Rogers (1902 to 1987) spoke of an actualising tendency in every organism, which flourishes under unconditional positive regard. Example: a successful engineer leaves a secure job to train as a classical musician because she feels it is what she was meant to do; Maslow would call this self-actualisation. Option c is right because self-actualisation is the central motivational concept of humanistic theory. Exam trap: self-efficacy (Bandura's social cognitive idea) and the ideal self (Rogers) sound similar, but self-actualisation means fulfilling one's potential, and it belongs to the humanistic school.", "topic": "Humanistic theory and self-actualisation"}, {"q": "Which part of the nervous system is responsible for rest and digest?", "options": {"a": "Sympathetic", "b": "Somatic", "c": "Parasympathetic", "d": "Central"}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is c: the parasympathetic nervous system is responsible for rest and digest. The nervous system divides into the central nervous system (brain and spinal cord) and the peripheral nervous system. The peripheral system has a somatic branch, which carries sensation and controls voluntary skeletal muscles, and an autonomic branch, which runs the internal organs automatically. The autonomic system has two divisions that usually work in opposition. The sympathetic division mobilises energy for emergencies, the fight-or-flight response described by Walter Cannon (1915): the heart races, pupils dilate, airways widen, digestion halts and the adrenal medulla releases adrenaline. The parasympathetic division conserves and restores energy: it slows the heart, constricts the pupils, increases salivation and stimulates digestion and elimination. Its fibres leave the brainstem in cranial nerves III, VII, IX and X and the sacral spinal cord (the craniosacral outflow), and the vagus nerve (X) carries most of them to the heart, lungs and gut. Acetylcholine is its transmitter at both ganglia and target organs. Most organs receive both divisions, so heart rate reflects a balance between sympathetic acceleration and vagal braking. Example: after a big lunch you feel drowsy, your heart slows and your stomach gets on with digestion; the parasympathetic system is in charge. Option c is right because rest and digest is the textbook label for parasympathetic function. Exam trap: the sympathetic division has the thoracolumbar outflow and the parasympathetic the craniosacral; in sexual response, erection is parasympathetic but ejaculation sympathetic ('point and shoot').", "topic": "Parasympathetic rest and digest"}, {"q": "Classical conditioning involves:", "options": {"a": "Reinforcement", "b": "Voluntary behavior", "c": "Involuntary response to stimuli", "d": "Modeling"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: classical conditioning involves an involuntary response to stimuli. Ivan Petrovich Pavlov (1849 to 1936), a Russian physiologist who won the 1904 Nobel Prize for his work on digestion, noticed that his dogs salivated before food arrived, at the sight of the dish or the attendant's footsteps, and from about 1897 studied these 'psychic secretions' systematically. An unconditioned stimulus (UCS, food) naturally produces an unconditioned response (UCR, salivation). A neutral stimulus, such as a metronome (popularly a bell), is presented just before the food; after several pairings it becomes a conditioned stimulus (CS) that alone produces a conditioned response (CR, salivation). The responses involved are reflexive and involuntary: salivation, fear, nausea, eye blinks, heart-rate change. Related phenomena include extinction, spontaneous recovery, generalisation and discrimination. John B. Watson and Rosalie Rayner's Little Albert study (1920) showed that human fear can be conditioned in the same way, and Joseph Wolpe's systematic desensitisation later used conditioning to reverse such fears. Example: a patient who receives chemotherapy in one clinic starts to feel nauseous on entering its waiting room; the room (CS) now triggers involuntary nausea (CR). Option c is right because classical conditioning attaches an existing involuntary, reflexive response to a new stimulus through association. Exam trap: Pavlov himself called the food the 'reinforcement' of the conditioned reflex, but in exam language reinforcement means a consequence that strengthens behaviour in operant conditioning; classical conditioning works by pairing stimuli before the response, operant conditioning by consequences after it.", "topic": "Classical conditioning and involuntary responses"}, {"q": "The 'Yerkes-Dodson Law' explains the relationship between arousal and:", "options": {"a": "Emotion", "b": "Learning", "c": "Performance", "d": "Memory"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: the Yerkes-Dodson law explains the relationship between arousal and performance. In 1908 Robert Mearns Yerkes and John Dillingham Dodson trained mice on a brightness discrimination, giving electric shocks for wrong choices. Learning was fastest at intermediate shock strengths, and the harder the discrimination, the weaker the best shock. Later textbooks generalised this into the Yerkes-Dodson law: performance rises with arousal (or anxiety, or motivation) up to an optimal level and then falls, an inverted-U curve. The optimum depends on task difficulty: simple or well-learned tasks are done best at relatively high arousal, complex or new tasks at lower, moderate arousal. Too little arousal leaves a person drowsy and careless; too much makes them tense and disorganised. Donald Hebb's optimal arousal theory (1955) gave the idea a physiological basis, and Robert Zajonc's account of social facilitation (1965) used similar logic: an audience raises arousal, helping easy tasks and harming difficult ones. Example: mild exam nerves sharpen your focus in an easy MCQ section, but panic during a hard statistics problem makes you forget formulas you knew well. Option c is right because the law describes how the level of arousal affects the quality of performance. Exam trap: the relationship is an inverted U, never linear, and complex tasks have a lower optimum, not a higher one. Key note: the original 1908 study actually measured how quickly mice learned a habit, so 'learning' (b) has some historical basis, but the law as universally taught links arousal to performance, and the official key c stands.", "topic": "Yerkes-Dodson law: arousal and performance"}, {"q": "Which lobe of the brain is responsible for vision?", "options": {"a": "Parietal", "b": "Frontal", "c": "Temporal", "d": "Occipital"}, "correct": "d", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is d: the occipital lobe is responsible for vision. Each cerebral hemisphere has four lobes, divided by the central sulcus and the lateral (Sylvian) fissure: frontal, parietal, temporal and occipital. The occipital lobe, at the back of the brain, contains the primary visual cortex (V1, also called striate cortex or Brodmann area 17) along the calcarine sulcus. Signals travel from the retina through the optic nerve, the optic chiasm (where fibres from the nasal half of each retina cross) and the lateral geniculate nucleus of the thalamus to V1, so each hemisphere sees the opposite visual field. David Hubel and Torsten Wiesel shared the 1981 Nobel Prize for showing that V1 cells respond to edges and lines of particular orientations. Beyond V1, processing splits into two streams described by Leslie Ungerleider and Mortimer Mishkin (1982): a ventral 'what' stream into the temporal lobe for recognising objects and faces, and a dorsal 'where' (or 'how') stream into the parietal lobe for location and movement. Example: a stroke in the right occipital lobe leaves a person blind to the left side of space in both eyes (left homonymous hemianopia), even though the eyes themselves are healthy. Option d is right because primary visual processing takes place in the occipital lobe. Exam trap: damage outside the occipital lobe can still disturb seeing, since temporal damage causes prosopagnosia (failure to recognise faces) and parietal damage causes neglect; these are higher visual functions, but vision itself begins in the occipital cortex.", "topic": "Occipital lobe and vision"}, {"q": "Latent learning challenges which of the following assumptions of strict behaviorism?", "options": {"a": "Learning only occurs through shaping", "b": "Reinforcement is required for learning to occur", "c": "Extinction reduces the likelihood of behavior permanently", "d": "Classical conditioning precedes operant conditioning"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: latent learning challenges the strict behaviourist assumption that reinforcement is required for learning to occur. For behaviourists such as Edward Thorndike and Clark Hull, learning meant stamping in stimulus-response connections through reward: no reinforcement, no learning. Edward Chace Tolman (1886 to 1959), a purposive behaviourist at Berkeley, disagreed. In Tolman and Charles Honzik's experiment (1930), three groups of hungry rats ran a complex maze daily. One group always found food in the goal box, one never did, and the third found food only from day 11. Until then the third group made as many errors as the never-rewarded rats, but once food appeared its errors fell almost at once to the level of the always-rewarded group. It must have been learning the maze all along without reward; the food only gave it a reason to show what it knew. This is latent learning: learning without obvious reinforcement that stays hidden until there is an incentive to perform. Tolman (1948) said the rats had built a cognitive map, a mental representation of the maze. The study separates learning from performance, a distinction Albert Bandura later drew on. Example: a child who rides to school in the car every day without paying attention can suddenly guide a lost visitor there; the route was learned without any reward. Option b is right because the rats learned without reinforcement, contradicting the claim that reinforcement is necessary for learning. Exam trap: latent learning and cognitive maps are Tolman's, not Kohler's (insight) or Bandura's (observational learning), although all three challenged strict behaviourism.", "topic": "Tolman's latent learning"}]};
window.PREVIEW_DEEP = Object.assign(window.PREVIEW_DEEP||{}, {"gmch-2025": [{"q": "Which of the following models emphasizes the influence of early childhood experiences on psychopathology?", "concept": "The answer is c: the psychodynamic model emphasises the influence of early childhood experiences on psychopathology. The model grew out of Sigmund Freud's psychoanalysis, developed in Vienna from the 1890s. Freud argued that adult symptoms are the surface signs of unconscious conflicts between the drives of the id, the ego and the superego, and that these conflicts take shape in the first five or six years of life as the child passes through the oral, anal and phallic psychosexual stages. If a stage is badly frustrated or overindulged, libido stays partly fixated there, and stress in adulthood can trigger regression to it. Defence mechanisms such as repression keep the painful material out of awareness, so a symptom is a compromise between a wish and a defence. Later psychodynamic writers kept the emphasis: object-relations theorists put the early bond with the caregiver at the centre. Example: a woman who was harshly criticised by her father becomes anxious and self-punishing whenever a male boss evaluates her work; a psychodynamic therapist traces the anxiety to that early relationship and watches it replayed in the transference. Option c is right because only the psychodynamic model makes childhood experience, processed unconsciously, the main cause of later disorder. Exam trap: the biopsychosocial model also counts early experience as one psychological factor, and behaviourists accept that learning starts in childhood, but neither gives childhood a special causal status; 'early childhood experiences' is the signature of Freud's tradition.", "traps": {"a": "The behavioural model (Pavlov, Watson, Skinner) explains disorder as maladaptive learning through conditioning and reinforcement; it focuses on present stimuli and consequences, not on the formative weight of early childhood.", "b": "The cognitive model (Beck, Ellis) blames faulty beliefs and distorted thinking; Beck's core beliefs may form in childhood, but the model targets current thoughts, not childhood experience, as the cause.", "d": "The biopsychosocial model of George Engel (1977) explains illness through interacting biological, psychological and social factors; it is integrative and does not single out early childhood experience."}}, {"q": "What is the primary characteristic of a diathesis-stress model?", "concept": "The answer is b: the primary characteristic of the diathesis-stress model is the interaction between a vulnerability, often genetic, and environmental stress. A diathesis is a predisposition: usually an inherited biological vulnerability, but it can also be psychological, such as a pessimistic thinking style or an early loss. A stressor is the triggering event, such as a bereavement, a break-up or an exam failure. The model's central claim is that neither part is enough alone: a disorder appears only when stress pushes a vulnerable person past a threshold. Paul Meehl applied the idea to schizophrenia in 1962, proposing an inherited 'schizotaxia' that becomes illness only under adverse conditions, and Joseph Zubin and Bonnie Spring (1977) formalised it as the vulnerability-stress model: the more vulnerable you are, the less stress it takes to tip you into illness. Later versions add protective factors, such as social support, coping skills and continued medication, which raise the threshold and are used in relapse prevention. Example: identical twins share the genes for schizophrenia, yet only one, who loses her job and moves alone to a new city, develops psychosis; the shared diathesis was activated only in the twin under stress. Option b is right because the model is defined by the interaction of predisposition and stress, not by either alone. Exam trap: any option containing 'only', 'solely' or a single cause is wrong for diathesis-stress, and the wider biopsychosocial model of George Engel (1977) is a different framework.", "traps": {"a": "Emphasising unconscious conflicts is the psychodynamic model of Freud; the diathesis-stress model is about a vulnerability plus a trigger, not intrapsychic conflict.", "c": "Attributing mental illness solely to social factors describes a purely sociocultural view; the word 'solely' contradicts the core idea that vulnerability and stress must combine.", "d": "Considering only biological factors is the narrow biological (medical) model; diathesis-stress adds environmental stress as the necessary trigger."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT a component of Beck's cognitive triad?", "concept": "The answer is c: a negative view of the past is NOT part of Beck's cognitive triad. Aaron T. Beck, an American psychiatrist trained in psychoanalysis, noticed that his depressed patients reported quick, believable, self-critical automatic thoughts rather than the anger turned inward that Freudian theory predicted. He set out his model in Depression (1967) and the treatment in Cognitive Therapy of Depression (1979, with colleagues). At its centre is the cognitive triad: a negative view of the self ('I am worthless'), of the world or current experience ('everyone is against me') and of the future ('it will never get better'). These views are fed by deeper core beliefs, or schemas, formed in childhood, and are kept alive by cognitive distortions such as overgeneralisation, personalisation and all-or-nothing thinking. Therapy teaches the client to catch automatic thoughts, test them against evidence and build balanced alternatives. Example: a student who fails one test thinks 'I am stupid' (self), 'the teachers are unfair to me' (world) and 'I will fail the whole course' (future). Option c is right because the triad has exactly three parts, self, world and future, and the past is not one of them. Exam trap: students remember 'three time points' and pick past, present and future, or confuse the triad with the thoughts, feelings and behaviours that CBT links; remember self, world, future. Depressed people do brood about the past, but that is rumination, not a triad component.", "traps": {"a": "A negative view of the self, seeing oneself as defective, worthless or unlovable, is the first component of Beck's triad, so it is a true component and not the answer to a NOT question.", "b": "A negative view of the world, meaning one's current experiences and surroundings, is the second component of the triad, so it belongs to the model.", "d": "A negative view of the future, expecting present suffering to go on indefinitely, is the third component and links the triad to hopelessness and suicide risk."}}, {"q": "In research methodology, which type of validity ensures that the test measures what it intends to measure?", "concept": "The answer is d: construct validity is keyed as the type of validity that ensures a test measures what it intends to measure. Validity in general asks whether a test measures what it claims to measure, the classic definition associated with Henry Garrett. Many attributes psychologists care about, such as anxiety, intelligence or extraversion, are constructs: hypothetical qualities that cannot be observed directly. Lee Cronbach and Paul Meehl (1955) argued that such a test is validated by placing the construct in a nomological network, the web of relationships the theory predicts, and checking that scores behave accordingly. Evidence includes convergent validity (high correlations with other measures of the same construct), discriminant validity (low correlations with measures of different constructs), factor structure, group differences and changes after experimental manipulation. Samuel Messick (1989) went further and treated construct validity as the unifying idea that absorbs all the other kinds. Example: a new anxiety scale should correlate highly with the Beck Anxiety Inventory, weakly with a vocabulary test, and rise in students just before an exam; if it does, it really measures the anxiety construct. Option d is right because, of the four, construct validity is the one that asks directly whether a test captures the theoretical attribute it was built to measure. Exam trap: face validity sounds closest because it concerns whether a test looks as if it measures what it should, but appearance to laypeople is not evidence. Key note: the stem is really the general definition of validity itself, so a purist could say no single subtype fits; construct validity is the best fit, especially under Messick's unified view, and the official key stands.", "traps": {"a": "Content validity is how adequately the items sample the whole domain, judged by experts (as when a final exam covers every unit of the syllabus); it concerns coverage, not whether the underlying attribute is captured.", "b": "Face validity is only the appearance of validity to test takers and laypeople; it involves no statistical evidence, so it cannot ensure that a test measures what it intends to.", "c": "Criterion validity is how well scores correlate with an external criterion, now (concurrent) or later (predictive), as when an aptitude test predicts grades; it shows usefulness for prediction rather than what the test measures."}}, {"q": "Which statistical test is appropriate for comparing means between two independent groups?", "concept": "The answer is c: the t-test, specifically the independent-samples t-test, is the appropriate test for comparing the means of two independent groups. William Sealy Gosset, a chemist at the Guinness brewery in Dublin, worked out the t distribution for small samples and published it in Biometrika in 1908 under the pen name 'Student', hence Student's t. The test divides the difference between two sample means by the standard error of that difference; a large t means the gap is big relative to the chance variation expected. For two independent groups the degrees of freedom are n1 + n2 minus 2. It is a parametric test, assuming an interval or ratio dependent variable, roughly normal distributions, homogeneity of variance (checked with Levene's test) and independent observations. There are three versions: the one-sample t-test, the independent-samples t-test for two separate groups, and the paired (related) t-test for the same people measured twice. Example: a researcher compares the mean anxiety scores of 30 students given mindfulness training with those of 30 controls; an independent-samples t-test asks whether the difference exceeds what chance would produce. Option c is right because two groups plus a comparison of means is exactly the t-test's job. Exam trap: ANOVA can also handle two groups (F then equals t squared), but it is designed for three or more; and if the data are ordinal or badly non-normal, the non-parametric alternative is the Mann-Whitney U test, not chi-square.", "traps": {"a": "The chi-square test (Karl Pearson, 1900) compares frequencies or counts in categories, such as how many men and women choose each therapy; it does not compare means.", "b": "ANOVA, developed by Ronald Fisher, compares the means of three or more groups at once and avoids the inflated Type I error of many t-tests; with only two groups the t-test is the standard choice.", "d": "Regression analysis predicts one variable from one or more others and describes the relationship between them; it is not the standard test for a two-group difference in means."}}, {"q": "The probability of rejecting a true null hypothesis is known as:", "concept": "The answer is a: the probability of rejecting a true null hypothesis is the probability of a Type I error, written alpha. In significance testing the null hypothesis (H0) states that there is no effect or difference. Because decisions are made from samples, two mistakes are possible. A Type I error (alpha error) is rejecting H0 when it is actually true: a false positive. A Type II error (beta error) is retaining H0 when it is actually false: a false negative, a real effect missed. Jerzy Neyman and Egon Pearson, in papers of 1928 and 1933, framed testing as a decision between H0 and an alternative hypothesis with fixed error rates, which gave us these two errors and the idea of power (1 minus beta). The researcher chooses alpha in advance, usually .05 or .01, so the significance level is the Type I error rate. With sample size fixed, lowering alpha cuts Type I errors but raises beta; only a larger sample or a more precise measure reduces both. Example: a trial concludes at p < .05 that a new antidepressant beats placebo, but in truth the drug has no effect; the researchers have made a Type I error. In court terms, a Type I error convicts the innocent and a Type II error frees the guilty. Option a is right because rejecting a true null is the textbook definition of a Type I error. Exam trap: examiners swap the wordings; accepting a false null is Type II, and the probability of correctly rejecting a false null is power, not alpha.", "traps": {"b": "A Type II error is failing to reject a null hypothesis that is actually false, missing a real effect; its probability is beta, the opposite mistake from the one in the stem.", "c": "A confidence interval is a range of values that, with a stated confidence such as 95 percent, is expected to contain the population parameter; it is an estimate, not an error probability.", "d": "The standard deviation is a descriptive measure of how spread out scores are around the mean; it has nothing to do with decision errors in hypothesis testing."}}, {"q": "Which neurotransmitter is most commonly associated with schizophrenia?", "concept": "The answer is a: dopamine is the neurotransmitter most commonly associated with schizophrenia. The dopamine hypothesis grew from drug observations. Jean Delay and Pierre Deniker reported in 1952 that chlorpromazine, the first antipsychotic, calmed psychotic patients. Arvid Carlsson (Nobel Prize, 2000) showed that dopamine is a neurotransmitter, and in 1963 he and Margit Lindqvist argued that antipsychotics block dopamine receptors. Drugs that raise dopamine activity, such as amphetamine and L-dopa, can produce psychotic symptoms. The key evidence came in 1976, when Philip Seeman's group, and Ian Creese, David Burt and Solomon Snyder, showed that the clinical potency of typical antipsychotics tracks how strongly they bind to the D2 receptor. The revised hypothesis says that excess dopamine in the mesolimbic pathway produces positive symptoms (delusions, hallucinations), while too little in the mesocortical pathway contributes to negative and cognitive symptoms. Blocking D2 in the nigrostriatal pathway explains extrapyramidal side effects, and blocking it in the tuberoinfundibular pathway raises prolactin. Example: a man who binges on methamphetamine develops paranoid delusions that look like acute schizophrenia, and they settle when an antipsychotic blocks his D2 receptors. Option a is right because dopamine is the classic, best-established transmitter linked to schizophrenia and to how its drugs work. Exam trap: glutamate (NMDA receptor hypofunction) and serotonin (5-HT2A, the target of atypical drugs) are genuine modern additions, so do not be lured by them; 'most commonly associated' means dopamine.", "traps": {"b": "Serotonin is most associated with depression, anxiety and OCD, the targets of SSRIs; in schizophrenia it plays a secondary role through the 5-HT2A blockade of atypical antipsychotics and the effects of hallucinogens.", "c": "Acetylcholine is the transmitter most linked to Alzheimer's disease (loss of cholinergic neurons in the nucleus basalis of Meynert) and to the neuromuscular junction, not to schizophrenia.", "d": "Glutamate features in the newer NMDA receptor hypofunction hypothesis, because PCP and ketamine mimic both positive and negative symptoms, but it adds to the classic dopamine hypothesis rather than replacing it."}}, {"q": "A patient presents with fluctuating levels of consciousness, visual hallucinations, and autonomic dysfunction. Which condition is most likely?", "concept": "The answer is b: fluctuating consciousness, visual hallucinations and autonomic dysfunction point to dementia with Lewy bodies (DLB). Lewy bodies are abnormal clumps of the protein alpha-synuclein inside neurons, named after Friedrich Lewy, who described them in 1912 in Parkinson's disease. In DLB they spread through the cortex as well as the brainstem. The consensus criteria of Ian McKeith and colleagues (fourth report, 2017) list four core clinical features: fluctuating cognition with marked variation in attention and alertness; recurrent, well-formed visual hallucinations; REM sleep behaviour disorder, in which the person acts out dreams; and parkinsonism (slowness, rigidity, tremor). Supportive features include severe sensitivity to antipsychotics, repeated falls, fainting and autonomic dysfunction such as low blood pressure on standing, constipation and urinary incontinence. Memory may be fairly spared early, while attention, executive and visuospatial skills fail first. Example: a 72-year-old man is lucid one afternoon and drowsy and confused the next, sees small children sitting in his living room, has shouted and kicked in his sleep for years, feels faint on standing and walks with a shuffle. Option b is right because fluctuation, vivid visual hallucinations and autonomic problems together fit DLB and none of the other options. Exam trap: severe antipsychotic sensitivity is the safety point examiners love, since a typical antipsychotic given for the hallucinations can cause dangerous, even fatal, worsening of rigidity and confusion. Delirium also fluctuates, but it is acute and is not offered here.", "traps": {"a": "Schizophrenia usually begins in late adolescence or early adulthood with mainly auditory hallucinations and delusions in clear consciousness; fluctuating alertness and autonomic failure are not features.", "c": "Major depressive disorder involves low mood or loss of interest for at least two weeks; psychotic depression can add mood-congruent hallucinations, but not a fluctuating level of consciousness with autonomic dysfunction.", "d": "OCD consists of obsessions and compulsions with intact consciousness and insight usually preserved; it has no hallucinations, fluctuation or autonomic syndrome."}}, {"q": "The primary feature distinguishing bipolar I disorder from bipolar II disorder is:", "concept": "The answer is c: the presence of at least one manic episode is what distinguishes bipolar I disorder from bipolar II disorder. Emil Kraepelin grouped recurrent mood illness as manic-depressive insanity in 1899, and Karl Leonhard separated unipolar from bipolar forms in 1957. DSM-5-TR defines the episodes by severity and duration. A manic episode is at least one week of abnormally elevated, expansive or irritable mood with increased energy (any duration if hospitalisation is needed), causing marked impairment, hospitalisation or psychotic features. A hypomanic episode has the same kinds of symptoms for at least four days but is not severe enough to cause marked impairment and has no psychosis. Bipolar I needs only one manic episode; depressions are common but not required. Bipolar II needs at least one hypomanic episode and at least one major depressive episode, and never a full manic episode; a first mania changes the diagnosis to bipolar I. Example: a woman with two past depressions has a four-day spell of little sleep, extra productivity and chattiness that friends notice but that does not disrupt her job: bipolar II. If she later spends her savings, believes God has chosen her and is admitted to hospital, the diagnosis becomes bipolar I. Option c is right because mania is required for bipolar I and excluded from bipolar II. Exam trap: bipolar II is not simply milder bipolar I, since its depressions are frequent and disabling, and hypomania can occur in both types, so hypomania alone does not separate them.", "traps": {"a": "Major depressive episodes are required for bipolar II and common in bipolar I, so both types usually have them; their presence cannot tell the two apart.", "b": "Hypomanic episodes are required for bipolar II but can also occur in bipolar I, so hypomania is shared rather than distinguishing; the full manic episode is what marks bipolar I.", "d": "Absence of mood symptoms describes neither disorder; both are defined by episodes of mood disturbance."}}, {"q": "Which of the following tests is primarily used for assessing frontal lobe functioning?", "concept": "The answer is a: the Wisconsin Card Sorting Test (WCST) is the test primarily used to assess frontal lobe functioning. It was devised by David Grant and Esta Berg (1948), and Robert Heaton later wrote its clinical manual (1981). The patient matches response cards to four stimulus cards that differ in colour, shape and number of symbols. The examiner never states the rule and says only 'right' or 'wrong', and after ten correct sorts the rule changes without warning. A healthy person notices the run of 'wrong' feedback and shifts; a patient with frontal damage keeps sorting by the old rule. The key scores are categories achieved, perseverative errors and failure to maintain set, so the test measures executive functions such as abstract reasoning, set-shifting and cognitive flexibility. Brenda Milner (1963) showed in Montreal that patients with dorsolateral frontal excisions made many perseverative errors while those with temporal or posterior lesions did not, establishing the WCST as sensitive to dorsolateral prefrontal dysfunction. Example: after the rule switches from colour to number, a man who has had a frontal stroke keeps placing red cards under the red stimulus card, even while saying the rule has changed. Option a is right because the WCST is the classic executive, frontal-lobe test among the four. Exam trap: the WCST is sensitive to frontal damage but not specific to it, since perseveration also appears in schizophrenia and diffuse damage; and the Rey-Osterrieth figure involves planning in its copy phase but is mainly a test of visuospatial construction and visual memory.", "traps": {"b": "The Wechsler Memory Scale (David Wechsler, 1945) measures auditory, visual and working memory, which depend mainly on the medial temporal lobes and hippocampus, not on frontal executive function.", "c": "The Bender Visual-Motor Gestalt Test (Lauretta Bender, 1938) asks the person to copy nine figures, so it assesses visual-motor integration and was once a general screen for 'organicity', not a specific frontal test.", "d": "The Rey-Osterrieth Complex Figure Test (André Rey, 1941; standardised by Paul-Alexandre Osterrieth, 1944) is copied and then drawn from memory, testing visuospatial construction and visual memory rather than frontal executive function."}}, {"q": "A lesion in the left inferior frontal gyrus is most likely to cause:", "concept": "The answer is b: a lesion in the left inferior frontal gyrus is most likely to cause Broca's aphasia. In 1861 the French surgeon and anatomist Paul Broca examined a patient, Leborgne, nicknamed 'Tan' because it was nearly the only syllable he could say; at autopsy Broca found damage in the left frontal lobe. The region, Broca's area, lies in the pars opercularis and pars triangularis of the left inferior frontal gyrus (Brodmann areas 44 and 45), just in front of the motor cortex that controls the face and mouth. It programmes articulation and assembles grammatical sentences. Broca's aphasia, also called expressive or non-fluent aphasia, produces slow, effortful, telegraphic speech made of content words with the small grammatical words missing (agrammatism). Comprehension is relatively preserved, repetition is impaired, and patients know their speech is wrong and are frustrated. Because the motor strip is next door, right-sided weakness is common. Example: asked about his weekend, a man who has had a left frontal stroke says 'Saturday ... son ... park ... ball' and shakes his head in frustration, though he follows spoken instructions well. Option b is right because the left inferior frontal gyrus is the anatomical home of Broca's area. Exam trap: Broca's area is frontal and serves production, while Wernicke's area is temporal and serves comprehension; a handy mnemonic is 'Broca is broken speech, Wernicke is word salad'.", "traps": {"a": "Wernicke's aphasia (Carl Wernicke, 1874) follows damage to the posterior superior temporal gyrus and gives fluent but meaningless speech with poor comprehension; its lesion is temporal, not frontal.", "c": "Conduction aphasia follows damage to the arcuate fasciculus or supramarginal gyrus that links the two language areas; its hallmark is very poor repetition with fluent speech and good comprehension.", "d": "Anomic aphasia is fluent speech with word-finding pauses and good comprehension and repetition; its lesion site is variable (often temporal or angular gyrus), not specifically the inferior frontal gyrus."}}, {"q": "The primary function of the limbic system is:", "concept": "The answer is b: the primary function of the limbic system is emotion regulation and memory. The name comes from the Latin limbus, 'border', because these structures form a ring around the thalamus at the inner edge of the cerebral hemispheres. In 1937 James Papez proposed an emotional circuit running from the hippocampus through the fornix, mammillary bodies, anterior thalamus and cingulate gyrus and back to the hippocampus. Paul MacLean added the amygdala and other structures and named the whole the limbic system in 1952. Its main members are the hippocampus, which forms new declarative memories and spatial maps; the amygdala, which detects threat and drives fear and emotional memory; the cingulate cortex; the fornix; the septum; and the mammillary bodies, with the hypothalamus closely linked. Evidence comes from H.M., who lost both medial temporal lobes in 1953 surgery and could no longer form new declarative memories, and from the monkeys of Heinrich Klüver and Paul Bucy, which became tame and fearless after removal of the temporal lobes including the amygdala. Example: you remember exactly where you were when you heard shocking news because the amygdala tags the event as emotional and the hippocampus stores it strongly. Option b is right because emotion and memory are the two functions every textbook assigns to the limbic system. Exam trap: the basal ganglia sit close by and help select movements, but motor coordination belongs to the cerebellum, not to the limbic system.", "traps": {"a": "Motor coordination is chiefly the work of the cerebellum, with the basal ganglia selecting and starting movements; the limbic system is not a motor system.", "c": "Visual processing takes place mainly in the occipital lobe, beginning in the primary visual cortex (area 17) and continuing in the ventral and dorsal streams.", "d": "Language production depends on Broca's area in the left inferior frontal gyrus; the limbic system adds emotional tone to speech but does not produce language."}}, {"q": "The MMPI-2 is primarily used to assess:", "concept": "The answer is b: the MMPI-2 is primarily used to assess personality traits and psychopathology. The original Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory was built by the psychologist Starke Hathaway and the psychiatrist J. Charnley McKinley at the University of Minnesota and published in 1943. They used empirical (criterion) keying: an item joined a scale if patients with a given diagnosis answered it differently from normal people, whatever the item seemed to be about. The MMPI-2 (James Butcher and colleagues, 1989) has 567 true-or-false items, updated wording and new US norms. Its validity scales check test-taking attitude, such as L (lie), F (infrequency) and K (defensiveness), and its ten clinical scales include Hypochondriasis, Depression, Hysteria, Psychopathic Deviate, Paranoia, Psychasthenia, Schizophrenia and Hypomania. Scores are T scores, with 65 or above marking clinical elevation, and clinicians read the pattern of high scales as a profile code. It is used in psychiatric diagnosis, treatment planning, forensic and custody evaluations and screening for high-risk jobs. Example: a profile with high scales 1 and 3 and a lower scale 2, the 'conversion V', suggests a person who turns stress into physical complaints. Option b is right because the MMPI-2 is the most widely used objective inventory of personality and psychopathology. Exam trap: it is an objective self-report test, not a projective one, and its relatives form one family: the MMPI-A for adolescents (1992), the MMPI-2-RF (2008, 338 items) and the MMPI-3 (2020).", "traps": {"a": "Intelligence is measured by tests such as the Wechsler scales and the Stanford-Binet; the MMPI-2 contains no ability items.", "c": "Neuropsychological deficits are assessed by batteries such as the Halstead-Reitan and Luria-Nebraska and by tests such as the WCST; the MMPI-2 can show emotional reactions to brain injury but does not measure cognitive deficits.", "d": "Emotional intelligence is measured by instruments such as the Mayer-Salovey-Caruso Emotional Intelligence Test (MSCEIT) or the Bar-On EQ-i; it is not what the MMPI-2 was designed to assess."}}, {"q": "The WAIS-IV measures which of the following cognitive abilities?", "concept": "The answer is a: the WAIS-IV measures verbal comprehension, perceptual reasoning, working memory and processing speed. David Wechsler, chief psychologist at New York's Bellevue Hospital, published the Wechsler-Bellevue scale in 1939 because the Stanford-Binet suited adults poorly, leaned heavily on words and relied on mental age. The Wechsler Adult Intelligence Scale followed in 1955, and the fourth edition, the WAIS-IV, appeared in 2008 for ages 16 to 90. It dropped the old Verbal IQ and Performance IQ and organised its ten core subtests into four index scores: Verbal Comprehension (Similarities, Vocabulary, Information), Perceptual Reasoning (Block Design, Matrix Reasoning, Visual Puzzles), Working Memory (Digit Span, Arithmetic) and Processing Speed (Symbol Search, Coding). The four combine into the Full Scale IQ, a deviation IQ with a mean of 100 and a standard deviation of 15, and an optional General Ability Index uses only the first two. Example: a student with a learning disorder may score 115 on Verbal Comprehension but 82 on Processing Speed, a gap that the index profile reveals and a single IQ would hide. Option a is right because those four domains are exactly the four WAIS-IV indices. Exam trap: the WISC-V (2014) and the WAIS-5 (2024) have five primary indices because perceptual reasoning was split into visual spatial and fluid reasoning, so do not carry a five-index answer into a WAIS-IV question.", "traps": {"b": "Emotional intelligence is not measured by any Wechsler intelligence scale, and memory and executive functioning are assessed by tools such as the Wechsler Memory Scale and the WCST, not by the WAIS-IV indices.", "c": "Personality traits are assessed by inventories such as the MMPI-2 or the 16PF; the WAIS-IV is a cognitive ability test and measures no personality traits.", "d": "Sensory-motor integration and coordination are assessed by tests such as the Bender-Gestalt or developmental motor scales; the WAIS-IV has no index for them."}}, {"q": "Which psychological test is used to assess attention deficits in children?", "concept": "The answer is b: the Conners' Continuous Performance Test (the paper spells it Connors') is the test used to assess attention deficits in children. The continuous performance test paradigm began with H. Enger Rosvold and colleagues (1956), who used it to detect brain damage through lapses of sustained attention. C. Keith Conners, an American psychologist also known for the Conners Rating Scales for ADHD, developed the computerised Conners' CPT, now in its third edition (Conners CPT 3), with a separate version for young children. Letters flash on a screen in a long, monotonous stream and the child must press a key for every letter except X, which appears only rarely, so the task demands steady vigilance and the inhibition of a habitual response. Errors of omission (missed responses) reflect inattention, errors of commission (responding when one should not) reflect impulsivity, and slow or highly variable reaction times reflect poor sustained attention. Scores are compared with age norms and support, but never alone make, a diagnosis of attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder. Example: a nine-year-old whose omission errors and reaction-time variability climb steeply in the last blocks of the test shows the declining vigilance typical of ADHD. Option b is right because it is the only option built to measure attention. Exam trap: the correct spelling is Conners, after C. Keith Conners, not Connors; and the WCST also needs attention, but it is primarily a test of executive set-shifting rather than sustained attention.", "traps": {"a": "The Wisconsin Card Sorting Test (Grant and Berg, 1948) measures executive set-shifting and is sensitive to dorsolateral prefrontal damage; it is not the standard measure of sustained attention in children.", "c": "The Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory (Hathaway and McKinley, 1943) is a self-report inventory of personality and psychopathology for adults, with an adolescent version from age 14; it does not measure attention.", "d": "The Beck Depression Inventory (Aaron Beck, 1961; BDI-II, 1996) is a 21-item self-report measure of the severity of depression for ages 13 and over; it does not assess attention."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is a core principle of cognitive-behavioural therapy (CBT)?", "concept": "The answer is b: identifying and modifying dysfunctional thoughts is a core principle of cognitive-behavioural therapy (CBT). CBT rests on the cognitive model: it is not events themselves but how we interpret them that shapes our feelings and behaviour, and thoughts, emotions, bodily sensations and actions keep feeding one another. Two founders stand out: Albert Ellis, whose rational therapy (1955) became rational emotive behaviour therapy, and Aaron Beck, whose cognitive therapy grew from his research on depression. In Beck's approach therapist and client work in collaborative empiricism, treating thoughts as hypotheses to be tested rather than facts. Socratic questioning ('What is the evidence? Is there another way to see it?') guides the client to notice overlooked information. The dysfunctional thought record (1979) has columns for the situation, the emotion and its intensity, the automatic thought, a balanced response and the outcome. Behavioural experiments, activity scheduling and homework turn the new thinking into action, and sessions are structured, time-limited and focused on present problems. Example: a man with social anxiety thinks 'Everyone will see me sweating and think I am weird'; he records the thought, rates his belief at 90 percent, weighs the evidence and tests it by speaking at a meeting, after which his belief falls to 30 percent. Option b is right because cognitive restructuring of dysfunctional thoughts is the defining method of CBT. Exam trap: free association, dream analysis and resolving unconscious conflict are all psychoanalytic; CBT works with conscious, accessible thoughts in the present.", "traps": {"a": "Free association, the 'fundamental rule' of Freud's psychoanalysis, asks the patient to say whatever comes to mind without censoring it so that unconscious material surfaces; CBT does not use it.", "c": "Dream analysis is Freud's 'royal road to the unconscious' (The Interpretation of Dreams, 1900), decoding the manifest content of a dream into its latent wishes; it belongs to psychoanalysis, not CBT.", "d": "Unconscious conflict resolution is the aim of psychodynamic therapy, pursued through interpretation of resistance and transference; CBT targets conscious thoughts and present behaviour."}}, {"q": "In exposure therapy for anxiety disorders, the patient is:", "concept": "The answer is a: in exposure therapy the patient is gradually exposed to the feared stimulus in a controlled manner. Exposure grew out of behaviour therapy, especially Joseph Wolpe's systematic desensitisation (1958), which paired gradual imagined exposure with relaxation. Modern exposure therapy is repeated or prolonged confrontation with a feared object, situation, memory or bodily sensation, without escape, until the fear subsides. Therapist and client first build a fear hierarchy, rating each step on the Subjective Units of Distress Scale (SUDS, 0 to 100), and then climb it step by step. It works through habituation, as anxiety falls with repeated contact, and through extinction or new learning, as the feared outcome fails to happen. Exposure can be in vivo (real life), imaginal, interoceptive (deliberately producing feared sensations in panic disorder) or by virtual reality; exposure and response prevention for OCD and Edna Foa's prolonged exposure for PTSD are leading variants. Example: a woman afraid of dogs first looks at photographs, then watches a dog through a window, then stands across the room from a calm puppy and finally strokes it, staying in each situation until her SUDS rating has halved. Option a is right because gradual, controlled, repeated contact with the feared stimulus is the essence of exposure. Exam trap: flooding is also exposure but starts at the top of the hierarchy at full intensity, so 'gradually' describes graded exposure, the commonest form; and avoidance and safety behaviours maintain anxiety, which is why exposure removes them rather than adding them.", "traps": {"b": "Thought suppression tends to backfire: Daniel Wegner's 'white bear' studies showed that trying not to think of something makes it rebound, so exposure asks patients to approach anxious thoughts, not suppress them.", "c": "Avoidance maintains anxiety: in O. Hobart Mowrer's two-factor theory, escape is negatively reinforced by relief, so the fear is never unlearned; exposure deliberately reverses avoidance.", "d": "Medication before exposure, especially a benzodiazepine, can act as a safety signal and weaken new learning, so giving drugs is not a defining part of exposure therapy."}}, {"q": "Which type of therapy focuses on the client's capacity for self-actualization?", "concept": "The answer is b: humanistic therapy focuses on the client's capacity for self-actualisation. Humanistic psychology, the 'third force' after psychoanalysis and behaviourism, took shape in the United States in the mid-twentieth century around Abraham Maslow and Carl Rogers. It sees people as basically trustworthy, free to choose and naturally striving to grow. Maslow (1943) placed self-actualisation, becoming everything one is capable of becoming, at the top of his hierarchy of needs. Rogers built a therapy on the same faith. His client-centred therapy (Client-Centered Therapy, 1951) assumes an inborn actualising tendency that is blocked when a child absorbs conditions of worth, producing incongruence between self-concept and experience. Healing comes not from interpretation or advice but from three core conditions offered by the therapist: genuineness (congruence), unconditional positive regard and accurate empathic understanding. In that climate the client's own growth tendency resumes and moves the person towards becoming fully functioning. Example: a student who has always studied engineering to please her parents is allowed, without judgement, to voice her love of music; she gradually trusts her own valuing and chooses her own path. Option b is right because self-actualisation and the actualising tendency are the centre of humanistic theory and therapy. Exam trap: existential therapy, a close cousin, aims at meaning, freedom and responsibility, so 'meaning' points to existential and 'self-actualisation' to humanistic; strictly, Rogers's own term was the actualising tendency and self-actualisation is Maslow's.", "traps": {"a": "Cognitive therapy (Aaron Beck) aims to identify and correct distorted automatic thoughts and core beliefs; it targets thinking and symptoms, not an inborn growth tendency.", "c": "Behaviour therapy (Wolpe, Skinner and others) changes observable behaviour through conditioning and reinforcement; it does not invoke self-actualisation.", "d": "Psychoanalysis (Freud) seeks insight into unconscious conflict through free association and interpretation; Freud saw human nature as driven by instincts, not by a growth tendency."}}, {"q": "Which of the following therapeutic approaches emphasizes modifying maladaptive behaviours through reinforcement?", "concept": "The answer is c: behaviour therapy emphasises modifying maladaptive behaviours through reinforcement. Behaviour therapy applies experimentally established principles of learning to psychological problems. It grew from three roots in the 1950s: Joseph Wolpe and Arnold Lazarus in South Africa, working from classical conditioning; Hans Eysenck and Monte Shapiro at the Maudsley Hospital in London; and B. F. Skinner and his students in the United States, who carried operant conditioning onto hospital wards and into classrooms. The name first appeared in a 1953 report by Ogden Lindsley, Skinner and Harry Solomon. Its assumptions are that problem behaviour is learned by the same principles as normal behaviour, that the behaviour itself is the problem rather than a sign of hidden conflict, and that change must be measured. The operant strand uses reinforcement directly: positive reinforcement rewards a target behaviour immediately, shaping reinforces successive approximations, the Premack principle uses a preferred activity as the reward, and the token economy (Teodoro Ayllon and Nathan Azrin, 1968) gives tokens exchangeable for privileges. Unwanted behaviour is weakened by extinction, time-out or response cost. Example: a child with autism who never asks for things is rewarded first for eye contact, then for any sound, then for saying 'wa' and finally for saying 'water'. Option c is right because reinforcement is the core tool of behaviour therapy. Exam trap: cognitive therapy also sets homework and behavioural experiments, but its target is thoughts; reinforcement of overt behaviour is the behaviourist signature.", "traps": {"a": "Psychoanalysis (Freud) works through free association, interpretation and transference to make unconscious conflicts conscious; it does not systematically reinforce behaviour.", "b": "Cognitive therapy (Aaron Beck) changes behaviour indirectly by restructuring distorted thoughts and beliefs; its focus is cognition, not reinforcement contingencies.", "d": "Humanistic therapy (Rogers, Maslow) offers empathy, genuineness and unconditional positive regard so that growth can unfold; unconditional regard is deliberately not contingent on behaviour, the opposite of reinforcement."}}, {"q": "Dialectical Behavior Therapy (DBT) was originally developed for treating which disorder?", "concept": "The answer is c: dialectical behaviour therapy (DBT) was originally developed for treating borderline personality disorder. Marsha Linehan, an American psychologist at the University of Washington, developed it for chronically suicidal, self-harming women, most of whom met criteria for borderline personality disorder (BPD), and published the treatment manuals in 1993. Standard CBT's constant push for change felt invalidating to these clients and many dropped out, yet acceptance alone did not stop the self-harm. DBT's dialectic synthesises the two: full acceptance of the person together with a firm push for change. The acceptance side, including mindfulness, came from Zen practice; the change side came from behaviour therapy. Linehan's biosocial theory sees BPD as pervasive emotion dysregulation that arises when a biologically sensitive, reactive child grows up in an invalidating environment. Standard DBT has four modes: individual therapy, a weekly skills group, telephone coaching between sessions and a therapist consultation team. Its four skills modules are core mindfulness, distress tolerance, emotion regulation and interpersonal effectiveness. Example: a 22-year-old who cuts herself after arguments learns distress tolerance skills, such as holding ice or exercising hard, to ride out the urge, and then uses interpersonal effectiveness skills to ask for what she needs. Option c is right because DBT was designed for, and first tested with, suicidal clients with BPD. Exam trap: DBT has since been adapted for eating disorders, substance use, adolescents and depression, so 'used for' is broad, but 'originally developed for' always means borderline personality disorder.", "traps": {"a": "Major depressive disorder is treated mainly with Beck's cognitive therapy, behavioural activation, interpersonal psychotherapy and mindfulness-based cognitive therapy; DBT was adapted for depression only later.", "b": "Schizophrenia is treated first with antipsychotics, supported by CBT for psychosis, family interventions and social skills training; DBT was not designed for it.", "d": "Generalised anxiety disorder is usually treated with CBT, applied relaxation and worry-focused methods; DBT's emotion dysregulation model was built for BPD, not GAD."}}, {"q": "Which therapy technique involves identifying and challenging irrational beliefs?", "concept": "The answer is a: rational emotive behaviour therapy (REBT) is the technique that involves identifying and challenging irrational beliefs. Albert Ellis, a New York psychologist trained in psychoanalysis, grew frustrated with its slow, passive method and launched rational therapy in 1955; it became rational emotive therapy in 1961 and REBT in 1993. Its core is the ABC model: an Activating event (A) does not directly cause the emotional and behavioural Consequence (C); the Belief (B) about the event does. Rational beliefs are flexible preferences ('I would like to pass'); irrational beliefs are rigid demands, or musts ('I must pass, or I am worthless'), with offshoots such as awfulising, low frustration tolerance and rating one's whole self. The therapist then Disputes (D) each belief actively and directly, asking whether it is logical, realistic and helpful, to reach an Effective new philosophy (E). Ellis grouped the musts into three, about oneself, about others and about the world, and aimed at unconditional self-acceptance: rate your actions, never your whole self. Example: after a rejection a man feels depressed because he believes 'I must be loved by everyone, and it is awful that she rejected me'; the therapist challenges the demand until he holds the preference 'I wanted her to like me, but I can bear this and still accept myself'. Option a is right because disputing irrational beliefs is the defining REBT method. Exam trap: Beck's cognitive therapy also challenges thoughts, but through gentler Socratic questioning of automatic thoughts; 'irrational beliefs' and forceful disputing are Ellis's signature.", "traps": {"b": "Client-centred therapy (Carl Rogers, 1951) is non-directive: the therapist offers empathy, genuineness and unconditional positive regard and does not challenge or dispute the client's beliefs.", "c": "Psychoanalysis (Freud) works on unconscious conflicts through free association and the interpretation of resistance and transference, not by disputing conscious irrational beliefs.", "d": "Gestalt therapy (Fritz Perls; Gestalt Therapy, 1951, with Ralph Hefferline and Paul Goodman) builds here-and-now awareness through techniques such as the empty chair; it does not centre on disputing beliefs."}}, {"q": "In psychodynamic therapy, which defense mechanism involves attributing one's own unacceptable feelings to others?", "concept": "The answer is a: projection is the defence mechanism that involves attributing one's own unacceptable feelings to others. Defence mechanisms are the ego's unconscious strategies for protecting itself from anxiety. Sigmund Freud described many of them, and his daughter Anna Freud set them out systematically in The Ego and the Mechanisms of Defence (1936). In projection, an impulse or feeling that would be too threatening to acknowledge as one's own, such as hostility, envy or sexual desire, is disowned and perceived instead in another person. The feeling does not change; only its owner does. Freud linked projection to paranoia, and George Vaillant later placed it among the immature defences in his hierarchy, below neurotic defences such as repression and displacement and mature ones such as sublimation and humour. Freud also used the word in a broader sense for any colouring of perception by the inner world, which is the basis of projective tests such as the Rorschach. Example: a man attracted to a colleague, who cannot accept the feeling, accuses his wife of flirting with everyone; or a student who dislikes her roommate insists, 'She hates me'. Option a is right because attributing one's own unacceptable feelings to others is the textbook definition of projection. Exam trap: in displacement the feeling stays yours but moves to a safer target, whereas in projection the feeling is placed in someone else; ask whether the owner or the target has changed.", "traps": {"b": "Repression, which Freud called the cornerstone of psychoanalysis, pushes threatening thoughts and memories out of awareness altogether; nothing is attributed to anyone else.", "c": "Sublimation channels an unacceptable impulse into a socially valued activity, as when aggression is poured into boxing or surgery; Vaillant counts it as a mature defence.", "d": "Displacement redirects a feeling from a threatening target to a safer one, as when anger at the boss is taken out on a child; the person still owns the feeling, unlike in projection."}}, {"q": "What is the main goal of existential therapy?", "concept": "The answer is c: the main goal of existential therapy is finding meaning in life. Existential therapy is more a philosophy of living than a set of techniques. It draws on philosophers such as Søren Kierkegaard, Jean-Paul Sartre and Martin Heidegger, whose Being and Time (1927) described human existence as Dasein, 'being there' in a world. Ludwig Binswanger and Medard Boss brought these ideas into psychiatry, Rollo May carried them to America, and Irvin Yalom, in Existential Psychotherapy (1980), named four ultimate concerns: death, freedom with its responsibility, isolation and meaninglessness. Anxiety about these givens is normal; problems arise when people flee into inauthentic living. The therapist helps the client face the givens honestly, accept responsibility for choices and build a meaningful life. Viktor Frankl, a Viennese psychiatrist who survived the Nazi camps, made meaning the explicit centre of his logotherapy and of Man's Search for Meaning (German original, 1946): the primary human motive is the will to meaning, and its frustration produces an existential vacuum. Meaning comes from creating, from loving and experiencing, and from the attitude one takes to unavoidable suffering. Example: a widower who feels his life is pointless is helped to see that caring for his grandchildren and honouring his wife's memory can give his remaining years purpose. Option c is right because confronting meaninglessness and finding meaning is the core aim of existential therapy. Exam trap: self-actualisation is the humanistic goal of Maslow and Rogers; the schools overlap and are sometimes taught together as existential-humanistic therapy, but 'meaning' is the existential keyword.", "traps": {"a": "Resolving unconscious conflicts is the goal of psychoanalysis and psychodynamic therapy (Freud and his successors), not of existential therapy, which works with conscious choice and responsibility.", "b": "Enhancing self-actualisation is the goal of humanistic therapy, especially Rogers's person-centred therapy and Maslow's psychology; it overlaps with existential thought but is not its main aim.", "d": "Modifying maladaptive behaviours through conditioning and reinforcement is the goal of behaviour therapy, not existential therapy."}}, {"q": "Which psychologist is known as the father of modern psychology?", "concept": "The answer is b: Wilhelm Wundt is known as the father of modern psychology. Wundt, a German physician who had worked as an assistant to Hermann von Helmholtz, argued that the mind could be studied with the experimental methods of physiology. In Principles of Physiological Psychology (1873 to 1874) he announced a new science, and in 1879 at the University of Leipzig his students began original research in what is regarded as the first psychology laboratory; 1879 is the conventional birth year of psychology as an independent science. He founded the journal Philosophische Studien in 1881 and trained many pioneers, including Edward Titchener, G. Stanley Hall, James McKeen Cattell, Lightner Witmer and Hugo Münsterberg. Wundt studied immediate conscious experience, sensations and feelings, through introspection under controlled experimental conditions: trained observers gave brief reports on precisely timed stimuli. Because he stressed attention and will in organising experience, his system is called voluntarism, and Titchener's structuralism was a narrower version of it. Example: in a Leipzig reaction-time study, an observer pressed a key when a light flashed and then reported whether attention had been on the light or on the movement. Option b is right because Wundt gave psychology its first laboratory, its first journal and its first trained students, making it a discipline separate from philosophy. Exam trap: 'father' depends on the qualifier: William James is called the father of American psychology, Freud the father of psychoanalysis, Witmer the father of clinical psychology and Fechner the founder of psychophysics.", "traps": {"a": "Sigmund Freud (1856 to 1939) founded psychoanalysis and is the father of that school; his work was clinical rather than laboratory-based, so he is not the father of modern scientific psychology.", "c": "Carl Rogers (1902 to 1987) founded client-centred therapy and led humanistic psychology, the 'third force' of the mid-twentieth century, long after psychology was established.", "d": "B. F. Skinner (1904 to 1990) was the leading radical behaviourist who developed operant conditioning; even behaviourism itself was founded by John B. Watson (1913), not Skinner."}}, {"q": "What is the primary focus of applied psychology?", "concept": "The answer is c: the primary focus of applied psychology is the practical application of psychological knowledge in real-world settings. Psychology has two broad aims. Basic (pure) research seeks understanding for its own sake, asking, for example, how memory or perception works. Applied research and practice use that knowledge to solve concrete problems in clinics, schools, workplaces, courts, hospitals and communities. Applied psychology took shape in the decades around 1900. Lightner Witmer, a student of Wundt, opened the first psychological clinic at the University of Pennsylvania in 1896, the start of clinical psychology. Hugo Münsterberg, another of Wundt's students, applied psychology to eyewitness testimony in On the Witness Stand (1908) and to work in Psychology and Industrial Efficiency (1913), founding forensic and industrial psychology. The intelligence test of Alfred Binet (1905) and the mass testing of soldiers in the First World War spread applied work further. Today its main fields include clinical, counselling, educational and school, industrial-organisational, health, forensic, sports and community psychology. Example: basic research shows that leading questions can distort memory; a forensic psychologist applies that finding to design police interview protocols that avoid suggestive questioning. Option c is right because applied psychology is defined by using psychological principles to solve practical, real-world problems. Exam trap: applied psychologists still run experiments and use research methods, so 'experiments' and 'research methodology' are tools shared with basic psychology, not the defining focus of the applied field.", "traps": {"a": "Theoretical concepts are the main concern of basic or theoretical psychology, which builds and tests explanations for their own sake; applied psychology borrows those concepts to solve practical problems.", "b": "Experiments are a method used across all of psychology, especially experimental psychology; applied psychologists may run them, but the method is not what defines the applied field.", "d": "Research methodology is a toolkit for answering questions in every branch of psychology; it is studied in its own right in methods courses but is not the focus that defines applied psychology."}}, {"q": "What is a primary focus of Acceptance and Commitment Therapy (ACT)?", "concept": "The answer is c: the central aim of Acceptance and Commitment Therapy (ACT) is developing psychological flexibility. ACT (said as the word 'act') was developed by Steven C. Hayes with Kirk Strosahl and Kelly Wilson; their main book appeared in 1999, and it is one of the third-wave cognitive behavioural therapies. It rests on relational frame theory, Hayes's account of human language: because words link events together, the memory of a failure can hurt almost as much as failing. People then try to escape or control painful thoughts and feelings (experiential avoidance), and this struggle narrows their lives. ACT therefore does not try to remove symptoms or win arguments with thoughts. Psychological flexibility means staying in contact with the present moment, thoughts and feelings included, while acting on one's chosen values. It is built through six processes, the hexaflex: acceptance, cognitive defusion, contact with the present moment, self-as-context, values and committed action. Example: a socially anxious student learns to say 'I am having the thought that everyone will laugh at me', notices the anxiety without fighting it, and still raises her hand in class because learning matters to her. Option c is right because psychological flexibility is the stated overall goal of ACT and sits at the centre of the hexaflex. Exam trap: 'challenging irrational beliefs' sounds like any CBT, but ACT deliberately does not dispute the content of thoughts; it changes the person's relationship to them, which is what makes it third wave rather than second wave.", "traps": {"a": "Challenging or disputing irrational beliefs is the core of Albert Ellis's rational emotive behaviour therapy (and close to Beck's cognitive restructuring), second-wave approaches that change thought content; ACT changes the relationship to thoughts instead.", "b": "Suppressing negative emotions is a form of experiential avoidance, the very process ACT treats as the problem; Daniel Wegner's 'white bear' studies (1987) showed that suppressed thoughts tend to rebound.", "d": "Exploring unconscious conflicts is the aim of Freudian psychoanalysis and psychodynamic therapy; ACT works on present experience and values, not on the unconscious."}}, {"q": "Which technique is commonly used in Motivational Interviewing?", "concept": "The answer is b: reflective listening is a core technique of Motivational Interviewing (MI). MI was first described by the psychologist William R. Miller in 1983 for problem drinkers and developed with Stephen Rollnick in their book Motivational Interviewing (1991). It is a collaborative, person-centred but goal-directed way of helping someone resolve ambivalence, the state of wanting and not wanting to change at the same time. Arguing for change usually makes the client defend the status quo, so the counsellor instead draws out the client's own reasons for change ('change talk'). The core skills are summarised as OARS: Open questions, Affirmations, Reflections (reflective listening) and Summaries. In a reflection the counsellor works out what the client means and says it back as a statement, often stressing the side that favours change. The earlier principles were express empathy, develop discrepancy, roll with resistance and support self-efficacy. Example: a client says, 'I know drinking is hurting my marriage, but it is the only way I relax.' The counsellor reflects, 'Part of you is worried about your marriage, and drinking feels like your only way to unwind', which lets the client hear both sides of his own ambivalence. Option b is right because reflective listening (the R of OARS) is the most used MI skill, inherited from Carl Rogers's client-centred approach. Exam trap: MI is often paired with the stages of change of Prochaska and DiClemente, but that is a separate model; the MI technique asked about here is reflective listening, not exposure, confrontation or advice-giving.", "traps": {"a": "Free association is Sigmund Freud's 'fundamental rule' of psychoanalysis, in which the patient says whatever comes to mind so that unconscious material can surface; it has no place in MI.", "c": "Exposure therapy is a behavioural technique that confronts a person with feared cues until anxiety falls, used for phobias, PTSD and OCD; it does not address ambivalence about change.", "d": "Systematic desensitisation is Joseph Wolpe's (1958) method of pairing relaxation with a graded fear hierarchy for phobias; it is a behaviour therapy technique, not an MI skill."}}, {"q": "Which therapy is most effective for Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder (OCD)?", "concept": "The answer is b: cognitive behavioural therapy (CBT) with exposure and response prevention (ERP) is the most effective psychological treatment for obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD). In OCD, intrusive obsessions (for example, 'my hands are contaminated') raise anxiety, and compulsions such as washing or checking bring brief relief. That relief negatively reinforces the compulsion, so the person never learns that the feared outcome would not happen and that anxiety falls on its own. ERP was introduced by the British psychologist Victor Meyer in 1966 and later refined by Edna Foa and others. It has two parts: exposure, in which the client faces the feared cue step by step up a hierarchy, and response prevention, in which the client deliberately does not perform the ritual. Anxiety then falls through habituation and extinction, and the obsession loses its power. Cognitive work is often added to tackle beliefs such as inflated responsibility or the overimportance of thoughts. Example: a woman who washes her hands 40 times a day touches a door handle and then refrains from washing for an hour, rating her anxiety as it peaks and then drops; over weeks the urge weakens. Option b is right because clinical guidelines (for example, NICE in the United Kingdom) recommend CBT including ERP as the first-line psychological treatment for OCD, often alongside SSRIs. Exam trap: insight-oriented therapies sound attractive because obsessions seem 'deep', but OCD responds to behavioural exposure, not interpretation; this paper asks the same question again as Q43, with ERP named on its own.", "traps": {"a": "Psychoanalysis, Freud's insight therapy, explained obsessional neurosis through anal-stage conflicts (the Rat Man case, 1909), but it lacks controlled evidence for OCD and does not target the obsession and compulsion cycle.", "c": "Client-centred therapy is Carl Rogers's non-directive approach built on empathy, genuineness and unconditional positive regard; it is supportive but not an evidence-based treatment for OCD symptoms.", "d": "Gestalt therapy, from Fritz Perls, Laura Perls and Paul Goodman, builds here-and-now awareness with methods such as the empty chair; it has no established evidence for OCD."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT a key element of Dialectical Behavior Therapy (DBT)?", "concept": "The answer is c: dream analysis is not a key element of Dialectical Behaviour Therapy (DBT). DBT was developed by the American psychologist Marsha M. Linehan, whose treatment manuals appeared in 1993, for chronically suicidal women, most of whom had borderline personality disorder. Standard CBT's constant push for change felt invalidating to these clients, while pure acceptance left their self-harm untouched. The 'dialectic' is the synthesis of acceptance and change: the therapist fully validates the client and at the same time pushes firmly for new behaviour. The acceptance side comes from Zen practice, the change side from behaviour therapy. Linehan's biosocial theory sees borderline personality disorder as emotion dysregulation arising when a biologically sensitive child grows up in an invalidating environment. Standard DBT has four modes (individual therapy, a weekly skills group, telephone coaching and a therapist consultation team) and teaches four skills modules: core mindfulness, distress tolerance, emotion regulation and interpersonal effectiveness. Example: a client feeling an urge to cut herself uses a distress tolerance skill such as holding ice, then uses emotion regulation skills to name the feeling and act opposite to the urge. Option c is right because dream analysis is a psychoanalytic technique (Freud's 'royal road to the unconscious') and has no place in DBT's skills-based, present-focused model. Exam trap: in NOT questions all three wrong options are true DBT elements; students sometimes doubt 'mindfulness' because it sounds like ACT or MBCT, but core mindfulness is DBT's first module.", "traps": {"a": "Mindfulness is DBT's core module (observing without judgement and finding 'wise mind' between emotion mind and reasonable mind), drawn from Zen practice; because it is a key element, it cannot be the NOT answer.", "b": "Interpersonal effectiveness is one of the four DBT skills modules, teaching clients to ask for what they need and refuse requests while keeping relationships and self-respect; it is a genuine element, so it is wrong here.", "d": "Emotion regulation is a DBT skills module that teaches naming emotions, reducing vulnerability and acting opposite to unhelpful urges; as a true element it is not the answer to a NOT question."}}, {"q": "In which therapy approach is the \"miracle question\" commonly used?", "concept": "The answer is a: the 'miracle question' is a signature technique of Solution-Focused Brief Therapy (SFBT). SFBT was developed by Steve de Shazer and Insoo Kim Berg and their colleagues at the Brief Family Therapy Center in Milwaukee, founded in 1978; de Shazer set out the miracle question in his 1988 book Clues. SFBT deliberately does not dig into the causes or history of a problem. It assumes that clients already have resources and that small changes can snowball, so therapy is brief and builds on what the client wants and what already works. Its main tools are questions: the miracle question (picturing life without the problem), exception questions (times when the problem was absent or smaller), scaling questions ('on a scale of 0 to 10, where are you now?') and coping questions. Example: the therapist asks, 'Suppose tonight, while you are asleep, a miracle happens and the problem that brought you here is solved. When you wake up, what is the first thing you would notice that tells you things are different?' A client may answer, 'I would have breakfast with my family instead of skipping it', which becomes a concrete first goal. Option a is right because the miracle question was created within, and is identified with, solution-focused brief therapy. Exam trap: narrative therapy (Michael White and David Epston) is the other postmodern brief therapy; its signature is externalising the problem, not the miracle question, so do not swap the two.", "traps": {"b": "Cognitive therapy, Aaron Beck's approach, uses Socratic questioning and thought records to test automatic thoughts; it asks for evidence about beliefs, not for a picture of a problem-free future.", "c": "Existential therapy (Rollo May, Irvin Yalom, Viktor Frankl's logotherapy) helps clients face death, freedom, isolation and meaninglessness; it is a philosophical stance rather than a toolkit of solution questions.", "d": "Psychodynamic therapy explores unconscious conflict, defences and transference rooted in the past; SFBT explicitly avoids this problem-and-cause focus."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is a key component of trauma-focused CBT?", "concept": "The answer is a: gradual exposure to traumatic memories, through the trauma narrative, is a key component of trauma-focused cognitive behavioural therapy (TF-CBT). TF-CBT was developed by the child psychiatrist Judith A. Cohen with the psychologists Anthony P. Mannarino and Esther Deblinger, for children and adolescents (roughly 3 to 18 years) who have suffered abuse or other trauma, usually with a non-offending parent joining sessions. Its components are remembered by the acronym PRACTICE: Psychoeducation and parenting skills, Relaxation, Affect modulation, Cognitive coping, Trauma narrative and processing, In vivo mastery of trauma reminders, Conjoint child-parent sessions and Enhancing safety. Coping skills are taught first so that the child can tolerate distress; then the child gradually tells, writes or draws the story of the trauma in more and more detail. Repeated retelling in a safe setting reduces the fear attached to the memory and lets the therapist correct beliefs such as 'it was my fault'. Example: a nine-year-old who was sexually abused writes a 'book' about what happened, one chapter per session, and later reads it to her mother in a joint session. Option a is right because the trauma narrative is a structured, graded exposure to the traumatic memories and is the central goal of the model. Exam trap: option d, systematic desensitisation, sounds close because TF-CBT 'desensitises' memories, but systematic desensitisation is Joseph Wolpe's named phobia technique (relaxation plus a fear hierarchy), not a TF-CBT component.", "traps": {"b": "Free association is Freud's psychoanalytic method of saying whatever comes to mind so that unconscious material surfaces; TF-CBT is structured and directive, not free-associative.", "c": "Dream interpretation is a psychoanalytic technique (Freud, The Interpretation of Dreams, 1900); nightmares may be discussed in TF-CBT, but interpreting dreams is not one of its components.", "d": "Systematic desensitisation is Joseph Wolpe's (1958) phobia treatment that pairs relaxation with an imagined fear hierarchy; TF-CBT uses relaxation and graded exposure, but its named component is the trauma narrative, not Wolpe's procedure."}}, {"q": "Which therapy technique involves challenging cognitive distortions?", "concept": "The answer is c: cognitive restructuring is the technique that involves identifying and challenging cognitive distortions. The idea comes from the cognitive therapies of Albert Ellis (rational therapy from 1955) and especially Aaron T. Beck, who from the 1960s described automatic thoughts and the systematic errors of thinking behind depression, such as overgeneralisation and arbitrary inference. Cognitive restructuring treats a thought as a hypothesis to test rather than a fact. The client learns to catch the automatic thought, name the distortion, weigh the evidence for and against it, and build a more balanced alternative. Beck's therapists do this through collaborative empiricism and Socratic questioning ('What is the evidence? How else could you see it?'), and clients practise it with the dysfunctional thought record (1979), which lists the situation, the emotion rated 0 to 100, the automatic thought, a balanced response and the outcome. Behavioural experiments then test predictions in real life. Example: a student who gets a B thinks, 'I am stupid; I will never pass the course.' Restructuring asks what her other grades are and what she would tell a friend, leading to, 'One B in a hard course does not make me stupid; I can revise this topic.' Her low mood eases as the thought changes. Option c is right because challenging and replacing distorted thoughts is the very definition of cognitive restructuring. Exam trap: behavioural activation is also part of Beck's package for depression, so students pick it, but it changes behaviour (scheduling activity), not the content of thoughts.", "traps": {"a": "Behavioural activation, which grew from Peter Lewinsohn's reinforcement view of depression, schedules valued and pleasant activity to break withdrawal; it targets behaviour, not distorted thoughts.", "b": "Mindfulness meditation (brought into medicine by Jon Kabat-Zinn's MBSR in 1979) teaches non-judgemental awareness of thoughts without trying to change or dispute them, the opposite stance to restructuring.", "d": "Flooding is a behavioural exposure technique in which the client stays in the most feared situation at full intensity, with escape blocked, until anxiety extinguishes; it does not work on thoughts directly."}}, {"q": "Which therapy technique is based on the concept of \"unconditional positive regard\"?", "concept": "The answer is b: client-centred therapy is built on the concept of unconditional positive regard. It was developed by the American psychologist Carl Rogers (1902 to 1987), first as non-directive counselling in Counseling and Psychotherapy (1942) and then as client-centred therapy in his 1951 book of that name; it is now usually called person-centred therapy. Rogers held that every person has an actualising tendency, a drive to grow. Problems arise from conditions of worth: a child who is loved only when she meets others' standards learns to deny feelings that do not fit, creating incongruence between self-concept and experience. In a 1957 paper Rogers named the 'necessary and sufficient' conditions for change, three of which are the therapist's core conditions: congruence (genuineness), unconditional positive regard (warm acceptance of the client as a person, without judgement or conditions) and accurate empathic understanding. When the client feels accepted, the need to defend the self falls and he can own denied feelings. Example: a client admits he resents his ill mother. The therapist neither praises nor criticises but accepts him and reflects, 'You feel angry, and guilty about feeling angry', so he can explore the feeling rather than hide it. Option b is right because unconditional positive regard is one of Rogers's core conditions and the defining attitude of client-centred therapy. Exam trap: unconditional positive regard is an attitude, not a technique, and it does not mean approving of every behaviour; it means valuing the person, which separates it from praise or reinforcement in behaviour therapy.", "traps": {"a": "Psychoanalysis, Freud's method, relies on free association, interpretation of resistance and transference, and a neutral 'blank screen' analyst, not the warm acceptance of unconditional positive regard.", "c": "Behavioural therapy applies conditioning principles (reinforcement, exposure, modelling) to change specific behaviours; reinforcement there is contingent on behaviour, the opposite of unconditional regard.", "d": "Gestalt therapy, from Fritz Perls, is an active, often confrontational here-and-now therapy using the empty chair and awareness exercises; it is humanistic but not built around unconditional positive regard."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is an essential component of Mindfulness-Based Cognitive Therapy (MBCT)?", "concept": "The answer is b: enhancing self-awareness and acceptance is an essential component of Mindfulness-Based Cognitive Therapy (MBCT). MBCT was developed by Zindel V. Segal, J. Mark G. Williams and John D. Teasdale and set out in their 2002 book. They combined Jon Kabat-Zinn's mindfulness-based stress reduction (MBSR, begun in 1979) with ideas from cognitive therapy, and designed MBCT as an eight-week group course for people who have recovered from recurrent major depression, to prevent relapse. The key insight is that relapse is triggered when a small dip in mood reactivates old patterns of negative, ruminative thinking. Rather than arguing with those thoughts, MBCT teaches decentering: noticing thoughts as passing mental events, not facts, and accepting feelings without being drawn into them. Practices include the body scan, sitting meditation and the three-minute breathing space. In a trial by Teasdale and colleagues (2000), patients with three or more past episodes who received MBCT relapsed far less often than those given usual care. Example: a recovered patient wakes up feeling low and notices 'here is the thought that I am a failure again'; she takes a breathing space and lets the thought pass instead of ruminating for hours. Option b is right because awareness of one's present experience and acceptance of it, the stance of decentering, is the core of MBCT. Exam trap: option a, challenging automatic thoughts, is the method of Beck's standard cognitive therapy; although MBCT has 'cognitive' in its name, it changes the relationship to thoughts, not their content.", "traps": {"a": "Challenging automatic thoughts is the core of Aaron Beck's cognitive therapy (second wave), which tests and disputes the content of thoughts; MBCT deliberately does not dispute content but observes thoughts with acceptance.", "c": "Encouraging free association is Freud's psychoanalytic method for reaching unconscious material; MBCT works with present-moment awareness, not free association.", "d": "Interpreting unconscious conflicts is the work of psychoanalytic and psychodynamic therapy; MBCT makes no interpretations and does not assume hidden conflicts."}}, {"q": "Which therapeutic approach was developed by Fritz Perls?", "concept": "The answer is a: Fritz Perls developed Gestalt therapy. Frederick Salomon 'Fritz' Perls (1893 to 1970), a German-born psychiatrist trained in psychoanalysis, created Gestalt therapy with his wife Laura Perls and the writer Paul Goodman; their book Gestalt Therapy appeared in 1951, and Perls later made the approach famous through workshops at the Esalen Institute in California. Gestalt therapy is an experiential, humanistic, here-and-now therapy. Its central aim is awareness: helping the client notice what he is feeling, thinking and doing in the present moment, take responsibility for it ('I' language rather than 'it' or 'you') and complete unfinished business, unexpressed feelings from the past that still intrude. Perls described the 'topdog' (the demanding 'you should' voice) and the 'underdog' (the excuse-making voice). Signature techniques include the empty chair, the hot seat in group workshops, and dream work in which the client speaks as each part of the dream, treating it as a disowned part of the self. Example: a woman who never said goodbye to her late father is asked to imagine him in an empty chair and tell him what she wishes she had said; her grief, held back for years, is finally expressed. Option a is right because Perls is the recognised founder of Gestalt therapy. Exam trap: Gestalt therapy is not Gestalt psychology, the perception school of Max Wertheimer, Wolfgang Köhler and Kurt Koffka; Perls borrowed the word 'Gestalt' (whole), but the perception school rejected his use of its terms.", "traps": {"b": "Cognitive behavioural therapy grew from Aaron Beck's cognitive therapy and Albert Ellis's work combined with behaviour therapy; it is not associated with Perls.", "c": "Rational emotive behaviour therapy was founded by Albert Ellis in 1955 (as rational therapy), built on the ABC model and on disputing irrational 'musts'.", "d": "Dialectical behaviour therapy was developed by Marsha Linehan (manuals 1993) for chronically suicidal clients with borderline personality disorder."}}, {"q": "In cognitive therapy, which cognitive distortion involves interpreting ambiguous situations negatively?", "concept": "The answer is d: the official key gives selective abstraction as the distortion in which ambiguous situations are read negatively. Aaron T. Beck, who set out the cognitive model of depression in Depression (1967) and its treatment in Cognitive Therapy of Depression (1979, with colleagues), named a set of systematic errors in thinking: arbitrary inference, selective abstraction, overgeneralisation, magnification and minimisation, personalisation and dichotomous thinking. David Burns later popularised a longer list in Feeling Good (1980). Selective abstraction, also called the mental filter, means focusing on one negative detail taken out of context while ignoring the rest of the situation, so that the whole experience is judged by that one detail. In an ambiguous situation that holds both good and bad cues, the person filters out the positive and builds a negative conclusion. Example: a teacher gives a lecture that most students enjoy, but she notices two students yawning and concludes, 'My class finds me boring.' Option d is right because, of the four choices, selective abstraction is the only distortion about how a mixed or unclear situation is processed, by attending only to its negative part. Exam trap: catastrophising (expecting the worst), overgeneralisation (a broad rule from one event) and personalisation (blaming oneself for outside events) all sound negative, so learn each by its exact mechanism rather than by its mood. Key note: strictly, 'interpreting ambiguous situations negatively' without evidence fits Beck's arbitrary inference (jumping to conclusions, including mind reading), which is not offered; selective abstraction is the best of the four options, and the official key stands.", "traps": {"a": "Catastrophising (in Burns's list, close to Beck's magnification) is expecting or blowing up the worst possible outcome, as in 'If I fail this exam, my life is over'; it is about predicted consequences, not how an ambiguous situation is filtered.", "b": "Overgeneralisation, one of Beck's original distortions, draws a sweeping rule from one or a few events ('I failed statistics, so I fail at everything'); the stem is not about generalising from a single event.", "c": "Personalisation, also Beck's, is taking responsibility or blame for outside events without a basis ('The party was dull because of me'); it concerns self-blame, not a negative reading of an ambiguous scene."}}, {"q": "Which technique is NOT commonly used in psychoanalytic therapy?", "concept": "The answer is c: cognitive restructuring is not a technique of psychoanalytic therapy. Psychoanalysis was developed by the Viennese neurologist Sigmund Freud (1856 to 1939) from the 1890s, after he dropped hypnosis and the cathartic method he had shared with Josef Breuer. Its goal is insight: making unconscious conflicts conscious so that the ego can deal with them. The analyst works with a few classic tools. Free association, Freud's 'fundamental rule', asks the patient to say whatever comes to mind without censoring it. Dream analysis treats dreams as disguised wish fulfilments, decoding the manifest content to reach the latent content (The Interpretation of Dreams, 1900). Transference interpretation shows the patient how he relives feelings toward early figures, such as a parent, in the relationship with the analyst; interpreting resistance is another core tool. Cognitive restructuring, by contrast, comes from the cognitive therapies of Aaron Beck and Albert Ellis: identifying conscious automatic thoughts and distortions, testing them against evidence and replacing them with balanced thoughts. It assumes the problem lies in present conscious thinking, not in buried conflict. Example: a patient who keeps feeling that his analyst is about to reject him is helped to see that this fear echoes his experience with a cold father (transference), rather than being asked to fill in a thought record. Option c is right because cognitive restructuring belongs to cognitive and cognitive behavioural therapy, not psychoanalysis. Exam trap: in NOT questions, three options are genuine psychoanalytic tools; pick the one from a different school, here the CBT technique.", "traps": {"a": "Free association, Freud's 'fundamental rule', is the basic method of psychoanalysis, so it is commonly used and cannot be the answer.", "b": "Dream analysis, which Freud called the 'royal road' to the unconscious, is a core psychoanalytic technique, so it is not the exception.", "d": "Transference interpretation, showing how the patient re-enacts early relationships with the analyst, is central to psychoanalytic therapy, so it does not fit a NOT question."}}, {"q": "In behavioral therapy, systematic desensitization is used primarily to treat:", "concept": "The answer is b: systematic desensitisation is used primarily to treat phobias. The method was developed by the South African psychiatrist Joseph Wolpe (1915 to 1997) and set out in Psychotherapy by Reciprocal Inhibition (1958). It grew from Mary Cover Jones's 1924 treatment of Peter, a boy afraid of rabbits, and from Wolpe's own experiments with cats made anxious in a cage. The principle is reciprocal inhibition: a person cannot be deeply relaxed and highly anxious at the same time, so if relaxation is paired with a feared stimulus, the relaxation response gradually replaces the fear (counterconditioning). Treatment has three steps. First the client learns deep muscle relaxation, a shortened form of Edmund Jacobson's progressive muscle relaxation. Second, client and therapist build an anxiety hierarchy, ranking feared situations from least to most frightening using subjective units of disturbance (SUDS, 0 to 100). Third, the relaxed client imagines each scene in turn, moving up only when a step no longer causes anxiety. Example: a person with a fear of flying first imagines seeing a plane on television, then booking a ticket, then sitting at the gate, and finally take-off, staying relaxed at each step. Option b is right because systematic desensitisation was designed for, and works best with, specific phobias and other clear, cue-bound fears. Exam trap: it rests on reciprocal inhibition (classical counterconditioning), not on operant extinction or insight, and flooding differs because it presents the most feared stimulus at once, without a hierarchy or relaxation.", "traps": {"a": "Depression is treated with cognitive therapy, behavioural activation, interpersonal therapy and medication; it is not a cue-bound fear, so a fear hierarchy does not fit it.", "c": "Schizophrenia is treated mainly with antipsychotic medication plus psychosocial work such as family psychoeducation, social skills training and CBT for psychosis; systematic desensitisation is not a treatment for it.", "d": "Bipolar disorder is treated with mood stabilisers such as lithium, plus psychoeducation and structured psychotherapies; it is a mood disorder, not a phobic condition."}}, {"q": "Which of the following therapies is most effective for treating PTSD?", "concept": "The answer is b: trauma-focused cognitive behavioural therapy (TF-CBT) is the most effective of the listed therapies for post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD). PTSD follows exposure to actual or threatened death, serious injury or sexual violence and shows as intrusive memories and nightmares, avoidance of reminders, negative changes in thinking and mood, and hyperarousal. Trauma-focused CBT is a family of treatments that work directly on the trauma memory and its meaning. Prolonged exposure, developed by Edna B. Foa, combines imaginal exposure (repeatedly retelling the trauma) with in vivo exposure to safe but avoided reminders. Cognitive processing therapy, developed by Patricia Resick for rape survivors, challenges 'stuck points' about blame, safety and trust. For children, the TF-CBT model of Judith Cohen, Anthony Mannarino and Esther Deblinger teaches coping skills and then builds a trauma narrative. Along with eye movement desensitisation and reprocessing (EMDR), from Francine Shapiro, these are the first-line psychological treatments recommended in guidelines such as NICE's. Example: a survivor of a road accident who avoids driving retells the crash in detail in sessions, examines the belief 'it was all my fault', and then drives short distances in steps until the fear fades. Option b is right because trauma-focused CBT has the strongest evidence base for PTSD in both adults and children. Exam trap: the acronym TF-CBT strictly names the Cohen, Mannarino and Deblinger model for children, but in exam options 'trauma-focused CBT' stands for the whole group of trauma-focused cognitive behavioural treatments; supportive humanistic or existential work is not first line.", "traps": {"a": "Psychoanalysis seeks insight into unconscious conflict through free association and interpretation; it has little controlled evidence for PTSD and does not systematically process the trauma memory.", "c": "Humanistic therapy, such as Rogers's person-centred approach, offers empathy and acceptance; it can support a survivor but is not an evidence-based first-line treatment for PTSD symptoms.", "d": "Existential therapy (May, Yalom, Frankl) helps people face meaning, death and freedom, questions that may follow trauma, but it is not a trauma-focused treatment with strong PTSD evidence."}}, {"q": "What is the main focus of interpersonal therapy (IPT)?", "concept": "The answer is b: the official key gives improving social and communication skills as the main focus of interpersonal therapy (IPT). IPT was developed by the American psychiatrist Gerald Klerman (1928 to 1992) and the epidemiologist Myrna Weissman, his wife, and published in Interpersonal Psychotherapy of Depression (1984). It draws on Adolf Meyer, Harry Stack Sullivan and John Bowlby, and holds that depression both follows and worsens problems in current relationships. IPT is brief (usually 12 to 16 weekly sessions) and present-focused; it treats depression as a medical illness, does not interpret transference and does not dig into childhood. After an interpersonal inventory, therapist and patient choose one or two of four problem areas: grief, interpersonal role disputes, role transitions (such as a new job or retirement) and interpersonal deficits (isolation or poor social skills). Techniques include communication analysis, role-play and encouraging the expression of feelings, all aimed at improving how the patient handles relationships so that mood lifts. Example: a woman who became depressed after moving to a new city for her husband's job (a role transition) role-plays telling him how lonely she feels and plans ways to make friends. Option b is right because, of the four choices, it is the only one about current interpersonal functioning, which is what IPT works on. Exam trap: IPT grew from Sullivan's interpersonal theory but is not psychodynamic, so unconscious conflicts and past trauma are wrong. Key note: IPT's precise focus is resolving current interpersonal problems in the four areas and linking them to mood; skills training is mainly a tactic for interpersonal deficits, so b is the closest wording rather than a perfect definition, and the official key stands.", "traps": {"a": "Resolving unconscious conflicts is the aim of psychoanalysis and psychodynamic therapy; IPT, though rooted in Sullivan's interpersonal theory, deliberately avoids interpreting the unconscious or the transference.", "c": "Exploring past trauma belongs to psychodynamic and trauma-focused therapies; IPT stays with current relationships and present problems, using the past only as background.", "d": "Developing mindfulness skills is central to third-wave therapies such as MBCT, DBT and ACT; it is not part of IPT's interpersonal focus."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is a core skill taught in Dialectical Behavior Therapy (DBT)?", "concept": "The answer is a: mindfulness is a core skill taught in Dialectical Behaviour Therapy (DBT). DBT was developed by Marsha M. Linehan (manuals published in 1993) for chronically suicidal clients with borderline personality disorder. Linehan combined behaviour therapy's techniques for change with acceptance practices drawn from Zen, which she practised, and with a dialectical stance that holds acceptance and change together. In the weekly skills group, clients learn four modules. Core mindfulness comes first and runs through all the others: observing, describing and participating in experience without judgement, one thing at a time, and finding 'wise mind', the balance between 'emotion mind' and 'reasonable mind'. Distress tolerance helps clients survive crises without making them worse (distraction, self-soothing, radical acceptance). Emotion regulation teaches naming emotions, reducing vulnerability and 'opposite action'. Interpersonal effectiveness teaches asking for what one needs and saying no while keeping relationships and self-respect. Linehan's trials (1991 and 2006) found fewer suicidal acts with DBT than with comparison treatments. Example: a client flooded with rage after a text from her partner pauses, observes the sensations in her body and labels 'he is abandoning me' as a thought, which gives her time to choose a skill instead of self-harming. Option a is right because mindfulness is named as the core module of DBT's skills training. Exam trap: mindfulness also appears in ACT, MBSR and MBCT, so students sometimes reject it as 'not DBT'; it is in fact DBT's foundation skill, while free association, dream analysis and aversion therapy belong to other schools.", "traps": {"b": "Free association is Freud's psychoanalytic 'fundamental rule' for reaching the unconscious; DBT is structured and skills-based, not free-associative.", "c": "Dream analysis is a psychoanalytic and Jungian technique for interpreting unconscious content; it is not one of DBT's four skills modules.", "d": "Aversion therapy is a behavioural technique that pairs an unwanted behaviour (such as drinking) with an unpleasant stimulus (such as a nausea-producing drug); it is not part of DBT's skills training."}}, {"q": "The primary goal of Rational Emotive Behavior Therapy (REBT) is to:", "concept": "The answer is a: the primary goal of Rational Emotive Behaviour Therapy (REBT) is to modify irrational beliefs. REBT was founded by the American psychologist Albert Ellis (1913 to 2007), who left psychoanalysis because insight rarely helped his patients. Drawing on Stoic philosophy (Epictetus taught that people are disturbed not by things but by the views they take of them), he began rational therapy in 1955; it became rational-emotive therapy and, in 1993, REBT. It is usually called the first cognitive behavioural therapy. Its core is the ABC model: A, the activating event; B, the person's beliefs about it; C, the emotional and behavioural consequences. A does not cause C directly; B does. Irrational beliefs are rigid demands ('musts', which Ellis mocked as 'musturbation') plus awfulising, low frustration tolerance and global rating of oneself or others. Therapy adds D, disputing the beliefs by asking for logical, empirical and practical evidence, leading to E, an effective new philosophy. Homework, such as shame-attacking exercises, puts the new beliefs into action. Example: a man is rejected for a job (A), believes 'I must succeed, or I am worthless' (B) and becomes depressed (C). The therapist disputes: 'Where is the evidence that one rejection makes you worthless?' He moves to 'I would prefer to get the job, but I can cope and keep trying' (E) and feels disappointed rather than depressed. Option a is right because changing irrational beliefs into rational preferences is the explicit aim of REBT. Exam trap: in Ellis's ABC, B is beliefs and C is consequences; do not confuse it with the behavioural ABC (antecedent, behaviour, consequence) of functional analysis.", "traps": {"b": "Analysing childhood experiences is the method of psychoanalysis, which Ellis abandoned; REBT focuses on present beliefs, not early history.", "c": "Encouraging catharsis, the release of pent-up emotion, goes back to Breuer and Freud's cathartic method in Studies on Hysteria (1895) and is used in psychodrama; Ellis held that venting alone does not change the beliefs that create disturbance.", "d": "Dream interpretation is a Freudian and Jungian technique for reaching unconscious content; REBT works with conscious beliefs and makes no use of dreams."}}, {"q": "Which therapy is most effective in treating Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder (OCD)?", "concept": "The answer is b: exposure and response prevention (ERP) is the most effective psychological treatment for obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD). OCD is driven by a vicious cycle: an obsession (an intrusive, unwanted thought, image or urge, such as 'I may have left the gas on') raises anxiety, and a compulsion (checking, washing, counting, seeking reassurance) brings quick relief. Because the relief negatively reinforces the ritual, the person never discovers that the feared disaster would not happen and that anxiety falls by itself. ERP, introduced by the British psychologist Victor Meyer in 1966 and developed further by Edna Foa and colleagues, breaks the cycle in two ways. Exposure means deliberately and repeatedly facing the feared cue, in real life or in imagination, working up a graded hierarchy. Response prevention means not carrying out the ritual, including hidden mental rituals and reassurance seeking. Anxiety rises, peaks and then falls through habituation and extinction, and the person learns that the feared outcome does not follow. Unlike Wolpe's systematic desensitisation, ERP needs no relaxation training. Example: a man who checks the stove 20 times before leaving home switches it off once, walks out and does not return, staying with the anxiety until it fades; within weeks his checking drops sharply. Option b is right because ERP, delivered on its own or as the core of CBT, is the first-line psychological treatment for OCD in guidelines such as NICE's, often combined with an SSRI. Exam trap: this is the paper's repeat of Q28; whether the option says 'ERP' or 'CBT with ERP', choose it over insight-based or humanistic therapies, which lack controlled evidence for OCD.", "traps": {"a": "Psychoanalysis treats obsessional neurosis as a defence against unconscious anal-stage conflicts (Freud's Rat Man case, 1909); it does not break the obsession and compulsion cycle and lacks controlled evidence for OCD.", "c": "Gestalt therapy, from Fritz Perls, Laura Perls and Paul Goodman, works on here-and-now awareness and unfinished business through methods like the empty chair; it is not an evidence-based OCD treatment.", "d": "Humanistic therapy, such as Carl Rogers's person-centred approach, offers empathy and acceptance to support growth; it does not target compulsions, so it is not the most effective treatment for OCD."}}, {"q": "Cognitive distortions are a key focus in which therapeutic approach?", "concept": "The answer is a: cognitive distortions are a key focus of cognitive behavioural therapy (CBT). The concept comes from the American psychiatrist Aaron T. Beck (1921 to 2021), who in the 1960s noticed that his depressed patients had quick, believable, negative automatic thoughts that came before their feelings. Beck described the cognitive triad (negative views of the self, the world and the future) and the systematic errors in thinking that keep it going: arbitrary inference, selective abstraction, overgeneralisation, magnification and minimisation, personalisation and dichotomous (all-or-nothing) thinking. David Burns added further names such as mind reading, fortune telling, emotional reasoning and should statements in Feeling Good (1980). In CBT the therapist and client treat these thoughts as hypotheses: they identify the distortion, examine the evidence through Socratic questioning and thought records, and test predictions with behavioural experiments, so that emotion and behaviour change as thinking becomes more balanced. Albert Ellis's REBT shares this focus on faulty beliefs. Example: a man whose friend does not return a call concludes, 'He is angry with me' (arbitrary inference). In CBT he lists other explanations (the friend is busy, or his phone is off), then texts the friend and learns he was travelling. Option a is right because identifying and correcting cognitive distortions is the defining work of cognitive and cognitive behavioural therapy. Exam trap: client-centred and existential therapies also care about the client's view of the world, but neither labels or challenges thinking errors; that is specific to CBT.", "traps": {"b": "Existential therapy (Rollo May, Irvin Yalom, Viktor Frankl) helps clients confront death, freedom, isolation and meaninglessness; it explores meaning rather than correcting thinking errors.", "c": "Psychodynamic therapy focuses on unconscious conflict, defences and transference rooted in early relationships, not on conscious distorted thoughts.", "d": "Client-centred therapy (Carl Rogers) provides empathy, genuineness and unconditional positive regard so that the client can grow; it is non-directive and does not challenge the client's thoughts."}}, {"q": "In family therapy, the term \"triangulation\" refers to:", "concept": "The answer is b: in family therapy, triangulation means a third party, often a child, being drawn into a conflict between two others. The term comes mainly from the American psychiatrist Murray Bowen (1913 to 1990), founder of the transgenerational family systems school, whose master concept is differentiation of self. Bowen saw the triangle as the smallest stable relationship system: when tension rises between two people, such as a husband and wife, one or both pull in a third person to lower their own anxiety, for example by confiding in a child, uniting against a 'problem child', or using the child as a messenger. The two-person conflict is then detoured rather than resolved, and the child may become symptomatic. Salvador Minuchin's structural family therapy (Families and Family Therapy, 1974) listed triangulation among the rigid triads, along with detouring and stable coalitions, which cross the boundary between the parental and child subsystems. Treatment aims to detriangulate: Bowen worked as a calm coach who stayed out of the family's triangles and helped members deal with each other directly, while Minuchin strengthened the parental subsystem. Example: parents who no longer speak to each other each complain to their teenage daughter about the other, and she develops headaches and stops going to school. Option b is right because triangulation is defined by a third person being pulled into a two-person conflict. Exam trap: triangulation in qualitative research (Norman Denzin's use of several data sources, methods, investigators or theories to cross-check findings) is a completely different concept with the same name, and this paper asks about it separately.", "traps": {"a": "Strengthening communication between two family members is a goal of therapy, close to what detriangulation achieves; triangulation is the opposite, bypassing direct communication by pulling in a third person.", "c": "Establishing clear boundaries is Minuchin's structural goal, between diffuse boundaries (enmeshment) and rigid boundaries (disengagement); triangulation is a boundary violation, not boundary-setting.", "d": "Reducing stress through role-playing describes action methods such as Jacob Moreno's psychodrama; it has nothing to do with the meaning of triangulation."}}, {"q": "Which of the following therapies is best suited for treating Borderline Personality Disorder (BPD)?", "concept": "The answer is b: Dialectical Behaviour Therapy (DBT) is the therapy best suited to borderline personality disorder (BPD). Marsha M. Linehan developed DBT, with treatment manuals in 1993, for chronically suicidal women, most of whom met criteria for BPD, a disorder marked by unstable relationships, identity disturbance, impulsivity, intense anger, chronic emptiness and recurrent self-harm. Linehan's biosocial theory holds that BPD arises when a child with a biological emotional vulnerability (high sensitivity, strong reactions and a slow return to baseline) grows up in an invalidating environment that dismisses or punishes emotions. DBT therefore balances acceptance (validation and mindfulness, drawn from Zen) with change (behaviour therapy). It runs in four modes: individual therapy, a weekly skills group, telephone coaching for crises and a consultation team for therapists. Skills cover mindfulness, distress tolerance, emotion regulation and interpersonal effectiveness, and individual sessions target life-threatening behaviour first. In Linehan's 1991 trial, women given a year of DBT made fewer and less medically serious suicidal acts and spent fewer days in hospital than those given usual treatment. Example: a client who cuts herself after arguments learns to rate the urge, use distress tolerance skills and phone her therapist for coaching before acting. Option b is right because DBT was designed for BPD and has the earliest and strongest controlled evidence in it. Exam trap: other therapies with evidence for BPD exist (Anthony Bateman and Peter Fonagy's mentalisation-based treatment, Jeffrey Young's schema therapy, Otto Kernberg's transference-focused psychotherapy), but none is offered here, and plain cognitive therapy or classical psychoanalysis is not the specialist choice.", "traps": {"a": "Cognitive therapy (Aaron Beck) is first line for depression and anxiety; its steady push for change felt invalidating to many clients with BPD, which is why Linehan built DBT.", "c": "Classical psychoanalysis, with free association on the couch, is not recommended for BPD; specialised psychodynamic adaptations such as transference-focused psychotherapy exist, but plain psychoanalysis is not the best-suited option.", "d": "Behavioural activation (from Lewinsohn's work, later Jacobson and colleagues) is an evidence-based treatment for depression that schedules rewarding activity; it does not address the emotion dysregulation and self-harm of BPD."}}, {"q": "Which therapeutic approach was developed by Aaron Beck?", "concept": "The answer is b: Aaron Beck developed cognitive therapy. Aaron Temkin Beck (1921 to 2021) was a psychoanalytically trained American psychiatrist at the University of Pennsylvania. In the late 1950s and 1960s he set out to test Freud's idea that depression is anger turned inward, but found that his depressed patients' dreams and thoughts were full of loss and defeat instead. He also noticed a stream of quick, believable, negative automatic thoughts that came before their feelings. He set out the model in Depression (1967) and the treatment in Cognitive Therapy of Depression (1979, with colleagues). The model has three levels: core beliefs or schemas formed early in life, intermediate rules and assumptions, and automatic thoughts. Depression shows the cognitive triad (negative views of the self, the world and the future), kept going by cognitive distortions such as arbitrary inference and overgeneralisation. Therapy is brief, structured and collaborative: through collaborative empiricism, Socratic questioning, thought records and behavioural experiments, the client learns to test and correct distorted thinking. Beck also built widely used scales such as the Beck Depression Inventory (1961), and his daughter Judith S. Beck continues the work. Example: a depressed student thinks, 'I will never make friends'; therapist and client treat this as a hypothesis, list counter-evidence, plan to join one club and then review what happened. Option b is right because Beck is the founder of cognitive therapy. Exam trap: Albert Ellis's REBT (1955) came first and shares the aim of changing thinking, so students mix the two; the cognitive triad and the thought record are Beck's, while the ABC model and disputing are Ellis's.", "traps": {"a": "Gestalt therapy was developed by Fritz Perls with Laura Perls and Paul Goodman (Gestalt Therapy, 1951), a here-and-now experiential therapy known for the empty chair.", "c": "Existential therapy has no single founder; it is linked with Rollo May, Irvin Yalom (Existential Psychotherapy, 1980) and Viktor Frankl's logotherapy, and focuses on meaning, freedom and death.", "d": "Narrative therapy was developed by Michael White and David Epston (Narrative Means to Therapeutic Ends, 1990) and is known for externalising the problem and re-authoring life stories."}}, {"q": "The \"empty chair\" technique is commonly used in:", "concept": "The answer is b: the empty chair technique is commonly used in Gestalt therapy. Gestalt therapy was developed by Fritz Perls (1893 to 1970) with Laura Perls and Paul Goodman (Gestalt Therapy, 1951). It is an experiential, here-and-now therapy that aims to increase awareness and help clients finish unfinished business, feelings from past relationships that were never expressed and still shape present life. In the empty chair technique, the client imagines a person (often a parent, a former partner or someone who has died) or a disowned part of the self sitting in an empty chair and speaks to it directly, in the present tense. The client may then switch chairs and answer as the other person or part. This turns talk about feelings into direct experience of them, so that anger, grief or longing can be expressed and integrated. A related form is the two-chair dialogue between the 'topdog' (the critical 'you should' voice) and the 'underdog' (the apologetic voice that wins by stalling). Example: a man who never told his late father how hurt he felt by his criticism speaks to the empty chair, then moves into it and answers as his father; he ends the session tearful but relieved. Option b is right because the empty chair is the best-known signature technique of Gestalt therapy. Exam trap: emotion-focused therapy (Leslie Greenberg) and psychodrama (Jacob Moreno) also use chair work, but in exam options the empty chair points to Gestalt therapy, never to REBT, psychoanalysis or behaviour therapy.", "traps": {"a": "Rational emotive behaviour therapy (Albert Ellis) disputes irrational beliefs through the ABC model and homework; it is didactic and argumentative, not built on chair dialogues.", "c": "Psychoanalysis uses free association, dream analysis and transference interpretation with a largely silent analyst; it does not ask the patient to address an imagined person in a chair.", "d": "Behavioural therapy uses conditioning methods such as exposure, reinforcement and modelling; role-play appears in assertiveness training, but the empty chair is not a behavioural technique."}}, {"q": "A key characteristic of Client-Centered Therapy is:", "concept": "The answer is a: a key characteristic of client-centred therapy is the therapist providing unconditional positive regard. Client-centred (now person-centred) therapy was developed by Carl Rogers (1902 to 1987) in the 1940s and 1950s, with Client-Centered Therapy published in 1951. Rogers believed that people have an actualising tendency, an inborn drive toward growth, and that distress comes from incongruence: a gap between the self-concept and actual experience, created when love was given only under conditions of worth. The therapist does not diagnose, interpret or advise. Instead, as Rogers argued in his 1957 paper on the necessary and sufficient conditions of therapeutic change, the therapist offers three core conditions: congruence or genuineness, unconditional positive regard (warm, non-judgemental acceptance of the client as a person) and accurate empathic understanding, communicated through reflection of feelings. In that climate the client stops defending the self, owns denied feelings and moves toward becoming a 'fully functioning person'. Example: a student confesses that she has failed her exams and lied to her parents about it. The therapist conveys acceptance of her as a person and reflects, 'You feel ashamed and trapped between telling the truth and keeping their approval', and she begins to explore what she really wants. Option a is right because unconditional positive regard is one of Rogers's core conditions and defines the client-centred relationship. Exam trap: the other three options each name a different school (psychoanalysis, dream interpretation, CBT); in Rogers's approach the client, not the therapist, leads, and the therapist never interprets or disputes.", "traps": {"b": "A focus on past unconscious conflicts is the hallmark of psychoanalysis and psychodynamic therapy; Rogers worked with present, conscious experience in the here and now.", "c": "Direct interpretation of dreams belongs to Freud's psychoanalysis (and Jung's analytical psychology); a client-centred therapist does not interpret at all.", "d": "Challenging cognitive distortions is the method of Beck's cognitive therapy and CBT; Rogers's non-directive approach accepts rather than disputes the client's view."}}, {"q": "Which therapy focuses on helping clients find meaning in life despite suffering?", "concept": "The answer is a: existential therapy focuses on helping clients find meaning in life despite suffering. Existential therapy is less a set of techniques than a philosophical view of human life, drawing on Søren Kierkegaard, Martin Heidegger (Being and Time, 1927) and Jean-Paul Sartre. It sees people as free and responsible for creating their own meaning. Irvin Yalom, in Existential Psychotherapy (1980), named four ultimate concerns: death, freedom, isolation and meaninglessness; anxiety about them is normal, and problems arise when people flee it into inauthentic living. Rollo May brought these ideas into American psychology. The form most tied to meaning is logotherapy, founded by the Viennese psychiatrist Viktor Frankl (1905 to 1997), who survived the Nazi concentration camps and described the experience in Man's Search for Meaning. Frankl held that the will to meaning is the primary human motive; when it is frustrated, the result is an existential vacuum or a noogenic neurosis. Meaning can be found by creating a work, by experiencing love or beauty, and through the attitude taken toward unavoidable suffering. Example: Frankl described an elderly doctor who could not get over his wife's death. Frankl asked what would have happened if he had died first; the man said she would have suffered terribly, and Frankl pointed out that by surviving her he had spared her that pain. The suffering gained a meaning and became bearable. Option a is right because the search for meaning in the face of suffering is the defining theme of existential therapy and logotherapy. Exam trap: humanistic therapies (Rogers) also value growth, but 'meaning despite suffering' is the specific signature of Frankl and the existential school.", "traps": {"b": "Behavioural therapy changes observable behaviour through conditioning and learning principles such as exposure and reinforcement; it does not address questions of life meaning.", "c": "Psychoanalysis, Freud's method, makes unconscious conflicts conscious through free association and interpretation; Frankl set his will to meaning against Freud's pleasure principle.", "d": "Cognitive therapy (Aaron Beck) identifies and corrects distorted automatic thoughts to relieve depression and anxiety; it targets thinking errors, not the search for meaning."}}, {"q": "In Behavior Therapy, reinforcement is used to:", "concept": "The answer is a: in behaviour therapy, reinforcement is used to encourage desired behaviours. Behaviour therapy treats problems as learned habits that can be relearned, and reinforcement is its main tool for building behaviour. The idea comes from Edward Thorndike's law of effect (responses followed by satisfying consequences are 'stamped in') and from B. F. Skinner's operant conditioning, set out in The Behavior of Organisms (1938). A reinforcer is any consequence that makes the behaviour it follows more likely. Positive reinforcement adds something pleasant (praise, a token, a privilege); negative reinforcement removes something unpleasant (a nagging parent stops once homework starts). Both increase behaviour; punishment, by contrast, decreases it. In therapy the reinforcer is given immediately after the target behaviour, every time while the behaviour is new, then intermittently so that it lasts. Techniques built on it include shaping (reinforcing successive approximations), the Premack principle and the token economy of Teodoro Ayllon and Nathan Azrin (1968). Example: a child with autism earns a sticker each time he makes eye contact, and later only on some occasions, until eye contact becomes part of his everyday behaviour. Option a is right because reinforcement exists to increase the frequency of a chosen, desirable behaviour. Exam trap: negative reinforcement is not punishment; it also strengthens behaviour, by taking away something aversive, and the other three options describe psychodynamic and cognitive goals, not behavioural ones.", "traps": {"b": "Identifying unconscious motives is the aim of psychoanalysis and psychodynamic therapy (Sigmund Freud); behaviour therapy deliberately sets aside unobservable inner motives and works on observable behaviour and its consequences.", "c": "Modifying cognitive distortions is the work of Aaron Beck's cognitive therapy or Albert Ellis's rational emotive behaviour therapy; it changes thoughts, whereas reinforcement changes behaviour through its consequences.", "d": "Developing insight into past trauma is a psychodynamic goal; behaviour therapy focuses on present behaviour and its consequences and does not need the client to understand the past for change to happen."}}, {"q": "Which therapy technique involves gradual exposure to a feared stimulus while practicing relaxation?", "concept": "The answer is a: systematic desensitisation is the technique of gradual exposure to a feared stimulus while practising relaxation. It was developed by the South African psychiatrist Joseph Wolpe and set out in Psychotherapy by Reciprocal Inhibition (1958). Its forerunner was Mary Cover Jones's 1924 treatment of Peter, a small boy afraid of rabbits, who was fed while a rabbit was brought a little closer each day. Wolpe's principle, reciprocal inhibition, holds that two incompatible responses cannot occur together, so a deeply relaxed body cannot also be anxious. Treatment has three steps. First, the client learns Edmund Jacobson's progressive muscle relaxation. Second, client and therapist build an anxiety hierarchy of 10 to 20 situations, each rated on Wolpe's subjective units of distress scale (SUDS, 0 to 100). Third, while relaxed, the client imagines (or meets) the lowest item and moves up only when it no longer causes anxiety. Example: a woman afraid of dogs begins by picturing a photograph of a puppy and, many sessions later, pats a calm dog on a lead without distress. Option a is right because the stem names both defining ingredients, graded exposure and relaxation, which together make systematic desensitisation. Exam trap: flooding also uses exposure, but it starts at the top of the hierarchy with no relaxation and no gradual steps; 'gradual plus relaxation' always means systematic desensitisation.", "traps": {"b": "Free association is Sigmund Freud's 'fundamental rule' of psychoanalysis, saying whatever comes to mind without censorship; it involves no feared stimulus, no hierarchy and no relaxation.", "c": "Thought stopping, popularised by Wolpe, interrupts an unwanted thought with a sharp 'Stop!', first aloud and later silently; it suppresses a thought rather than gradually exposing the person to a feared stimulus, and it has faded because suppressed thoughts tend to rebound.", "d": "Psychoeducation is teaching clients and families about a disorder and its treatment; it informs and motivates but involves no graded exposure or relaxation."}}, {"q": "Motivational Interviewing is most commonly used to treat:", "concept": "The answer is a: motivational interviewing (MI) is most commonly used to treat substance use disorders. William R. Miller first described it in 1983 in work with problem drinkers and developed it with Stephen Rollnick in Motivational Interviewing (1991; later editions 2002 and 2013). MI is a collaborative, person-centred but goal-directed conversation that strengthens a person's own motivation to change by resolving ambivalence, the state of wanting and not wanting change at the same time. Arguing for change only makes the client defend the status quo, so the counsellor instead uses the OARS skills (open questions, affirmations, reflections and summaries), expresses empathy, develops discrepancy between behaviour and the client's own goals, rolls with resistance and supports self-efficacy, drawing out the client's own 'change talk'. It is often paired with Prochaska and DiClemente's stages of change, and is especially useful for people in precontemplation and contemplation. Example: a man who drinks heavily says he enjoys alcohol but hates missing his daughter's school events; the counsellor reflects both sides and asks what he would like to be different, letting him voice his own reasons to cut down. Option a is right because addiction is MI's original and best-supported field, where ambivalence about change is the central clinical problem. Exam trap: MI was created by Miller and Rollnick, not by Prochaska and DiClemente, who built the transtheoretical (stages of change) model that MI is often combined with.", "traps": {"b": "Schizophrenia is treated mainly with antipsychotic medication plus family intervention, CBT for psychosis and rehabilitation; MI may be added to improve medication adherence or comorbid drug use, but schizophrenia is not its main target.", "c": "Autism spectrum disorder is helped mostly by early behavioural and developmental interventions such as applied behaviour analysis and parent-mediated programmes; MI, which relies on verbal reflection about ambivalence, is not a treatment for autism.", "d": "ADHD is treated with stimulant medication, parent training and classroom behavioural management; MI is not a standard treatment for its core symptoms."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is a key component of Acceptance and Commitment Therapy (ACT)?", "concept": "The answer is a: cognitive defusion is a key component of acceptance and commitment therapy (ACT). ACT (said as the word 'act') was developed by Steven C. Hayes with Kirk Strosahl and Kelly Wilson; their book appeared in 1999. It belongs to the 'third wave' of behaviour therapy and rests on relational frame theory, Hayes's account of how language lets a thought carry the sting of what it stands for. Trying to escape unwanted thoughts and feelings (experiential avoidance) backfires and narrows life, so ACT aims at psychological flexibility rather than symptom removal. Flexibility is built through six processes, the hexaflex: acceptance, cognitive defusion, contact with the present moment, self-as-context, values and committed action. Cognitive defusion means stepping back from thoughts and seeing them as words and pictures passing through the mind, not literal truths or orders. Its opposite is cognitive fusion, being caught up in a thought as if it were reality. Example: instead of 'I am a failure', the client is taught to say 'I am having the thought that I am a failure', or to repeat the word 'failure' aloud until it loses its grip. Option a is right because defusion is one of the six named core processes of ACT. Exam trap: defusion does not dispute the content of a thought, as Beck's cognitive restructuring does; it changes the person's relationship to the thought while leaving the thought itself alone.", "traps": {"b": "Interpretation of dreams is Sigmund Freud's psychoanalytic technique (The Interpretation of Dreams, 1900), uncovering latent wishes behind the manifest content; ACT does not search for hidden meanings.", "c": "Hypnosis is an induced state of focused attention and heightened suggestibility, named by James Braid in the 1840s; it is not one of ACT's hexaflex processes.", "d": "Free association is the 'fundamental rule' of Freud's psychoanalysis, in which the patient says whatever comes to mind; it aims at insight into the unconscious, not at psychological flexibility."}}, {"q": "In Structural Family Therapy, the therapist aims to:", "concept": "The answer is a: in structural family therapy the therapist aims to reorganise family hierarchies and boundaries. The approach was developed by Salvador Minuchin, who worked first with poor families of delinquent boys (Families of the Slums, 1967) and then at the Philadelphia Child Guidance Clinic (Families and Family Therapy, 1974). Minuchin looked at the family's present structure: its subsystems (spousal, parental, sibling), a hierarchy with parents in charge, and the boundaries between members. Healthy boundaries are clear, firm yet permeable. Diffuse boundaries produce enmeshment (over-involvement, intrusion, little autonomy); rigid boundaries produce disengagement (distance and little support). Symptoms are seen as signs of a faulty structure, so changing the structure relieves them. The therapist first joins the family by accommodating to its style, then draws a structural map and asks for an enactment, the conflict acted out in the session rather than described. Unbalancing, boundary making and reframing then restructure the system. Example: a mother and teenage son are allied against a father who has no say; the therapist sides with the father during an enactment and asks both parents to agree a curfew together, restoring the parental subsystem. Option a is right because restructuring hierarchy and boundaries is the defining goal of Minuchin's method. Exam trap: structural is Minuchin, strategic is Jay Haley, multigenerational (family systems) is Murray Bowen and experiential is Virginia Satir; enmeshment means diffuse boundaries, while rigid boundaries mean disengagement.", "traps": {"b": "Exploring early childhood experiences is the method of psychodynamic therapy; structural family therapy is present-focused and deals with how the family is organised now.", "c": "Focusing solely on individual behaviour contradicts the systems view on which all family therapy rests: the identified patient's symptom is understood as part of the family's pattern of relationships.", "d": "Dream analysis is a technique of Freud's psychoanalysis and Jung's analytical psychology; it plays no part in Minuchin's action-oriented, here-and-now method."}}, {"q": "Which therapy primarily focuses on increasing awareness of the present moment?", "concept": "The answer is a: mindfulness-based cognitive therapy (MBCT) primarily focuses on increasing awareness of the present moment. Mindfulness, which Jon Kabat-Zinn defined as paying attention on purpose, in the present moment and non-judgementally, entered medicine through his mindfulness-based stress reduction (MBSR), founded in 1979 at the University of Massachusetts Medical School. Zindel Segal, Mark Williams and John Teasdale combined MBSR with Beck's cognitive therapy to create MBCT (their book appeared in 2002), an eight-week group programme for people who have recovered from recurrent depression. Through the body scan, sitting meditation and the three-minute breathing space, participants learn decentring: noticing thoughts as passing mental events rather than facts, so that a dip in mood does not spiral into rumination and relapse. In the trial of Teasdale and colleagues (2000), relapse among patients with three or more past episodes fell from 66 to 37 percent. Example: a woman who once slid into depression after every criticism learns to notice 'here is the thought that I am useless', feel her breath and return to the task, instead of brooding for days. Option a is right because present-moment, non-judgemental awareness is the core skill MBCT trains. Exam trap: MBCT is a relapse-prevention treatment for people who are currently well, not a treatment for acute depression; do not confuse it with MBSR (Kabat-Zinn, for stress and pain) or with ACT, which includes present-moment contact as only one of six processes.", "traps": {"b": "Psychoanalysis (Sigmund Freud) looks backwards, using free association, dream analysis and transference to uncover repressed childhood conflicts; its focus is the unconscious past, not present-moment awareness.", "c": "Existential therapy (Rollo May, Irvin Yalom, Viktor Frankl's logotherapy) helps clients face the givens of existence, such as death, freedom, isolation and meaninglessness; it values authentic living, but its primary focus is meaning and responsibility rather than training attention to the present moment.", "d": "Solution-focused brief therapy (Steve de Shazer and Insoo Kim Berg) is future-oriented, using the miracle question, exception questions and scaling to build solutions; it does not train moment-to-moment awareness."}}, {"q": "Type of thinking in which a person starts from one and comes up with many different ideas is an example of", "concept": "The answer is a: thinking that starts from one point and produces many different ideas is divergent thinking. The distinction comes from J. P. (Joy Paul) Guilford, whose 1950 American Psychological Association presidential address launched the modern study of creativity. In his structure of intellect model (The Nature of Human Intelligence, 1967), divergent production and convergent production are separate mental operations. Divergent thinking fans out from one starting point to many possible answers; convergent thinking narrows many pieces of information down to the single correct answer. Guilford scored divergent production on four dimensions: fluency (number of ideas), flexibility (number of different categories), originality (statistical rarity) and elaboration (detail). Tasks such as alternative uses and the Torrance Tests of Creative Thinking measure it. Example: asked 'How many uses can you think of for a brick?', one person lists a wall, a doorstop, a paperweight, a weapon and red powder for paint; that spread of ideas from one stimulus is divergent thinking. Solving 'seven times eight' needs convergent thinking because there is only one right answer. Option a is right because the stem describes exactly the one-to-many movement that defines divergent thinking. Exam trap: divergent thinking is closely tied to creativity but is not the same as intelligence; ordinary IQ tests mostly reward convergent thinking, which is why a highly intelligent person may still score modestly on divergent tasks.", "traps": {"b": "Convergent thinking, Guilford's opposite term, moves from many pieces of information to the one correct answer, as in an arithmetic or multiple-choice problem; it narrows down rather than branching out.", "c": "Critical analysis (critical thinking) evaluates the evidence and logic of claims already made; it judges ideas rather than generating many new ones.", "d": "Decision making is choosing among available alternatives, studied through Herbert Simon's bounded rationality and Kahneman and Tversky's heuristics; it ends in a single choice, not many ideas."}}, {"q": "Behavioral Activation is primarily used to treat:", "concept": "The answer is a: behavioural activation (BA) is primarily used to treat depression. Its roots lie in Peter Lewinsohn's 1970s theory that depression follows a loss of response-contingent positive reinforcement: when fewer of a person's actions are rewarded, he withdraws, which removes even more reward, and the spiral deepens. In 1996 Neil Jacobson and colleagues found that the activation component of Beck's cognitive therapy worked as well as the full package, and Jacobson, Christopher Martell and Michael Addis then developed BA as a treatment in its own right. In the trial of Sona Dimidjian and colleagues (2006), BA matched antidepressant medication for more severely depressed patients. BA works from the outside in: rather than waiting to feel motivated, the client acts first, and mood follows. Steps include activity monitoring, rating activities for mastery and pleasure, scheduling valued and enjoyable activities, breaking tasks into small steps and tackling avoidance and rumination. Example: a student who has stopped leaving her room records her day, notices her mood lifts slightly after a walk, and schedules a short walk and a call to a friend each day, gradually rebuilding the rewarding routine depression took away. Option a is right because BA was built from a reinforcement model of depression and has its strongest evidence there. Exam trap: BA means engaging in meaningful, rewarding activity; it is not relaxation, hypnosis or analysis of childhood, and it is not exposure therapy, the core method for anxiety disorders.", "traps": {"b": "Schizophrenia is treated mainly with antipsychotics, family intervention and CBT for psychosis; activity scheduling may help negative symptoms as part of rehabilitation, but BA was not developed for psychosis.", "c": "PTSD's first-line psychological treatments are trauma-focused: Edna Foa's prolonged exposure, Patricia Resick's cognitive processing therapy and EMDR; BA is at most an add-on there.", "d": "Phobias respond best to exposure, either graded (systematic desensitisation) or intense (flooding); the active ingredient is confronting the feared stimulus, not scheduling rewarding activity."}}, {"q": "The core principle of Exposure Therapy is:", "concept": "The answer is a: the core principle of exposure therapy is repeated confrontation with feared stimuli. Exposure therapy is repeated or prolonged contact with a feared object, situation, memory or bodily sensation, without escape or avoidance, until the fear subsides. Its roots lie in learning theory: Mary Cover Jones's 1924 work with Peter and Joseph Wolpe's systematic desensitisation (1958) showed that fear can be unlearned. Two processes explain it. Habituation is the fall in anxiety with repeated or prolonged contact with a stimulus; extinction occurs as the stimulus keeps appearing without the feared outcome, so the conditioned link weakens. Michelle Craske's inhibitory learning view adds that the client learns a new, competing memory ('the dog did not bite'). Avoidance, by contrast, keeps fear alive because the relief it brings negatively reinforces it. Exposure can be graded or intense (flooding), in vivo or imaginal, interoceptive (spinning or overbreathing for panic) or in virtual reality. In obsessive-compulsive disorder it is combined with response prevention (ERP), described by Victor Meyer in 1966; for PTSD Edna Foa built prolonged exposure. Example: a man afraid of lifts rides one floor repeatedly until his anxiety falls, then two floors, then ten. Option a is right because the confrontation itself, repeated without escape, is the active ingredient. Exam trap: exposure must last long enough for anxiety to fall and must block escape and safety behaviours; leaving at the peak of fear teaches that escape works and makes the fear stronger.", "traps": {"b": "Challenging irrational thoughts is cognitive restructuring, the core of Albert Ellis's rational emotive behaviour therapy and Aaron Beck's cognitive therapy; it changes beliefs through disputation, not through direct contact with feared stimuli.", "c": "Exploring early childhood trauma is a psychodynamic aim; even trauma-focused exposure for PTSD confronts specific memories and reminders to extinguish fear, rather than exploring the past for insight.", "d": "Positive affirmations are self-help statements repeated to boost self-esteem; they involve no feared stimulus and are not a principle of any established behaviour therapy."}}, {"q": "Narrative Therapy helps clients by:", "concept": "The answer is a: narrative therapy helps clients by reconstructing their personal stories in a meaningful way. It was created by the Australian social worker Michael White in Adelaide and David Epston in New Zealand, and set out in Narrative Means to Therapeutic Ends (1990). It grew from family therapy and social constructionism: people make sense of their lives through stories, and no single account is the true one. Clients often arrive with a thin, 'problem-saturated' story in which the problem defines who they are. The therapist first externalises the problem ('the person is not the problem; the problem is the problem'), giving it a name such as 'the Anger' or White's famous 'Sneaky Poo', so that person and problem can be seen as separate. Next comes the search for unique outcomes, moments when the problem did not win. Finally the therapist helps the client re-author a richer, preferred story around those exceptions, often supported by therapeutic letters, certificates and an audience of significant others. Example: a teenager who calls herself 'a depressed person' begins to speak of 'the Gloom' that visits her, recalls the week she went to a concert despite it, and builds a story of herself as someone who stands up to the Gloom. Option a is right because re-authoring the client's life story is the defining aim of narrative therapy. Exam trap: externalisation belongs to narrative therapy, exception and miracle questions to solution-focused brief therapy, and prescribing the symptom to strategic family therapy.", "traps": {"b": "Analysing unconscious motives is the method of psychoanalysis and psychodynamic therapy; narrative therapy deliberately avoids expert interpretation and treats the client as the expert on their own life.", "c": "Mindfulness exercises belong to MBSR, MBCT, DBT and ACT, which train present-moment awareness; narrative therapy works through conversations about stories, not meditation.", "d": "Systematic desensitisation is Joseph Wolpe's (1958) behavioural technique of graded exposure paired with relaxation for phobias; it has nothing to do with retelling life stories."}}, {"q": "Which of the following best describes delusion?", "concept": "The answer is b: a delusion is a fixed false belief not amenable to reason. In descriptive psychopathology a delusion is a false, firmly held belief that is not shaken by argument or contrary evidence and is out of keeping with the person's cultural, religious and educational background. Karl Jaspers, in General Psychopathology (1913), named its three external marks: subjective certainty, incorrigibility (it cannot be corrected by experience or reasoning) and impossibility or falsity of content. The cultural clause matters: a community's shared belief in the evil eye or in spirits is not a delusion, however strange it seems to an outsider. Delusions are disorders of thought content, not of perception. They can be primary (not understandable from any other experience) or secondary (understandable from a mood or hallucination), and they are classified by theme: persecutory (the commonest), reference, grandiose, nihilistic, guilt, jealousy, erotomania and somatic. Together with hallucinations and disorganised speech or behaviour, delusions mark psychosis. Example: a clerk is utterly convinced that his neighbours have planted cameras in his walls to steal his ideas; when shown that no cameras exist, he says they have been cleverly hidden. Option b is right because fixity and imperviousness to reason are the defining features of a delusion. Exam trap: an overvalued idea (Carl Wernicke's term) is also a strongly held belief, but it is understandable and lacks absolute fixity, as in the anorexic patient's conviction that she is fat; an obsession is recognised as one's own and resisted.", "traps": {"a": "A perceptual disturbance is a hallucination or illusion, a disorder of perception; a delusion is a disorder of the content of thought, a belief, not a perception.", "c": "A mood disorder is a diagnostic category (depression, bipolar disorder), not a symptom; delusions can occur within severe mood disorders but are not defined by mood.", "d": "An obsession is an intrusive, unwanted thought that the person recognises as their own and as senseless, and tries to resist, as in OCD; a delusion is held with full conviction and not resisted."}}, {"q": "A primary delusion is:", "concept": "The answer is b: a primary delusion is one arising suddenly without cause, meaning without any understandable psychological antecedent. The concept comes from Karl Jaspers, who in General Psychopathology (1913) divided delusions into two kinds. A secondary delusion (a delusion-like idea) can be understood as growing out of some other experience: guilt from deeply depressed mood, persecution to explain hostile voices, grandiosity from manic elation. A primary or autochthonous ('self-born') delusion cannot be traced back to anything else; it arrives fully formed, with immediate conviction, and empathic understanding cannot get behind it. Jaspers held that this ununderstandability was the mark of a true psychotic process, typically schizophrenia. Primary delusions appear as delusional mood (a sense that something sinister is going on), delusional perception, delusional memory and the sudden delusional idea. Delusional perception, in which a normal perception suddenly carries a private, delusional meaning, is one of Kurt Schneider's first-rank symptoms. Example: a young man sees a red traffic light and instantly 'knows' that he has been chosen to save the world; nothing in his mood or other experiences explains the link. Option b is right because sudden onset without an understandable cause in other experience is exactly what makes a delusion primary. Exam trap: options a and c are the textbook examples of secondary delusions, and a delusion based on mood or hallucination is never primary, however bizarre it is. Key note: 'without cause' is loose wording; strictly, a primary delusion has no psychological antecedent that explains it, though it may still have a biological cause, and a phase of delusional mood can precede it.", "traps": {"a": "A delusion secondary to hallucination is, by definition, a secondary delusion: the belief is an understandable attempt to explain the voices or visions, as in persecution invented to account for threatening voices.", "c": "A delusion based on mood is also secondary, such as delusions of guilt or poverty in severe depression or grandiose delusions in mania; these are mood-congruent and understandable from the mood.", "d": "A delusion induced by another person is shared psychotic disorder, or folie à deux (Charles Lasègue and Jules Falret, 1877), in which a dependent partner takes on the delusion of a dominant one; its origin in another person is the opposite of arising unprompted."}}, {"q": "Which term describes a false sensory perception without external stimulus?", "concept": "The answer is b: a hallucination is a false sensory perception without an external stimulus. The French psychiatrist Jean-Étienne Esquirol gave the term its modern meaning in the early nineteenth century, separating it from the illusion, which needs a real object. In descriptive psychopathology a true hallucination has three features: it arises with no external stimulus, it is as vivid and real as a normal perception, and it is located in outer, objective space (the voice comes from the corridor). A pseudohallucination, by contrast, is experienced in inner space ('a voice inside my head') or is recognised as unreal. Hallucinations can occur in any sense. Auditory ones are the commonest in schizophrenia, and third-person voices, running commentary and thought echo are Schneider's first-rank symptoms; visual hallucinations suggest an organic cause such as delirium; tactile formication suggests cocaine use or alcohol withdrawal; olfactory ones suggest temporal lobe epilepsy. Hypnagogic (falling asleep) and hypnopompic (waking) hallucinations occur in healthy people. Example: alone in a silent room, a woman clearly hears a man's voice outside her window calling her a thief, though no one is there. Option b is right because perception in the absence of any stimulus is the definition of a hallucination. Exam trap: the distinction is the stimulus. An illusion is a misperception of something real (a coat on a hook seen as an intruder); a hallucination needs no stimulus at all; a delusion is a belief, not a perception.", "traps": {"a": "A delusion is a fixed false belief not amenable to reason, a disorder of thought content; it involves no false sensory experience.", "c": "An illusion is a misperception of a real external stimulus, such as faces seen in clouds (pareidolia) or a frightened person mistaking a shadow for a figure; unlike a hallucination, the stimulus exists.", "d": "An obsession is an intrusive, unwanted thought, image or urge recognised as one's own and resisted, as in OCD; it is a thought, not a perception."}}, {"q": "An individual believing the TV is sending them messages is experiencing:", "concept": "The answer is a: believing that the television is sending one messages is a referential delusion, or delusion of reference. In a delusion of reference, neutral events, objects or other people's words are believed to carry a special, personal meaning for the patient: a newsreader is speaking directly to him, a song on the radio was chosen for him, strangers' laughter is about him, a newspaper headline is a coded instruction. The belief has the usual marks of a delusion described by Karl Jaspers: certainty, incorrigibility and false content. It should be separated from an idea of reference, in which the person has the same self-referring thought but without full conviction ('I wonder whether they were laughing at me'), which is common in social anxiety and in schizotypal or paranoid personality. Referential delusions are frequent in schizophrenia and often travel with persecutory ones, since the broadcasts may be taken as warnings or threats. Example: a young woman says the weather forecaster's gestures each evening are instructions telling her which streets she may walk on, and no argument dislodges the idea. Option a is right because the core of the experience is that an ordinary, impersonal event is taken as a message aimed at her. Exam trap: delusions of reference are not one of Schneider's first-rank symptoms, whereas delusional perception is; and an idea of reference lacks the fixity of a true delusion.", "traps": {"b": "A nihilistic delusion is the belief that oneself, one's organs or the world do not exist or are dead; its extreme form is Cotard's syndrome (Jules Cotard, 1880s), seen mainly in severe psychotic depression.", "c": "A persecutory delusion is the belief that one is being deliberately plotted against, harmed or spied upon; the TV could form part of a persecutory system, but receiving personal messages from it is reference, not persecution itself.", "d": "A somatic (hypochondriacal) delusion concerns the body, such as the conviction that one's insides are rotting or that one is infested with parasites; it has nothing to do with messages from the environment."}}, {"q": "Changes in consciousness are likely seen in:", "concept": "The answer is d: changes in consciousness are characteristically seen in delirium. Delirium is an acute brain failure. DSM-5-TR defines it as a disturbance of attention (difficulty directing, focusing, sustaining or shifting it) and awareness (reduced orientation to the environment) that develops over a short period, usually hours to a few days, fluctuates in severity during the day, and brings an additional cognitive change such as disorientation, memory loss or perceptual disturbance, with evidence of a physical cause. Older texts called this clouding of consciousness, the mildest lowering of awareness. Causes include infection, metabolic or electrolyte upset, hypoxia, drugs, intoxication or withdrawal (delirium tremens) and surgery. Typical features are visual hallucinations and illusions, a reversed sleep-wake cycle, worsening in the evening (sundowning) and diffuse slowing on the EEG. Subtypes are hyperactive, hypoactive (quiet and easily missed) and mixed. Example: an 80-year-old woman two days after hip surgery is lucid in the morning but at night is drowsy, cannot follow a conversation, thinks she is at home and picks at the bedclothes; a urinary infection is found, and she clears when it is treated. Option d is right because disturbed consciousness and attention, with acute onset and fluctuating course, is the defining feature of delirium. Exam trap: in schizophrenia, depression and early dementia consciousness is clear; the most reliable clue separating delirium from dementia is disturbed attention or consciousness with acute onset, not memory loss or hallucinations, which occur in both.", "traps": {"a": "In schizophrenia consciousness is characteristically clear: ICD-10 describes it as occurring in clear consciousness, and Schneider's first-rank symptoms count only in the absence of organic brain disease.", "b": "Depression may impair concentration, but the sensorium is clear; even in depressive pseudodementia attention and consciousness are preserved.", "c": "In dementia consciousness stays clear until the late stages, while memory and other cognitive losses progress over months to years; fluctuating attention in Lewy body dementia and delirium superimposed on dementia are well-known exceptions, not the rule."}}, {"q": "Which symptom is considered a disorder of possession of thought?", "concept": "The answer is a: thought insertion is a disorder of the possession of thought. British descriptive psychopathology, especially Frank Fish's Clinical Psychopathology, which many Indian syllabuses follow, describes thought under four headings: stream (the speed and flow of thinking), form (how ideas are linked), content (what is thought, such as delusions) and possession (the sense that one's thoughts are one's own). Normally we take for granted that our thoughts belong to us and are under our control. In disorders of possession this ownership breaks down. Thought alienation has three classic forms: thought insertion (thoughts are put into the mind by an outside agency), thought withdrawal (thoughts are taken out) and thought broadcasting (thoughts pass out of the mind so that others know them). All three are first-rank symptoms of Kurt Schneider and strongly suggest schizophrenia in the absence of organic disease. Many Indian texts also place obsessions under possession, since the thought is known to be one's own yet cannot be controlled. Example: a patient says, 'These thoughts about killing are not mine; my neighbour beams them into my head with a machine.' The thoughts are experienced as foreign, not merely unwanted. Option a is right because thought insertion is a loss of ownership of thought, the defining feature of a possession disorder. Exam trap: circumstantiality, flight of ideas and tangentiality concern how thoughts flow and connect, so they are disorders of the stream or form of thought; and an obsession, unlike an inserted thought, is recognised as coming from one's own mind.", "traps": {"b": "Circumstantiality is speech loaded with needless detail and digressions that does finally reach its goal, seen in epilepsy, obsessional and organic states; it is a disorder of the form (or, in many Indian texts, the stream) of thought.", "c": "Flight of ideas, typical of mania, is rapid thought that jumps from one idea to the next before any is finished, with links of chance, rhyme or clang that can still be traced; it is a disorder of the stream or form of thought.", "d": "Tangentiality is answering obliquely and drifting away from the question without ever reaching the goal, seen in schizophrenia and mania; it is a disorder of the form or stream of thought, not of ownership."}}, {"q": "First rank symptoms are characteristic of which condition?", "concept": "The answer is b: first rank symptoms are characteristic of schizophrenia. They were listed by the German psychiatrist Kurt Schneider (1887 to 1967), whose Clinical Psychopathology appeared in English in 1959. Eugen Bleuler's fundamental symptoms (the four As) were hard to rate reliably, so Schneider picked out experiences that, in the absence of organic brain disease, strongly suggest schizophrenia. Their shared theme is a breakdown of the boundary between self and world. The usual count is eleven: three kinds of auditory hallucination (thought echo, voices arguing or discussing the patient in the third person, and a running commentary on his actions); thought alienation (thought insertion, withdrawal and broadcasting); passivity experiences (made feelings, made impulses, made volitional acts and somatic passivity); and delusional perception. His second-rank symptoms included other hallucinations, perplexity, sudden delusional ideas, mood changes and emotional blunting. Example: a 22-year-old student says that a voice keeps describing everything he does ('now he is opening the door') and that his arm is moved by a force from outside; both are first-rank symptoms. Option b is right because Schneider defined these symptoms specifically as the markers of schizophrenia. Exam trap: first rank symptoms are characteristic but not specific; they also occur in mania and other psychoses, so DSM-5 dropped their special weight; and delusions of reference, depersonalisation, negative symptoms and Bleuler's loosening of associations are not first rank.", "traps": {"a": "Depression is a mood disorder; even psychotic depression usually brings mood-congruent delusions of guilt, poverty or nihilism and second-person derogatory voices, not Schneider's first-rank symptoms.", "c": "In OCD the intrusive thoughts are recognised as one's own and resisted; that preserved ownership is the opposite of thought insertion, a first-rank symptom.", "d": "Anxiety disorders involve fear, worry and bodily arousal with intact reality testing; they produce no hallucinations, passivity experiences or delusional perception."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is used to compare means of two or more groups?", "concept": "The answer is a: analysis of variance (ANOVA) is used to compare the means of two or more groups. ANOVA was developed by Ronald A. Fisher in the 1920s, notably in Statistical Methods for Research Workers (1925); George Snedecor later named its statistic F in Fisher's honour. It compares means by analysing variances. The total sum of squares is split into variation between the group means and variation within the groups (error). The F ratio is the between-groups mean square divided by the within-groups mean square: if the groups differ only by chance, F is close to 1; a large F means the group means differ more than chance allows. A significant F says that at least one mean differs, and post hoc tests such as Tukey's then locate the difference. ANOVA assumes a continuous dependent variable, independent observations, roughly normal distributions and equal variances. It is used for three or more means because running many t-tests would inflate the familywise Type I error rate, but it also works for two groups, where F = t squared and both tests give the same p-value. Example: a researcher compares anxiety scores after CBT, medication and a waiting list; a one-way ANOVA tests all three means in one step. Option a is right because only ANOVA handles both two and more than two group means. Exam trap: the t-test compares at most two means, so it fails the 'or more' part of the stem; chi-square compares frequencies, not means.", "traps": {"b": "The t-test, published by William Sealy Gosset under the pen name 'Student' (1908), compares the means of only two groups or conditions; it cannot handle three or more in a single test.", "c": "Multiple regression predicts one continuous outcome from several predictor variables and estimates each predictor's unique contribution; it models relationships rather than being the standard test for comparing group means.", "d": "Chi-square, introduced by Karl Pearson in 1900, compares observed and expected frequencies of categorical data, such as how many men and women chose each option; it does not compare means."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is a negative symptom in schizophrenia?", "concept": "The answer is c: avolition is a negative symptom of schizophrenia. Symptoms of schizophrenia are grouped as positive, negative and cognitive. Positive symptoms are the presence of something abnormal, an excess or distortion of normal function: delusions, hallucinations, disorganised speech and grossly disorganised behaviour. Negative symptoms are the absence or reduction of something normal. The usual five are affective flattening (reduced emotional expression), alogia (poverty of speech), avolition (lack of self-initiated, purposeful activity), anhedonia (reduced pleasure) and asociality; ICD-10 lists marked apathy, paucity of speech and blunted or incongruous emotional responses. Timothy Crow (1980) linked negative symptoms to his Type II schizophrenia, with a chronic course, poor response to antipsychotics and enlarged ventricles, and Nancy Andreasen built the Scale for the Assessment of Negative Symptoms (SANS) to rate them. Negative symptoms predict poor social and occupational outcome more strongly than positive ones. Example: a 30-year-old man with schizophrenia, whose voices are controlled by medication, still lies in bed most of the day, does not wash unless reminded and has abandoned all plans to find work; he is not sad, he simply cannot start or sustain goal-directed activity. Option c is right because avolition is a loss of normal drive, the very definition of a negative symptom. Exam trap: positive and negative refer to the presence or absence of a function, not to whether a symptom is pleasant or harmful; hallucinations, delusions and disorganised behaviour are always positive, and Bleuler's ambivalence is not a negative symptom.", "traps": {"a": "Hallucination, a perception without a stimulus, is an added abnormal experience and therefore a positive symptom.", "b": "A delusion, a fixed false belief, is a distortion of normal thinking and the classic positive symptom.", "d": "Catatonia (Karl Kahlbaum, 1874) is a psychomotor syndrome of stupor, posturing, negativism, waxy flexibility, echolalia or excitement; DSM-5-TR lists grossly disorganised or catatonic behaviour as a criterion separate from negative symptoms, and catatonia also occurs in mood and medical disorders."}}, {"q": "In depressive pseudodementia, the cognitive deficits are typically:", "concept": "The answer is a: in depressive pseudodementia the cognitive deficits are typically variable and inconsistent. Pseudodementia is cognitive impairment caused by a psychiatric illness, usually depression in an older adult, that looks like dementia; Leslie Kiloh drew attention to it in a 1961 paper. The deficits come from poor concentration, slowed thinking and low motivation rather than loss of brain tissue, so performance varies from task to task and day to day, and effort is poor. In pseudodementia onset is fairly abrupt and can be dated, often after a loss; the patient complains loudly of poor memory and highlights failures; answers are often 'I don't know' rather than near misses; performance on tasks of similar difficulty is inconsistent; depressed mood, past episodes and sleep or appetite change are present; and the deficits improve with antidepressant treatment. In true dementia onset is insidious, the patient minimises or hides deficits and makes near misses, decline is consistent and progressive, and cortical signs such as aphasia, apraxia and agnosia appear. Example: a 70-year-old widower says 'My memory is finished' and answers most test questions with 'I don't know', yet recalls every detail of his pension dispute; after eight weeks of antidepressants his scores return to normal. Option a is right because fluctuating, effort-dependent, inconsistent performance is the hallmark of depressive pseudodementia. Exam trap: in dementia the patient conceals the problem and the family complains; in pseudodementia the patient complains, often more than the family does.", "traps": {"b": "Progressive and irreversible decline is the course of true degenerative dementia such as Alzheimer's disease; pseudodementia lifts when the depression is treated.", "c": "Cortical dementia, such as Alzheimer's disease, shows a consistent pattern of amnesia with aphasia, apraxia and agnosia; depressive pseudodementia, with its slowing and poor effort, does not fit that pattern.", "d": "Agnosia, a failure to recognise objects or faces despite intact senses, is a cortical sign of true dementia; it is not a feature of depression-related cognitive impairment."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is a hallmark of qualitative research?", "concept": "The answer is c: a hallmark of qualitative research is its focus on subjective experience. Qualitative research studies meaning, experience, process and context through words, images and observed action, and analyses them by interpretation rather than measurement. It seeks the insider's view: how people themselves understand and feel about what happens to them. Its roots include Verstehen (interpretive understanding, associated with Wilhelm Dilthey and Max Weber), Edmund Husserl's phenomenology and symbolic interactionism, the name Herbert Blumer gave in 1937 to George Herbert Mead's ideas. Its logic is inductive and bottom-up; samples are small and purposive; settings are natural; and the researcher is the instrument, which calls for reflexivity. Major traditions include phenomenology and interpretative phenomenological analysis (Jonathan Smith), grounded theory (Glaser and Strauss, 1967), ethnography, case study and narrative research. Quality is judged by trustworthiness (Lincoln and Guba, 1985) rather than by statistical validity and reliability. Example: instead of measuring caregiver burden on a scale, a researcher holds in-depth interviews with ten mothers of children with autism and draws out themes such as 'living with judgement' and 'finding a new normal'. Option c is right because capturing participants' own subjective experience and meanings is what qualitative research is designed to do. Exam trap: the other three options are hallmarks of quantitative research; in 'odd one out' questions, whatever implies objective measurement, probability samples, control or statistical generalisation is the non-qualitative feature.", "traps": {"a": "Randomised control (random assignment to conditions with a control group) is the hallmark of the true experiment and the randomised controlled trial, the core of quantitative, causal research.", "b": "Statistical generalisation from a probability sample to a population is a quantitative aim; qualitative studies offer transferability, judged by the reader from thick description, instead.", "d": "Large sample sizes are needed for statistical power in quantitative work; qualitative studies use small, purposive samples and stop at saturation."}}, {"q": "Grounded theory is used to:", "concept": "The answer is b: grounded theory is used to develop a theory from data. It was launched by the sociologists Barney Glaser and Anselm Strauss in The Discovery of Grounded Theory (1967), after their studies of dying patients in hospitals. They objected to research that only tested grand theories handed down from above; instead, theory should be 'grounded' in, and built up from, systematically gathered data. The method is inductive. Its key procedures are constant comparison (each new incident is compared with earlier ones and with emerging categories), theoretical sampling (choosing whom to study next according to what the developing theory needs), memo writing (recording the analyst's ideas as they form) and theoretical saturation (stopping when new data add nothing new to the categories). Strauss and Juliet Corbin (1990) set out three coding stages: open coding breaks the data apart and labels concepts; axial coding relates the categories to one another; selective coding integrates everything around one core category. Kathy Charmaz later developed a constructivist version. Example: a researcher interviews young adults recovering from a first episode of psychosis, codes the transcripts line by line, and builds a model in which 'reclaiming identity' is the core category linking stigma, family support and work. Option b is right because generating theory from the data, rather than testing a prior theory, is the defining purpose of grounded theory. Exam trap: grounded theory begins without a hypothesis; proving hypotheses, measuring correlation and testing causality are deductive, quantitative aims.", "traps": {"a": "Proving (more precisely, testing) a hypothesis is the hypothetico-deductive logic of quantitative research; grounded theory begins without a hypothesis, and science never strictly proves one anyway.", "c": "Measuring statistical correlation is correlational, quantitative research using coefficients such as Pearson's r; grounded theory works with words and meanings, not coefficients.", "d": "Testing causality requires an experiment with manipulation of the independent variable, random assignment and control; grounded theory is an inductive, qualitative method."}}, {"q": "Which method is not used in quantitative research?", "concept": "The answer is b: thematic analysis is not used in quantitative research; it is a qualitative method. Quantitative research measures variables as numbers and tests hypotheses statistically, while qualitative research analyses words, meanings and experiences by interpretation. Thematic analysis is a method for identifying, analysing and reporting patterns of meaning (themes) across a qualitative data set such as interview transcripts or focus-group discussions. Virginia Braun and Victoria Clarke set out its widely used form in 2006, in six phases: familiarising oneself with the data, generating initial codes, searching for themes, reviewing themes, defining and naming themes, and writing the report. It is flexible because it is not tied to one theory, which is why it is often the first qualitative method students learn. The other three options are all inferential statistics: the t-test compares two means, chi-square compares frequencies of categorical data, and regression predicts an outcome from one or more predictors. Example: after interviewing twelve nurses about burnout, a researcher codes the transcripts and identifies the themes 'emotional exhaustion', 'feeling unseen' and 'peer support as protection'; no numbers or significance tests are involved. Option b is right because thematic analysis works with textual meaning, not numerical data, and so belongs to qualitative research. Exam trap: content analysis can be quantitative (counting how often categories occur), but thematic analysis, grounded theory coding, interpretative phenomenological analysis and discourse analysis are qualitative methods.", "traps": {"a": "The t-test (William Gosset, 'Student', 1908) compares the means of two groups or conditions and is a standard quantitative, parametric test.", "c": "Chi-square (Karl Pearson, 1900) tests whether observed frequencies of categorical data differ from expected ones; it is a quantitative, non-parametric test.", "d": "Regression, a term Francis Galton introduced in the 1880s, predicts a dependent variable from one or more predictors and is a core quantitative technique."}}, {"q": "Triangulation in qualitative research refers to:", "concept": "The answer is c: triangulation in qualitative research refers to using multiple methods (and other multiple perspectives) to ensure credibility. The word is borrowed from surveying and navigation, where a position is fixed by taking bearings from two or more known points. Norman Denzin, in The Research Act (1970; the 1978 edition is the one most often cited), named four types: data triangulation (several sources, times, places or people), investigator triangulation (several researchers or analysts), theory triangulation (several theoretical lenses) and methodological triangulation (several methods, such as interviews, observation and documents, or qualitative with quantitative data). The logic is that each method has its own weaknesses, so if different approaches converge on the same finding, confidence in it grows; where they disagree, the difference itself is informative. Yvonna Lincoln and Egon Guba (1985) listed triangulation, with prolonged engagement, peer debriefing and member checking, as a route to credibility, the qualitative counterpart of internal validity. Example: to study stigma in a psychiatric outpatient clinic, a researcher interviews patients, observes waiting-room interactions and reviews case notes; when all three point to the same pattern, the finding is more credible. Option c is right because cross-checking a finding through several methods or sources is what triangulation means. Exam trap: the 'tri' does not mean three; triangulation is about multiple, converging perspectives, and this research sense is different from family-therapy triangulation, in which a child is drawn into the parents' conflict.", "traps": {"a": "Using three variables is a literal misreading of 'tri'; the number of variables in a study has nothing to do with triangulation, which concerns multiple perspectives on one question.", "b": "Repeating measures is a quantitative idea, as in test-retest reliability or a repeated-measures (within-subjects) design; it checks consistency over time, not convergence across different methods.", "d": "Combining three data sets again takes 'tri' literally; data triangulation does draw on several sources, but there is no rule of three, and the point is convergence across different methods and viewpoints."}}, {"q": "Member checking is used to:", "concept": "The answer is b: member checking is used to confirm validity with participants. Also called respondent validation, member checking means taking data, interpretations or draft findings back to the people who provided them and asking whether they recognise them as an accurate account of their experience. Yvonna Lincoln and Egon Guba, in Naturalistic Inquiry (1985), called it the most crucial technique for establishing credibility. Their trustworthiness framework replaces the quantitative criteria with four parallels: credibility (for internal validity), transferability (for external validity), dependability (for reliability) and confirmability (for objectivity). Credibility is built through prolonged engagement, peer debriefing, triangulation and member checking. Member checking can be done by returning interview transcripts, a summary of the themes or the final report, individually or in a group. Participants may confirm, correct or add to the interpretation; disagreement is not simply an error but new data to consider, since people's views change over time. Example: after analysing interviews with women who survived suicide attempts, a researcher meets several participants and shares the emerging theme 'being unheard before the crisis'; they confirm it but suggest that 'unseen' captures it better. Option b is right because the purpose of member checking is to establish the credibility, the qualitative form of validity, of findings by checking them with the participants themselves. Exam trap: member checking concerns validity (credibility), not reliability; the qualitative parallel of reliability is dependability, shown through an audit trail.", "traps": {"a": "Testing reliability means checking consistency of measurement (test-retest, inter-rater, internal consistency); in qualitative work its parallel is dependability, supported by an audit trail, not by returning findings to participants.", "c": "Comparing two groups is the job of statistical tests such as the independent-samples t-test or the Mann-Whitney U test, a quantitative task unrelated to member checking.", "d": "Analysing variance is ANOVA (Ronald Fisher), a quantitative technique for comparing group means; member checking involves no statistics."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is a type of ordinal data?", "concept": "The answer is b: an IQ score is treated in this paper as ordinal data. Stanley Smith Stevens (1946) sorted measurement into four levels, each adding a property: nominal (labels only, such as blood group), ordinal (rank order, with unknown distances between ranks), interval (equal units but an arbitrary zero, such as Celsius temperature) and ratio (a true zero, such as weight). IQ began with Alfred Binet's mental age (1905) and William Stern's ratio IQ (1912); David Wechsler replaced it with the deviation IQ (1939), which converts a person's rank among same-age peers into a scale with mean 100 and SD 15. The score shows where someone stands among peers, but nobody has shown that the 10 points between 70 and 80 equal the 10 points between 130 and 140 in real ability, and an IQ of 0 does not mean no intelligence. Example: Riya scores 120 and Arjun 110; we can safely say Riya ranks higher, but not that 120 is twice 60. Option b is right because, of the four, only IQ is a psychological score whose numbers rank people without guaranteed equal intervals. Exam trap: blood pressure and reaction time look like scores too, but both have a true zero (no pressure, no time), so they are ratio data. Key note: most statistics and psychometrics texts, including the Final Textbook, class IQ as interval data because deviation IQs are built to have equal units; strict measurement theorists reply that equal intervals in the underlying trait are assumed, not shown, so IQ is really ordinal. The official key takes the strict view, and among these options it is the only defensible ordinal choice.", "traps": {"a": "A blood pressure reading in mmHg is ratio data: equal units and a true zero (no pressure), so 140 mmHg is genuinely twice 70. It becomes ordinal only if collapsed into categories such as normal, elevated and high.", "c": "An interview transcript is qualitative (textual) data, words rather than numbers; it has no level of measurement until it is coded, and the codes are then usually nominal categories.", "d": "Reaction time, measured in milliseconds since Franciscus Donders' subtraction method (1868), is a classic ratio variable: zero means no time elapsed and 400 ms is twice 200 ms."}}, {"q": "Phenomenology focuses on:", "concept": "The answer is b: phenomenology focuses on lived experience. Phenomenology began as a philosophy with Edmund Husserl (1859 to 1938), whose aim was to go 'to the things themselves': describe experience exactly as it appears to the person before theorising about it. To do this the researcher practises bracketing (epoche), setting aside her own assumptions. Husserl's former assistant Martin Heidegger (Being and Time, 1927) argued that we can never step outside our world, so all understanding is interpretation; this gave hermeneutic or interpretive phenomenology. In psychology the best known method today is Jonathan Smith's interpretative phenomenological analysis (IPA, 1996), in which the researcher makes sense of participants making sense of their experience. A phenomenological study usually takes a small, purposive sample of people who have all lived through the phenomenon, uses long open interviews, and moves from significant statements to themes and finally to the essence of the experience. Example: a researcher interviews eight mothers of children with autism about what the day of diagnosis felt like, and distils themes such as shock, relief at having a name, and grief for an imagined future. Option b is right because the defining aim of phenomenology is to capture what an experience is like from the inside, in the participant's own terms. Exam trap: 'cultural interpretation' sounds close because phenomenology is interpretive, but studying a culture or group through long immersion is ethnography; phenomenology studies an experience, not a culture.", "traps": {"a": "Cultural interpretation is the aim of ethnography, rooted in Bronislaw Malinowski's Trobriand fieldwork (Argonauts of the Western Pacific, 1922) and Clifford Geertz's thick description (1973); it studies a group's shared meanings, not an individual's lived experience.", "c": "Experimental conditions belong to quantitative, positivist research, where the researcher manipulates an independent variable and controls the rest; phenomenology describes experience in its natural context without manipulation.", "d": "Survey responses are standardised answers from large samples analysed statistically; they capture opinions in pre-set categories rather than the rich first-person description phenomenology needs."}}, {"q": "What is a key limitation of qualitative research?", "concept": "The answer is c: a key limitation of qualitative research is limited generalisability. Qualitative research studies meaning, experience and process through words and observation, using small, purposively chosen samples and interpretive analysis (grounded theory from Barney Glaser and Anselm Strauss, 1967; phenomenology; ethnography; thematic analysis). Its strength is depth: it shows how and why people experience something. The price is breadth. With eight or twelve participants chosen because they are information-rich, not at random, the findings cannot be statistically projected to a whole population, and because the researcher is the instrument, another researcher might read the data differently. Yvonna Lincoln and Egon Guba (1985) answered this by replacing external validity with transferability: the researcher gives a thick description of setting and participants, and the reader judges whether the findings fit another context. Example: interviews with ten caregivers of people with schizophrenia in one Chandigarh hospital may reveal powerful themes of stigma, but you cannot claim from them that 70 per cent of Indian caregivers feel this way. Option c is right because small, non-random, context-bound samples are exactly what stop qualitative findings being generalised in the statistical sense. Exam trap: options a and b list supposed weaknesses that are really qualitative research's strengths; depth and the explanation of human experience are what it does best, so a question asking for a limitation wants generalisability (or subjectivity and time cost), never lack of depth.", "traps": {"a": "Depth is the main strength of qualitative research, not a weakness: long interviews and immersion give rich, detailed accounts that numbers cannot. It is quantitative surveys that are criticised as broad but shallow.", "b": "Explaining human experience is precisely the goal of qualitative traditions such as phenomenology and IPA, so saying it cannot do this reverses the truth.", "d": "Qualitative research raises many ethical concerns, often more than surveys do: close researcher relationships, rich identifying detail, confidentiality of quotes and the emotional impact of interviews on vulnerable people."}}, {"q": "Purposive sampling is:", "concept": "The answer is c: purposive sampling is based on characteristics of interest. Sampling methods fall into two families. In probability sampling (simple random, systematic, stratified, cluster) every member of the population has a known, non-zero chance of selection, which lets the researcher calculate sampling error and generalise. In non-probability sampling (convenience, purposive, quota, snowball) the chance of selection is unknown. Purposive sampling, also called judgemental sampling, is the method in which the researcher deliberately hand-picks cases because they have the features the study needs: they are typical, expert, extreme or simply information-rich. Michael Quinn Patton described many purposeful strategies for qualitative work, such as maximum variation, typical case, extreme or deviant case and criterion sampling. It is the standard sampling method of qualitative research, because a phenomenological or grounded theory study needs people who have actually lived the experience being studied. Example: to study coping after a first psychotic episode, a researcher recruits twelve outpatients who had a first episode within the past year and can talk about it, rather than drawing names at random from the city. Option c is right because the defining feature of purposive sampling is selection by the researcher's judgement of who has the characteristics of interest. Exam trap: purposive sampling is not quota sampling; quota sampling fixes numbers per subgroup to mirror population proportions and then fills them non-randomly, whereas purposive sampling chooses individuals for their relevance, with no attempt at proportions.", "traps": {"a": "Random selection defines probability sampling, such as simple random sampling, in which every member has an equal chance; purposive sampling deliberately replaces chance with the researcher's judgement.", "b": "Being representative of the population is the aim of probability methods such as stratified random sampling; a purposive sample is chosen for relevance and is usually not representative.", "d": "Purposive sampling is open to researcher bias, since the researcher decides who is typical or information-rich; no non-probability method can claim to be unbiased."}}, {"q": "Coding in qualitative research involves:", "concept": "The answer is c: coding in qualitative research involves labelling themes or patterns. A code is a short word or phrase the researcher attaches to a segment of an interview, field note or document to capture what it is about. Coding is the first step in turning pages of raw text into findings. In grounded theory, Anselm Strauss and Juliet Corbin (1990) described three stages: open coding breaks the data apart and labels concepts line by line, axial coding links the codes into categories, and selective coding integrates everything around one core category. In thematic analysis, Virginia Braun and Victoria Clarke (2006) set out six phases: familiarising with the data, generating initial codes, searching for themes, reviewing themes, defining and naming themes, and writing the report. Codes can be descriptive (what is said) or interpretive (what it means), and researchers often manage them in software such as NVivo or ATLAS.ti, though the software does not do the thinking. Example: a patient says, 'After the diagnosis my relatives stopped inviting us to weddings'; the researcher codes this 'social exclusion', and when similar codes recur across interviews they are grouped into the theme 'stigma'. Option c is right because qualitative coding is the interpretive labelling of meaningful segments so that patterns and themes can be identified. Exam trap: in quantitative research coding does mean assigning numbers to responses (male = 1, female = 2) for data entry, which is why option a tempts; the stem says qualitative research, where codes are labels of meaning, not numbers.", "traps": {"a": "Assigning numbers to data is coding in the quantitative sense, preparing survey answers for statistical software; qualitative codes are word labels of meaning.", "b": "Summarising statistical findings describes descriptive statistics or the results section of a quantitative report, not the analysis of text.", "d": "Programming a model is computer coding or statistical modelling, an unrelated use of the word."}}, {"q": "Who proposed the concept of 'schemas' in cognitive development?", "concept": "The answer is c: Jean Piaget proposed the concept of schemas in cognitive development. Piaget (1896 to 1980), a Swiss biologist turned developmental psychologist, studied how children's thinking changes by observing his own three children and by questioning children with his clinical method. For him the unit of knowledge is the scheme (usually written schema in English): an organised pattern of action or thought for dealing with a class of experiences. An infant's schemes are actions such as sucking and grasping; an older child's are mental operations such as adding or classifying. Schemes change through adaptation, which has two processes. Assimilation fits new experience into an existing scheme; accommodation changes the scheme when the experience will not fit. A mismatch creates disequilibrium, and resolving it, equilibration, carries the child through the four stages: sensorimotor, preoperational, concrete operational and formal operational. Example: a two-year-old whose 'doggie' schema means 'four-legged animal' calls a cow 'doggie' (assimilation); when told it is a cow that moos, the child splits the schema into dog and cow (accommodation). Option c is right because schemas, with assimilation and accommodation, are the building blocks of Piaget's theory of cognitive development. Exam trap: the word schema is also used by Frederic Bartlett (Remembering, 1932) for the reconstruction of memory and by Aaron Beck for core beliefs in depression; when the question says cognitive development, the answer is Piaget.", "traps": {"a": "B. F. Skinner was a radical behaviourist who explained behaviour by reinforcement histories and deliberately avoided internal mental structures such as schemas.", "b": "Sigmund Freud described development through psychosexual stages (oral, anal, phallic, latency, genital) and the id, ego and superego, not through cognitive schemas.", "d": "Abraham Maslow proposed the hierarchy of needs and self-actualisation, a theory of motivation and personality rather than of cognitive development."}}, {"q": "Which of the following best explains the participatory research from more traditional research paradigms?", "concept": "The answer is c: participatory research involves stakeholders as co-researchers throughout the research process, including question formulation and data interpretation. Traditional, positivist research treats participants as subjects: the expert researcher designs the study, collects data from people and interprets it alone, aiming at objectivity and distance. Participatory research reverses this power relationship. Its roots include Kurt Lewin's action research (1946), with its cycles of plan, act, observe and reflect; the Brazilian educator Paulo Freire's Pedagogy of the Oppressed (English edition 1970), which argued that oppressed people should create knowledge about their own lives; and the participatory action research of the Colombian sociologist Orlando Fals-Borda. John Heron's co-operative inquiry goes further, making every participant both co-researcher and co-subject. In health, community-based participatory research partners researchers with patients, families and communities. The goals are relevance, empowerment and change, not knowledge alone. Example: instead of designing a questionnaire on service gaps herself, a psychologist forms a team with parents of children with intellectual disability; together they choose the questions, run focus groups, interpret the findings and present them to the district health office to change services. Option c is right because shared ownership of every stage, including framing the question and interpreting the data, is what distinguishes participatory research from traditional paradigms. Exam trap: participatory research does not just mean that people 'participate' by giving data; everyone who answers a questionnaire is a participant, but only participatory research makes them partners in deciding what is studied and what the findings mean.", "traps": {"a": "Double-blind procedures, in which neither participants nor experimenters know who receives which condition, belong to the randomised controlled trial, the model of traditional experimental research; participatory research makes participants fully informed partners.", "b": "Strict researcher objectivity with no collaboration describes the positivist paradigm that participatory research rejects; it is the opposite of the participatory approach.", "d": "Random sampling for generalisability is a hallmark of quantitative survey research; participatory projects usually work with one community to solve a local problem rather than to generalise."}}, {"q": "Cognitive dissonance theory was developed by:", "concept": "The answer is a: cognitive dissonance theory was developed by Leon Festinger. Festinger (1919 to 1989), an American social psychologist and a student of Kurt Lewin, proposed it in A Theory of Cognitive Dissonance (1957). Dissonance is the uncomfortable tension of holding two inconsistent cognitions, especially when behaviour contradicts an attitude. Because the tension is unpleasant, people try to reduce it: by changing the attitude, changing the behaviour, adding consonant cognitions or trivialising the inconsistency. Dissonance grows with the importance of the cognitions and the ratio of dissonant to consonant ones. The classic test is Festinger and James Carlsmith's (1959) study: students did a boring task, then were paid either $1 or $20 to tell the next participant it was fun. The $1 group later rated the task as more enjoyable, because $1 was too little to justify lying, so they changed their attitude to fit their behaviour; the $20 group had an external justification. Other paradigms include effort justification (Elliot Aronson and Judson Mills, 1959) and post-decision dissonance (Jack Brehm, 1956). Example: a smoker who knows smoking causes cancer tells himself 'my grandfather smoked and lived to ninety'. Option a is right because Festinger named and formulated cognitive dissonance theory. Exam trap: Daryl Bem's self-perception theory (1967) explains the same $1 and $20 result without any tension, so if a question asks for the rival explanation of dissonance findings, the answer is Bem, not Festinger.", "traps": {"b": "Albert Bandura developed social learning theory (renamed social cognitive theory in 1986), with observational learning and self-efficacy (1977); he did not propose dissonance theory.", "c": "Albert Ellis developed rational emotive behaviour therapy (begun in 1955) and the ABC model of irrational beliefs, a clinical theory rather than a social psychology theory of attitude change.", "d": "Jean Piaget's disequilibrium, the conflict between a scheme and experience, sounds like dissonance, but it belongs to his theory of cognitive development, not to attitude change."}}, {"q": "Which concept refers to mental shortcuts in thinking?", "concept": "The answer is b: heuristics are mental shortcuts in thinking. A heuristic is a rule of thumb that lets us judge or decide quickly with little effort, at the cost of occasional systematic error. It contrasts with an algorithm, a step-by-step procedure that guarantees a correct answer if followed but may be slow. Herbert Simon's idea of bounded rationality explained why we need shortcuts: limited time, information and mental capacity mean we satisfice rather than optimise. Amos Tversky and Daniel Kahneman catalogued the main heuristics in their 1974 Science paper 'Judgment under Uncertainty: Heuristics and Biases'. The availability heuristic judges frequency by how easily examples come to mind; the representativeness heuristic judges probability by similarity to a typical case, producing base-rate neglect and the conjunction fallacy (the Linda problem); anchoring and adjustment starts from an initial value and adjusts too little. Gerd Gigerenzer later argued that fast and frugal heuristics are often ecologically rational, not merely error-prone. Kahneman received the Nobel Prize in economics in 2002. Example: after news of two plane crashes, a person judges flying more dangerous than driving because crashes are easy to recall (availability), although road deaths are far more common. Option b is right because 'mental shortcut' is the textbook definition of a heuristic. Exam trap: a schema (d) also speeds up thinking, but a schema is an organised store of knowledge about a category, whereas a heuristic is a strategy for reaching a judgement; do not confuse the knowledge structure with the shortcut rule.", "traps": {"a": "Insight is the sudden grasp of a solution by seeing how a problem's parts relate, shown by Wolfgang Kohler's chimpanzee Sultan joining two sticks on Tenerife; it is a kind of problem solving, not a shortcut rule.", "c": "Intelligence is the general capacity to learn, reason and adapt, measured by IQ tests; it is an ability, not a particular thinking strategy.", "d": "A schema is an organised mental framework of knowledge about a concept or situation (Piaget, Bartlett); it guides perception and memory but is a knowledge structure, not a decision rule."}}, {"q": "Lashley's \"mass action\" principle primarily challenged which of the following beliefs?", "concept": "The answer is d: Lashley's mass action principle challenged the belief that learning is localised in discrete brain regions. Karl Spencer Lashley (1890 to 1958) spent decades hunting the engram, the physical trace of a memory, a term coined by Richard Semon (1904). He trained rats to run mazes, then removed different parts of their cortex, expecting that some particular lesion would wipe out the maze habit. None did. In Brain Mechanisms and Intelligence (1929) he stated two principles. Mass action: how badly learning was impaired depended on how much cortex was removed, not where. Equipotentiality: within a functional area, any part of the cortex could take over the work of any other. His review 'In Search of the Engram' (1950) concluded, half in jest, that learning should be impossible, since he could find memory nowhere in particular; it must be distributed. This opposed the localisationist view, strengthened since Paul Broca's speech area (1861), that each function, including learned habits, sits in a specific spot. His student Donald Hebb explained the result with cell assemblies spread across the cortex (1949). Example: in Lashley's pattern, a rat that lost a little cortex made few extra maze errors, while one that lost half its cortex made many more, whichever area was removed. Option d is right because mass action argued against discrete localisation of learning, replacing it with a distributed, amount-dependent view. Exam trap: option c, memories stored diffusely across the cortex, is not what Lashley challenged; it is Lashley's own conclusion, so choosing it reverses the question.", "traps": {"a": "That specific brain areas control motor movements was well established (Gustav Fritsch and Eduard Hitzig's stimulation of the motor cortex, 1870) and Lashley did not deny it; his principles concerned learning and memory in the cortex.", "b": "No one held that lesions have no impact on learning; Lashley's own lesions did impair learning, in proportion to how much cortex was lost.", "c": "Diffuse storage across the cortex is the conclusion Lashley reached ('In Search of the Engram', 1950), so it is the view he supported, not the belief he challenged."}}, {"q": "Cognitive triad in depression includes:", "concept": "The answer is a: the cognitive triad in depression consists of the self, the world and the future. Aaron Temkin Beck (1921 to 2021), a psychoanalytically trained psychiatrist at the University of Pennsylvania, noticed in the late 1950s that depressed patients had streams of quick, self-critical automatic thoughts that came before their low mood. In Depression (1967) he proposed that depression is maintained by a negative cognitive triad: a negative view of the self ('I am worthless'), of the world or current experience ('nothing goes right, people are against me') and of the future ('it will never get better'). These views grow from negative core beliefs, or schemas, laid down in childhood and activated by stress, and they are kept alive by cognitive distortions such as overgeneralisation and selective abstraction. Cognitive therapy (Cognitive Therapy of Depression, 1979, with colleagues) teaches the patient to catch, test and restructure such thoughts. The negative view of the future, hopelessness, is especially linked to suicide risk; Beck also built the Beck Depression Inventory (1961) and the Beck Hopelessness Scale. Example: after failing one test a student thinks 'I am stupid' (self), 'teachers always mark me down' (world) and 'I will never get into an M.Phil' (future). Option a is right because self, world and future are the three targets of negative thinking in Beck's triad. Exam trap: this same paper asked the triad at Q3 as a NOT question, with 'negative view of past' as the odd one out; Beck's triad has no past, so 'past, present, future' (c) is the bait both times.", "traps": {"b": "Id, ego and superego form Freud's structural model of the mind (The Ego and the Id, 1923), a psychoanalytic theory of personality, not a set of depressive cognitions.", "c": "Past, present and future is a time sequence; Beck's triad has no past component, which is why 'negative view of past' was the correct NOT answer to Q3 in this paper.", "d": "Thoughts, emotions and behaviours are the three linked elements of the general CBT model (the cognitive triangle) describing how any problem is maintained, not the content of depressive thinking."}}, {"q": "What is a core concept of Bandura's cognitive theory?", "concept": "The answer is b: observational learning is a core concept of Bandura's social cognitive theory. Albert Bandura (1925 to 2021), a Canadian-born psychologist at Stanford, argued that people learn a great deal simply by watching others, called models, and the consequences of their behaviour, without performing the behaviour or being reinforced themselves. In the Bobo doll experiment (Bandura, Dorothea Ross and Sheila Ross, 1961), nursery-school children who watched an adult punch and kick an inflatable doll later copied the model's specific acts and words, while children who saw a calm model did not. In 1965, children who saw the model punished imitated less, until offered a reward to show what they had seen, when they reproduced it fully: reinforcement affects performance, not learning. Bandura named four processes: attention, retention, motor reproduction and motivation. With Richard Walters (1963) he developed social learning theory, which he renamed social cognitive theory in Social Foundations of Thought and Action (1986). Its other pillars are reciprocal determinism (person, behaviour and environment influence one another) and self-efficacy (1977), belief in one's capacity to perform a task. Example: a child watches an older sibling being praised for tying shoelaces, remembers the steps, and later ties her own when she wants praise too. Option b is right because learning by observing models is the defining idea of Bandura's theory. Exam trap: do not confuse Bandura with Julian Rotter, whose separate social learning theory (1954) is about expectancy, reinforcement value and locus of control; observational learning and the Bobo doll belong to Bandura.", "traps": {"a": "Classical conditioning, learning by pairing a neutral stimulus with an unconditioned one, is Ivan Pavlov's work; Bandura went beyond conditioning by showing learning without direct experience.", "c": "Psychoanalysis is Sigmund Freud's theory and therapy of unconscious conflict; Bandura's approach is empirical and cognitive-behavioural, not psychodynamic.", "d": "Moral reasoning, studied through stages of justice reasoning, is the domain of Jean Piaget (1932) and Lawrence Kohlberg; Bandura wrote about moral disengagement, but moral reasoning is not his core concept."}}, {"q": "According to Piaget, which stage involves abstract thinking?", "concept": "The answer is d: according to Piaget, abstract thinking appears in the formal operational stage. Jean Piaget (1896 to 1980) described four invariant stages of cognitive development, each a qualitatively different way of thinking: sensorimotor (birth to about 2 years), preoperational (about 2 to 7), concrete operational (about 7 to 11) and formal operational (from about 11 or 12 onward). In the formal stage thought is freed from concrete, present objects. Adolescents can reason about abstractions such as justice or infinity, about the possible as well as the real, and about propositions whose conclusions follow from their form. The hallmark is hypothetico-deductive reasoning: forming hypotheses and testing them systematically, one variable at a time. Barbel Inhelder and Piaget showed this in The Growth of Logical Thinking from Childhood to Adolescence (1958) with the pendulum problem: a concrete thinker changes length, weight and push together, while a formal thinker varies one factor at a time and finds that only string length matters. Formal thought also brings idealism and adolescent egocentrism (David Elkind's imaginary audience and personal fable), and not everyone reaches it; many adults reason formally only in familiar domains. Example: a 14-year-old asked 'If all dogs were purple and Bruno is a dog, what colour is Bruno?' answers 'purple', accepting the premise, while a 7-year-old protests that dogs are not purple. Option d is right because reasoning about abstract and hypothetical ideas defines the formal operational stage. Exam trap: concrete operational children do think logically (they master conservation and classification), but only about real, tangible objects; logical is not the same as abstract.", "traps": {"a": "The sensorimotor stage (birth to about 2) is thinking through senses and actions; its great achievement is object permanence, not abstraction.", "b": "The preoperational stage (about 2 to 7) brings symbolic thought and language but is marked by egocentrism, centration and failure to conserve.", "c": "The concrete operational stage (about 7 to 11) brings logical operations such as conservation, seriation and classification, but applied only to concrete, present objects."}}, {"q": "Which is an example of a cognitive distortion?", "concept": "The answer is b: mind reading is an example of a cognitive distortion. Cognitive distortions are systematic errors in processing information that keep negative emotions going. Aaron Beck identified the first set in his work on depression in the 1960s: arbitrary inference, selective abstraction, overgeneralisation, magnification and minimisation, personalisation and dichotomous (all-or-nothing) thinking. The psychiatrist David Burns popularised a longer list in Feeling Good (1980), where mind reading and fortune telling are the two forms of 'jumping to conclusions'. Mind reading means assuming you know what other people are thinking, usually something negative about you, without checking. Fortune telling is predicting that things will turn out badly. Other distortions include emotional reasoning ('I feel useless, so I am useless'), should statements, labelling and catastrophising. In cognitive therapy the patient records automatic thoughts, names the distortion, weighs the evidence for and against the thought, and writes a more balanced alternative. Example: during her case presentation a trainee sees her supervisor frowning and concludes 'she thinks I am incompetent'; in therapy she would note that the frown might equally mean concentration or a headache. Option b is right because mind reading is a standard listed cognitive distortion, an unsupported conclusion about what others think. Exam trap: mind reading is a specific form of Beck's broader arbitrary inference (a conclusion drawn without evidence), so when another question describes a conclusion with no evidence and offers 'arbitrary inference', that is the Beck-era name for the same family of error.", "traps": {"a": "Observational learning is Albert Bandura's learning by watching models (Bobo doll studies, 1961), a normal learning process, not an error of thinking.", "c": "Reinforcement is B. F. Skinner's operant term for any consequence that increases a behaviour; it is a learning principle, not a distortion.", "d": "Free association is Sigmund Freud's psychoanalytic technique of saying whatever comes to mind without censoring it, used to reach unconscious material."}}, {"q": "Cognitive appraisal refers to:", "concept": "The answer is b: cognitive appraisal refers to evaluating environmental stressors. Richard Lazarus (1922 to 2002) argued that stress and emotion depend less on events themselves than on how we interpret them. He set out the idea in Psychological Stress and the Coping Process (1966), and with Susan Folkman built the transactional model of stress (Stress, Appraisal, and Coping, 1984), in which stress is a relationship between person and environment that the person appraises as taxing or exceeding their resources. Primary appraisal asks 'Does this matter to me, and how?': the event is judged irrelevant, benign-positive or stressful, and if stressful, as harm-loss, threat or challenge. Secondary appraisal asks 'What can I do about it?', weighing coping resources and options. Reappraisal revises both as the situation and coping unfold. Because appraisal sits between event and response, two people facing the same stressor can react very differently, and treatments such as stress inoculation and cognitive restructuring work by changing appraisals. Example: two candidates face the same entrance exam; one appraises it as a threat and her preparation as thin, and panics, while the other sees a challenge, trusts her practice and feels focused. Option b is right because cognitive appraisal is exactly this evaluation of a potential stressor and of one's ability to cope with it. Exam trap: primary appraisal judges the event and secondary appraisal judges the coping resources; papers often swap them, and 'secondary' does not mean less important or a later reaction to stress.", "traps": {"a": "Judging others is social perception or attribution, how we explain other people's behaviour (Fritz Heider, 1958), not the appraisal of stressors.", "c": "Unconscious motives are the focus of psychoanalytic theory (Freud); appraisal is a largely conscious, though often rapid, evaluation.", "d": "Genetic traits are inherited dispositions studied by behaviour genetics; they may shape vulnerability to stress, but appraisal is a cognitive process, not a trait."}}, {"q": "Which school of psychology emphasized introspection?", "concept": "The answer is b: structuralism is the school of psychology that emphasised introspection. Wilhelm Wundt (1832 to 1920) founded experimental psychology at Leipzig in 1879 and studied immediate conscious experience by controlled introspection: trained observers gave short reports of their sensations and feelings in response to carefully controlled stimuli. His student Edward Bradford Titchener (1867 to 1927) took a stricter version to Cornell University and named it structuralism (1898). Its goal was to analyse consciousness into basic elements, sensations, images and affections, much as chemists built a periodic table, and to find how they combine. Introspection was its main method: observers had to describe raw experience and avoid the 'stimulus error' of naming the object instead. Structuralism faded after Titchener's death in 1927 because introspective reports could not be checked or settle disputes between laboratories, and because it excluded children, animals and applied problems. Functionalism (William James, John Dewey) asked what the mind is for, and behaviourism rejected introspection outright. Example: shown an apple, a structuralist observer should not say 'apple' but report 'a glossy red patch, roundish, cool to the touch, mildly pleasant'. Option b is right because introspection was the defining method of structuralism. Exam trap: Indian question papers often call Wundt himself a structuralist, but strictly Titchener named and led structuralism; if both appear, Titchener is the founder of structuralism and Wundt the father of experimental psychology, and both used introspection.", "traps": {"a": "Behaviourism, launched by John B. Watson's 1913 manifesto 'Psychology as the Behaviorist Views It', rejected introspection as unscientific and studied only observable behaviour.", "c": "Humanism (Maslow, Rogers; the third force of the 1950s and 1960s) valued subjective experience and self-actualisation, but it relied on therapy, case studies and the Q-sort, not laboratory introspection.", "d": "Psychoanalysis (Sigmund Freud) explored the unconscious through free association and dream analysis, arguing that the most important mental contents are hidden from introspection."}}, {"q": "Who is considered the father of experimental psychology?", "concept": "The answer is b: Wilhelm Wundt is considered the father of experimental psychology. Wundt (1832 to 1920), a German physician who had assisted Hermann von Helmholtz, argued that the mind could be studied with the experimental methods of physiology. His Principles of Physiological Psychology (1873 to 1874) announced a new science, and in 1879 at the University of Leipzig his students began original research in what is conventionally called the first psychological laboratory; that year is taken as the birth of psychology as an independent science. He founded the journal Philosophische Studien (1881) and trained many pioneers, including Edward Titchener, James McKeen Cattell, Emil Kraepelin and Charles Spearman. His method was controlled introspection: trained observers reacted to precise stimuli, such as a metronome beat or a flash of light, and reported their immediate experience while reaction times were measured. He also wrote the ten-volume Volkerpsychologie (folk psychology) on language, myth and custom, which he thought needed non-experimental methods. Example: in a Leipzig reaction-time study an observer pressed a key as soon as he heard a click, and Wundt compared this simple reaction with one that required recognising the sound first. Option b is right because Wundt gave psychology its first laboratory, journal and trained students, the marks of an experimental science. Exam trap: read the qualifier: Gustav Fechner (Elements of Psychophysics, 1860) is the father of psychophysics, William James of American psychology, John B. Watson of behaviourism and Sigmund Freud of psychoanalysis; only experimental psychology belongs to Wundt.", "traps": {"a": "Sigmund Freud is the father of psychoanalysis; his clinical method of free association and case study was not experimental.", "c": "John B. Watson is the father of behaviourism (1913) and ran the Little Albert study (1920), but experimental psychology had begun with Wundt decades earlier.", "d": "B. F. Skinner was the leading radical behaviourist and developer of operant conditioning (1938), working long after psychology's experimental foundation."}}, {"q": "Operant conditioning was proposed by:", "concept": "The answer is b: operant conditioning was proposed by B. F. Skinner. Burrhus Frederic Skinner (1904 to 1990), a radical behaviourist at Harvard, set out the idea in The Behavior of Organisms (1938). He separated respondent behaviour, elicited by a stimulus as in Pavlov's work, from operant behaviour, which the organism emits freely and which 'operates' on the environment. In operant conditioning the probability of a response is changed by its consequences: reinforcement (positive or negative) makes it more likely, and punishment less likely. Skinner built the operant chamber, or Skinner box, in which a rat could press a lever at any time while a cumulative recorder plotted its response rate, and with Charles Ferster (1957) he mapped schedules of reinforcement; he also studied shaping by successive approximations and extinction. His work underlies applied behaviour analysis, token economies and programmed instruction. Example: a child who gets a star each time she finishes homework without reminders does so more often; the star is positive reinforcement. Option b is right because Skinner coined the term operant and developed operant conditioning as a system. Exam trap: Edward Thorndike came first: his puzzle-box studies with cats (1898) and his law of effect (1911) described learning by consequences, now called instrumental learning, and Skinner built on them; if asked who first showed learning from consequences, think Thorndike, and if asked who named operant conditioning, choose Skinner. Key note: some texts credit Thorndike as the originator of this kind of learning and call Skinner its systematiser, so d has historical support, but the term and theory of operant conditioning are Skinner's, and the official key b stands.", "traps": {"a": "Albert Bandura proposed observational learning and social cognitive theory, showing that learning can occur without direct reinforcement, beyond operant conditioning.", "c": "Ivan Pavlov discovered classical (respondent) conditioning, in which a neutral stimulus paired with an unconditioned stimulus comes to elicit an involuntary response.", "d": "Edward Thorndike studied cats in puzzle boxes (1898) and stated the law of effect (1911), the forerunner of operant conditioning usually called instrumental or trial-and-error learning; he did not coin the term operant."}}, {"q": "Which type of memory lasts for only a few seconds?", "concept": "The answer is c: sensory memory lasts for only a few seconds. In Richard Atkinson and Richard Shiffrin's multi-store model (1968), information passes from sensory memory to short-term memory if attended, and on to long-term memory if rehearsed. Sensory memory, the sensory register, holds a large amount of raw information from each sense very briefly, just long enough for attention to select what matters. Ulric Neisser (1967) named the visual store iconic memory and the auditory store echoic memory. George Sperling (1960) flashed twelve letters for 50 milliseconds; people reported only four or five in all, but when a tone after the flash cued one row, they gave about three of its four letters, so most of the display had briefly been available. Delaying the tone by about a second removed the advantage: the icon fades within roughly a quarter to half a second. Echoic memory lasts about 2 to 4 seconds. Short-term memory lasts about 18 to 30 seconds unrehearsed (Lloyd and Margaret Peterson, 1959), and long-term memory from minutes to a lifetime. Example: when a friend asks 'What did I just say?' while you were reading, you can often recover the last few words from your echoic store. Option c is right because sensory memory is the store whose whole lifespan is a few seconds or less. Exam trap: working memory (d) is also brief, but it lasts tens of seconds unrehearsed and can be held indefinitely by rehearsal. Key note: some introductory books loosely say short-term memory lasts 'a few seconds', which makes d tempting, but sensory memory is the standard answer and the official key c stands.", "traps": {"a": "Long-term memory has no known capacity limit and lasts from minutes to a lifetime, the opposite of a few seconds.", "b": "Semantic memory is the part of long-term memory for general facts and meanings, distinguished from episodic memory by Endel Tulving (1972); it is lasting, not brief.", "d": "Working memory (Alan Baddeley and Graham Hitch, 1974) is the active short-term workspace; unrehearsed material fades over about 18 to 30 seconds and rehearsal can keep it as long as needed, so it outlasts sensory memory."}}, {"q": "Which theory emphasizes self-actualization?", "concept": "The answer is c: the humanistic theory emphasises self-actualisation. Humanistic psychology, which Abraham Maslow called the third force, arose in the 1950s and 1960s as a reaction against psychoanalysis (the first force), seen as pessimistic and focused on illness, and behaviourism (the second force), seen as mechanical. It holds that people are basically good, have free will and are motivated to grow. Self-actualisation is the striving to fulfil one's potential, to become everything one is capable of becoming. The neurologist Kurt Goldstein coined the term in The Organism (English edition 1939); Maslow (1908 to 1970) placed it at the top of his hierarchy of needs (1943; Motivation and Personality, 1954), above physiological, safety, belonging and esteem needs. He studied people he judged self-actualised, such as Abraham Lincoln and Eleanor Roosevelt, described their acceptance, spontaneity, autonomy and peak experiences, and estimated that 1 per cent of people or fewer reach this level. Carl Rogers (1902 to 1987) spoke of an actualising tendency in every organism, which flourishes under unconditional positive regard. Example: a successful engineer leaves a secure job to train as a classical musician because she feels it is what she was meant to do; Maslow would call this self-actualisation. Option c is right because self-actualisation is the central motivational concept of humanistic theory. Exam trap: self-efficacy (Bandura's social cognitive idea) and the ideal self (Rogers) sound similar, but self-actualisation means fulfilling one's potential, and it belongs to the humanistic school.", "traps": {"a": "Psychoanalytic theory (Freud) explains personality through unconscious conflict among id, ego and superego and through psychosexual stages; it sees people as driven by sexual and aggressive instincts, not by growth.", "b": "Behaviourist theory (Watson, Skinner) explains behaviour as shaped by environmental reinforcement and posits no inner drive toward growth.", "d": "Cognitive theory focuses on thinking, schemas and information processing (Piaget, Beck, George Kelly); self-actualisation is not its central concept."}}, {"q": "Which part of the nervous system is responsible for rest and digest?", "concept": "The answer is c: the parasympathetic nervous system is responsible for rest and digest. The nervous system divides into the central nervous system (brain and spinal cord) and the peripheral nervous system. The peripheral system has a somatic branch, which carries sensation and controls voluntary skeletal muscles, and an autonomic branch, which runs the internal organs automatically. The autonomic system has two divisions that usually work in opposition. The sympathetic division mobilises energy for emergencies, the fight-or-flight response described by Walter Cannon (1915): the heart races, pupils dilate, airways widen, digestion halts and the adrenal medulla releases adrenaline. The parasympathetic division conserves and restores energy: it slows the heart, constricts the pupils, increases salivation and stimulates digestion and elimination. Its fibres leave the brainstem in cranial nerves III, VII, IX and X and the sacral spinal cord (the craniosacral outflow), and the vagus nerve (X) carries most of them to the heart, lungs and gut. Acetylcholine is its transmitter at both ganglia and target organs. Most organs receive both divisions, so heart rate reflects a balance between sympathetic acceleration and vagal braking. Example: after a big lunch you feel drowsy, your heart slows and your stomach gets on with digestion; the parasympathetic system is in charge. Option c is right because rest and digest is the textbook label for parasympathetic function. Exam trap: the sympathetic division has the thoracolumbar outflow and the parasympathetic the craniosacral; in sexual response, erection is parasympathetic but ejaculation sympathetic ('point and shoot').", "traps": {"a": "The sympathetic division (thoracolumbar outflow, T1 to L2) produces fight or flight, speeding the heart and halting digestion, the opposite of rest and digest.", "b": "The somatic nervous system carries sensory input and controls voluntary skeletal muscles; it does not regulate the internal organs.", "d": "The central nervous system is the brain and spinal cord; it directs the autonomic system through the hypothalamus and brainstem, but rest and digest is the specific job of the peripheral parasympathetic division."}}, {"q": "Classical conditioning involves:", "concept": "The answer is c: classical conditioning involves an involuntary response to stimuli. Ivan Petrovich Pavlov (1849 to 1936), a Russian physiologist who won the 1904 Nobel Prize for his work on digestion, noticed that his dogs salivated before food arrived, at the sight of the dish or the attendant's footsteps, and from about 1897 studied these 'psychic secretions' systematically. An unconditioned stimulus (UCS, food) naturally produces an unconditioned response (UCR, salivation). A neutral stimulus, such as a metronome (popularly a bell), is presented just before the food; after several pairings it becomes a conditioned stimulus (CS) that alone produces a conditioned response (CR, salivation). The responses involved are reflexive and involuntary: salivation, fear, nausea, eye blinks, heart-rate change. Related phenomena include extinction, spontaneous recovery, generalisation and discrimination. John B. Watson and Rosalie Rayner's Little Albert study (1920) showed that human fear can be conditioned in the same way, and Joseph Wolpe's systematic desensitisation later used conditioning to reverse such fears. Example: a patient who receives chemotherapy in one clinic starts to feel nauseous on entering its waiting room; the room (CS) now triggers involuntary nausea (CR). Option c is right because classical conditioning attaches an existing involuntary, reflexive response to a new stimulus through association. Exam trap: Pavlov himself called the food the 'reinforcement' of the conditioned reflex, but in exam language reinforcement means a consequence that strengthens behaviour in operant conditioning; classical conditioning works by pairing stimuli before the response, operant conditioning by consequences after it.", "traps": {"a": "Reinforcement, a consequence that increases the probability of a behaviour, is the key mechanism of Skinner's operant conditioning, not of stimulus pairing.", "b": "Voluntary, emitted behaviour that operates on the environment is the domain of operant conditioning; classical conditioning deals with elicited reflexes.", "d": "Modelling, learning by observing others, is Albert Bandura's observational learning (Bobo doll studies, 1961), a third kind of learning."}}, {"q": "The 'Yerkes-Dodson Law' explains the relationship between arousal and:", "concept": "The answer is c: the Yerkes-Dodson law explains the relationship between arousal and performance. In 1908 Robert Mearns Yerkes and John Dillingham Dodson trained mice on a brightness discrimination, giving electric shocks for wrong choices. Learning was fastest at intermediate shock strengths, and the harder the discrimination, the weaker the best shock. Later textbooks generalised this into the Yerkes-Dodson law: performance rises with arousal (or anxiety, or motivation) up to an optimal level and then falls, an inverted-U curve. The optimum depends on task difficulty: simple or well-learned tasks are done best at relatively high arousal, complex or new tasks at lower, moderate arousal. Too little arousal leaves a person drowsy and careless; too much makes them tense and disorganised. Donald Hebb's optimal arousal theory (1955) gave the idea a physiological basis, and Robert Zajonc's account of social facilitation (1965) used similar logic: an audience raises arousal, helping easy tasks and harming difficult ones. Example: mild exam nerves sharpen your focus in an easy MCQ section, but panic during a hard statistics problem makes you forget formulas you knew well. Option c is right because the law describes how the level of arousal affects the quality of performance. Exam trap: the relationship is an inverted U, never linear, and complex tasks have a lower optimum, not a higher one. Key note: the original 1908 study actually measured how quickly mice learned a habit, so 'learning' (b) has some historical basis, but the law as universally taught links arousal to performance, and the official key c stands.", "traps": {"a": "Emotion features in arousal-based theories of emotion (James-Lange, Schachter-Singer), but the Yerkes-Dodson law concerns how arousal affects performance, not which emotion is felt.", "b": "Learning is what Yerkes and Dodson's mice were actually doing in 1908, which makes this tempting, but the law as taught and tested relates arousal to performance.", "d": "Memory can be affected by arousal (flashbulb memories, the weapon focus effect), but it is not the variable named in the Yerkes-Dodson law."}}, {"q": "Which lobe of the brain is responsible for vision?", "concept": "The answer is d: the occipital lobe is responsible for vision. Each cerebral hemisphere has four lobes, divided by the central sulcus and the lateral (Sylvian) fissure: frontal, parietal, temporal and occipital. The occipital lobe, at the back of the brain, contains the primary visual cortex (V1, also called striate cortex or Brodmann area 17) along the calcarine sulcus. Signals travel from the retina through the optic nerve, the optic chiasm (where fibres from the nasal half of each retina cross) and the lateral geniculate nucleus of the thalamus to V1, so each hemisphere sees the opposite visual field. David Hubel and Torsten Wiesel shared the 1981 Nobel Prize for showing that V1 cells respond to edges and lines of particular orientations. Beyond V1, processing splits into two streams described by Leslie Ungerleider and Mortimer Mishkin (1982): a ventral 'what' stream into the temporal lobe for recognising objects and faces, and a dorsal 'where' (or 'how') stream into the parietal lobe for location and movement. Example: a stroke in the right occipital lobe leaves a person blind to the left side of space in both eyes (left homonymous hemianopia), even though the eyes themselves are healthy. Option d is right because primary visual processing takes place in the occipital lobe. Exam trap: damage outside the occipital lobe can still disturb seeing, since temporal damage causes prosopagnosia (failure to recognise faces) and parietal damage causes neglect; these are higher visual functions, but vision itself begins in the occipital cortex.", "traps": {"a": "The parietal lobe, behind the central sulcus, holds the primary somatosensory cortex for touch and body sense and handles spatial attention; it receives the dorsal visual stream but is not the primary visual area.", "b": "The frontal lobe controls voluntary movement (primary motor cortex), planning and personality, and contains Broca's area for speech production; it does not process vision.", "c": "The temporal lobe houses the primary auditory cortex and Wernicke's area, and its lower part recognises objects and faces through the ventral 'what' stream, but basic vision is occipital."}}, {"q": "Latent learning challenges which of the following assumptions of strict behaviorism?", "concept": "The answer is b: latent learning challenges the strict behaviourist assumption that reinforcement is required for learning to occur. For behaviourists such as Edward Thorndike and Clark Hull, learning meant stamping in stimulus-response connections through reward: no reinforcement, no learning. Edward Chace Tolman (1886 to 1959), a purposive behaviourist at Berkeley, disagreed. In Tolman and Charles Honzik's experiment (1930), three groups of hungry rats ran a complex maze daily. One group always found food in the goal box, one never did, and the third found food only from day 11. Until then the third group made as many errors as the never-rewarded rats, but once food appeared its errors fell almost at once to the level of the always-rewarded group. It must have been learning the maze all along without reward; the food only gave it a reason to show what it knew. This is latent learning: learning without obvious reinforcement that stays hidden until there is an incentive to perform. Tolman (1948) said the rats had built a cognitive map, a mental representation of the maze. The study separates learning from performance, a distinction Albert Bandura later drew on. Example: a child who rides to school in the car every day without paying attention can suddenly guide a lost visitor there; the route was learned without any reward. Option b is right because the rats learned without reinforcement, contradicting the claim that reinforcement is necessary for learning. Exam trap: latent learning and cognitive maps are Tolman's, not Kohler's (insight) or Bandura's (observational learning), although all three challenged strict behaviourism.", "traps": {"a": "Shaping, reinforcing successive approximations, is one of Skinner's techniques; no behaviourist claimed it is the only way to learn, and latent learning does not test it.", "c": "Whether extinction is permanent is answered by spontaneous recovery (Pavlov), not by latent learning; behaviourists themselves accept that extinguished responses can return.", "d": "The order of classical and operant conditioning is not an assumption of behaviourism and has nothing to do with Tolman's maze studies."}}]});
